window.AV2_CARDS = [{"key": "M01:0", "module": "M01", "q": "<p>What was Brahmagupta's historic contribution to the concept of zero in 628 CE?</p>", "a": "<p>He established that zero is not just a positional placeholder but an integer with defined operational arithmetic rules (a + 0 = a, a - 0 = a, a x 0 = 0).</p>"}, {"key": "M01:1", "module": "M01", "q": "<p>Why does multiplying two negative numbers produce a positive number?</p>", "a": "<p>It is mathematically required to preserve the distributive law of arithmetic: a(b + (-b)) = 0 forces (-a)(-b) = ab.</p>"}, {"key": "M01:2", "module": "M01", "q": "<p>What is the central cell in every 3x3 normal magic square with digits 1-9?</p>", "a": "<p>The center cell must strictly be 5, because four lines pass through the center and 4 x 15 = 60 = 45 + 3c, yielding c = 5.</p>"}, {"key": "M01:3", "module": "M01", "q": "<p>What is Kaprekar's constant and how is it derived?</p>", "a": "<p>6174 is the 4-digit mathematical attractor reached in &lt;= 7 steps by repeatedly subtracting ascending digits from descending digits.</p>"}, {"key": "M01:4", "module": "M01", "q": "<p>Which positive integers CANNOT be expressed as a sum of two or more consecutive positive integers?</p>", "a": "<p>Powers of two (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, ...) because any consecutive integer sum must possess at least one odd prime factor.</p>"}, {"key": "M01:5", "module": "M01", "q": "<p>What is the winning strategy in the Game of 21 when taking 1 to 3 items?</p>", "a": "<p>Ensure your move always leaves the opponent with a multiple of 4 (4, 8, 12, 16, 20), forcing them into an unavoidable loss.</p>"}, {"key": "M02:0", "module": "M02", "q": "<p>How did Euclid prove that there are infinitely many prime numbers?</p>", "a": "<p>By contradiction: assuming finitely many primes p1..pk, N = p1<em>...</em>pk + 1 leaves remainder 1 when divided by any pi, requiring a new prime factor.</p>"}, {"key": "M02:1", "module": "M02", "q": "<p>What does the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic state?</p>", "a": "<p>Every integer greater than 1 can be represented uniquely as a product of prime powers, up to the order of factors.</p>"}, {"key": "M02:2", "module": "M02", "q": "<p>What is the relationship between the HCF, LCM, and product of two numbers?</p>", "a": "<p>HCF(a, b) x LCM(a, b) = a x b, because min(x, y) + max(x, y) = x + y for every prime factor's exponents.</p>"}, {"key": "M02:3", "module": "M02", "q": "<p>Why is the square root of 2 an irrational number?</p>", "a": "<p>If sqrt(2) = a/b in lowest terms, then 2b^2 = a^2, forcing both a and b to be even, contradicting that a/b was in lowest terms.</p>"}, {"key": "M02:4", "module": "M02", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of Euclid's GCD algorithm?</p>", "a": "<p>O(log(min(a, b))) division steps; by Lam\u00e9's theorem, the number of steps never exceeds five times the number of digits of the smaller number.</p>"}, {"key": "M02:5", "module": "M02", "q": "<p>Why does the divisibility rule for 9 work by summing decimal digits?</p>", "a": "<p>Because 10 = 9 + 1 = 1 (mod 9), so each power 10^k = 1 (mod 9), making the entire number congruent to the sum of its digits mod 9.</p>"}, {"key": "M03:0", "module": "M03", "q": "<p>Why does the 'invert and multiply' rule work for fraction division?</p>", "a": "<p>Multiplying numerator and denominator by the reciprocal of the divisor turns the denominator into 1, leaving (a/b) x (d/c).</p>"}, {"key": "M03:1", "module": "M03", "q": "<p>How is a repeating decimal like 0.1666... converted into a fraction?</p>", "a": "<p>Let x = 0.1666...; multiply by 100 (16.666...) and 10 (1.666...); subtract to get 90x = 15, yielding x = 15/90 = 1/6.</p>"}, {"key": "M03:2", "module": "M03", "q": "<p>What is the difference between an increase of 5 percentage points and 5 percent?</p>", "a": "<p>An increase from 20% to 25% is 5 percentage points, but a 25% relative increase ((25 - 20) / 20 = 0.25).</p>"}, {"key": "M03:3", "module": "M03", "q": "<p>What is the formula for compound interest compounded n times per year?</p>", "a": "<p>A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt), which approaches continuous compounding Pe^(rt) as frequency n approaches infinity.</p>"}, {"key": "M03:4", "module": "M03", "q": "<p>What does a cartographic Representative Fraction scale of 1:50,000 mean?</p>", "a": "<p>One centimeter on the map represents 50,000 centimeters (500 meters or 0.5 kilometers) in reality.</p>"}, {"key": "M03:5", "module": "M03", "q": "<p>Why does a 10% price increase followed by a 10% decrease result in a net loss?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the 10% decrease is calculated on a larger base: 100 x 1.10 = 110, then 110 x 0.90 = 99 (a net 1% loss).</p>"}, {"key": "M09:0", "module": "M09", "q": "<p>Why do we square the deviations from the mean instead of just averaging them?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the raw deviations always sum to exactly zero for every data set, so their average measures nothing. Squaring removes the cancellation.</p>"}, {"key": "M09:1", "module": "M09", "q": "<p>A dataset gets one new, enormous value. What happens to the mean and to the median?</p>", "a": "<p>The mean moves towards it, without bound. The median moves by at most one position in the sorted order, and often not at all.</p>"}, {"key": "M09:2", "module": "M09", "q": "<p>What exactly does the n-1 divisor in the sample variance achieve?</p>", "a": "<p>It makes the estimator unbiased: the expected value of the sample variance equals the true population variance, because deviations measured from the sample mean are systematically too small.</p>"}, {"key": "M09:3", "module": "M09", "q": "<p>When is the coefficient of variation the right thing to report?</p>", "a": "<p>When comparing the spread of two quantities with different units or very different means, because CV is a unit-free ratio of the standard deviation to the mean.</p>"}, {"key": "M09:4", "module": "M09", "q": "<p>How do you compute the angle of a pie-chart sector?</p>", "a": "<p>The sector's share of the total, multiplied by 360 degrees.</p>"}, {"key": "M09:5", "module": "M09", "q": "<p>What is the difference between a bar graph and a histogram?</p>", "a": "<p>A bar graph compares separate categories and has gaps between bars. A histogram shows continuous class intervals with no gaps, and the area of each bar is proportional to its frequency.</p>"}, {"key": "M09:6", "module": "M09", "q": "<p>In the grouped-data median formula, what is cf?</p>", "a": "<p>The cumulative frequency of all classes strictly before the median class, not including the median class's own frequency.</p>"}, {"key": "M09:7", "module": "M09", "q": "<p>Give one way a graph can mislead while every number on it is correct.</p>", "a": "<p>Truncating the vertical axis so it does not start at zero, which exaggerates small differences into apparently large ones.</p>"}, {"key": "M04:0", "module": "M04", "q": "<p>What is the product law of exponents and why is it true?</p>", "a": "<p>a^m x a^n = a^(m+n); it is proven by counting repeated factors: m copies of a multiplied by n copies of a yields m+n copies.</p>"}, {"key": "M04:1", "module": "M04", "q": "<p>Why is any non-zero number raised to the zero power equal to one?</p>", "a": "<p>By the quotient law: a^n / a^n = a^(n-n) = a^0 = 1, since any non-zero quantity divided by itself is 1.</p>"}, {"key": "M04:2", "module": "M04", "q": "<p>How does the Babylonian method achieve quadratic convergence for square roots?</p>", "a": "<p>By averaging x and S/x at each iteration: x_(k+1) = 0.5(x_k + S/x_k), which doubles the number of accurate decimal digits per step.</p>"}, {"key": "M04:3", "module": "M04", "q": "<p>What is the Hardy-Ramanujan taxicab number and why is it mathematically famous?</p>", "a": "<p>1729 is the smallest integer expressible as the sum of two positive cubes in two different ways: 1^3 + 12^3 = 9^3 + 10^3 = 1729.</p>"}, {"key": "M04:4", "module": "M04", "q": "<p>How does Nicomachus's theorem express n^3 as a sum of odd numbers?</p>", "a": "<p>Every cube n^3 is the sum of n consecutive odd numbers starting at n^2 - n + 1 (e.g. 3^3 = 7 + 9 + 11 = 27).</p>"}, {"key": "M04:5", "module": "M04", "q": "<p>What did Fermat's Last Theorem state, and who finally proved it?</p>", "a": "<p>No positive integers satisfy a^n + b^n = c^n for n &gt; 2; scrawled by Fermat in 1637 and proved by Andrew Wiles in 1995.</p>"}, {"key": "M05:0", "module": "M05", "q": "<p>Who is considered the historical father of algebra and where did the name originate?</p>", "a": "<p>Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi in 9th-century Baghdad; the word comes from al-jabr ('restoring' terms by adding to both sides).</p>"}, {"key": "M05:1", "module": "M05", "q": "<p>What is the geometric proof of the identity (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2?</p>", "a": "<p>A square of side (a+b) splits into four areas: two squares a^2 and b^2, plus two identical rectangles each of area ab.</p>"}, {"key": "M05:2", "module": "M05", "q": "<p>Why does subtracting a reversed two-digit number from the original always yield a multiple of 9?</p>", "a": "<p>(10t + u) - (10u + t) = 9t - 9u = 9(t - u), which has 9 as an explicit factor for all digits.</p>"}, {"key": "M05:3", "module": "M05", "q": "<p>What is a cryptarithm or alphametic puzzle?</p>", "a": "<p>A mathematical puzzle where letters represent distinct decimal digits (0-9) and leading letters cannot be zero.</p>"}, {"key": "M05:4", "module": "M05", "q": "<p>What does the Remainder Theorem state for polynomial division?</p>", "a": "<p>When a polynomial P(x) is divided by a linear factor (x - c), the remainder is simply the value P(c).</p>"}, {"key": "M05:5", "module": "M05", "q": "<p>What is the difference between an algebraic expression and an algebraic equation?</p>", "a": "<p>An expression is a combination of variables and operations without an equals sign (2x + 3); an equation asserts equality between expressions (2x + 3 = 7).</p>"}, {"key": "M06:0", "module": "M06", "q": "<p>What is the sum of interior angles of an $n$-sided polygon?</p>", "a": "<p>$(n-2) \\times 180^\\circ$</p>"}, {"key": "M06:1", "module": "M06", "q": "<p>What does the Triangle Inequality Theorem state?</p>", "a": "<p>The sum of the lengths of any two sides must be strictly greater than the third side.</p>"}, {"key": "M06:2", "module": "M06", "q": "<p>What is the sum of the exterior angles of any convex polygon?</p>", "a": "<p>360 degrees.</p>"}, {"key": "M06:3", "module": "M06", "q": "<p>Why does the SSA (Side-Side-Angle) condition fail to prove triangle congruence?</p>", "a": "<p>Because it can form two entirely different triangles (the ambiguous case), one acute and one obtuse.</p>"}, {"key": "M06:4", "module": "M06", "q": "<p>What is a transversal line?</p>", "a": "<p>A line that passes through two or more other lines.</p>"}, {"key": "M06:5", "module": "M06", "q": "<p>What are corresponding angles?</p>", "a": "<p>Angles in the same relative position at each intersection where a straight line crosses others.</p>"}, {"key": "M08:0", "module": "M08", "q": "<p>What is the formula for the area of a circle?</p>", "a": "<p>$\\pi r^2$</p>"}, {"key": "M08:1", "module": "M08", "q": "<p>What is the Pythagorean Theorem?</p>", "a": "<p>$a^2 + b^2 = c^2$</p>"}, {"key": "M08:2", "module": "M08", "q": "<p>How do you find the volume of a cuboid?</p>", "a": "<p>Length $\\times$ Width $\\times$ Height</p>"}, {"key": "M08:3", "module": "M08", "q": "<p>If you triple the side lengths of a square, what happens to its area?</p>", "a": "<p>It becomes 9 times larger ($3^2 = 9$).</p>"}, {"key": "M08:4", "module": "M08", "q": "<p>What is the perimeter of a shape?</p>", "a": "<p>The total length of the outside boundary of the shape.</p>"}, {"key": "M08:5", "module": "M08", "q": "<p>What is the formula for the area of a triangle?</p>", "a": "<p>$\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}$</p>"}, {"key": "M10:0", "module": "M10", "q": "<p>What three things define a random experiment in probability?</p>", "a": "<p>A procedure whose single result is unpredictable, a known set of possible outcomes called the sample space, and events defined as subsets of that sample space.</p>"}, {"key": "M10:1", "module": "M10", "q": "<p>Why must every probability lie between 0 and 1?</p>", "a": "<p>Because a favourable count cannot be negative and cannot exceed the total count, so the ratio is pinned between 0 and 1.</p>"}, {"key": "M10:2", "module": "M10", "q": "<p>State the complement rule and say why it is useful.</p>", "a": "<p>P(not E) = 1 - P(E). It turns a hard \"at least one\" count into an easy \"none at all\" product.</p>"}, {"key": "M10:3", "module": "M10", "q": "<p>What is the gambler's fallacy, in one sentence?</p>", "a": "<p>Believing that an independent mechanism owes you a compensating result because of what it did before.</p>"}, {"key": "M10:4", "module": "M10", "q": "<p>Does the law of large numbers say the count of heads approaches half the flips?</p>", "a": "<p>No. It says the proportion approaches one half. The expected gap in the raw count actually grows, roughly like the square root of the number of flips.</p>"}, {"key": "M10:5", "module": "M10", "q": "<p>What are the two rules for reading a tree diagram?</p>", "a": "<p>Multiply the probabilities along a path, and add the probabilities of the separate paths that satisfy the event.</p>"}, {"key": "M10:6", "module": "M10", "q": "<p>When does the past genuinely change the next probability?</p>", "a": "<p>When the mechanism has memory, such as drawing without replacement, where each draw changes what remains.</p>"}, {"key": "M10:7", "module": "M10", "q": "<p>If one trial succeeds with probability p, what is the chance of at least one success in n independent trials?</p>", "a": "<p>One minus the probability of no successes, which is 1 - (1 - p) to the power n.</p>"}, {"key": "M14:0", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>Give the sum of an arithmetic progression and the one-line reason it is true.</p>", "a": "<p>n/2 times (first + last). Writing the sum forwards and backwards and adding makes every column identical.</p>"}, {"key": "M14:1", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>When does an infinite geometric series converge, and to what?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly when the magnitude of the ratio is below 1, and then the sum is a/(1-r).</p>"}, {"key": "M14:2", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>The terms of a series tend to zero. Does it converge?</p>", "a": "<p>Not necessarily. The harmonic series is the standard counterexample: its terms tend to zero and it diverges.</p>"}, {"key": "M14:3", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>Why is appending to a doubling array O(1) amortised?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the total copies over n appends form a geometric series bounded by 2n, so the average per append is constant. Most of the cost is in the final doubling.</p>"}, {"key": "M14:4", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>What goes wrong if an array grows by a fixed number of slots instead of a fixed factor?</p>", "a": "<p>The total copying becomes 1+2+3+... which is quadratic, so each append costs linear time on average.</p>"}, {"key": "M14:5", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>What is a telescoping sum?</p>", "a": "<p>One where each term splits into pieces that cancel against neighbouring terms, leaving only the two ends.</p>"}, {"key": "M14:6", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>State AM-GM and its equality condition.</p>", "a": "<p>The arithmetic mean of non-negative numbers is at least their geometric mean, with equality exactly when all the numbers are equal.</p>"}, {"key": "M14:7", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>What is remarkable about the Koch snowflake's perimeter and area?</p>", "a": "<p>The perimeter grows by a factor of 4/3 each iteration and is unbounded, while the area converges to 8/5 of the starting triangle.</p>"}, {"key": "M14:8", "module": "M14", "q": "<p>Who found the series pi/4 = 1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - ... and what did he also understand about it?</p>", "a": "<p>Madhava of Sangamagrama, around 1400, in the Kerala school. He also gave correction terms, showing he knew how slowly it converges.</p>"}, {"key": "P01:0", "module": "P01", "q": "<p>What is two's complement?</p>", "a": "<p>A binary signed number representation where the MSB has negative weight -2^(N-1), avoiding dual zeros and unifying addition and subtraction circuits.</p>"}, {"key": "P01:1", "module": "P01", "q": "<p>How does UTF-8 encode characters?</p>", "a": "<p>Using 1 to 4 bytes per Unicode code point, remaining backward compatible with 7-bit ASCII and avoiding embedded null bytes.</p>"}, {"key": "P01:2", "module": "P01", "q": "<p>What does x &amp; (x - 1) compute?</p>", "a": "<p>Clears the lowest set bit in x; useful for counting set bits and testing powers of two.</p>"}, {"key": "P01:3", "module": "P01", "q": "<p>What does x &amp; (-x) compute?</p>", "a": "<p>Isolates the lowest set bit in x by exploiting two's complement negation -x = ~x + 1.</p>"}, {"key": "P01:4", "module": "P01", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Big-Endian and Little-Endian?</p>", "a": "<p>Big-endian places the most significant byte at the lowest memory address; little-endian places the least significant byte first.</p>"}, {"key": "P01:5", "module": "P01", "q": "<p>What is a Q16.16 fixed-point number?</p>", "a": "<p>A 32-bit number with 16 integer bits and 16 fractional bits, representing real values with scale factor 2^-16.</p>"}, {"key": "P02:0", "module": "P02", "q": "<p>What is the LEGB rule?</p>", "a": "<p>Local, Enclosing, Global, Builtin: the hierarchical lookup order for variable identifiers in lexical scope.</p>"}, {"key": "P02:1", "module": "P02", "q": "<p>What is the difference between identity and equality in Python?</p>", "a": "<p>Identity (is) tests if two references point to the exact same memory address; equality (==) tests value equivalence.</p>"}, {"key": "P02:2", "module": "P02", "q": "<p>What is an aliasing bug?</p>", "a": "<p>A bug caused when mutating a mutable object unexpectedly mutates another variable that references the exact same object in heap memory.</p>"}, {"key": "P02:3", "module": "P02", "q": "<p>How does 'git bisect' locate a bug?</p>", "a": "<p>By executing binary search over commit history in O(log N) evaluations rather than testing all N commits sequentially.</p>"}, {"key": "P02:4", "module": "P02", "q": "<p>What is a minimal reproducible example (MRE)?</p>", "a": "<p>The smallest possible self-contained code snippet that reliably reproduces a bug, stripping all extraneous logic.</p>"}, {"key": "P02:5", "module": "P02", "q": "<p>What causes a RecursionError?</p>", "a": "<p>Exceeding the maximum call stack limit when a recursive function fails to hit its base case or recurses too deeply.</p>"}, {"key": "M07:0", "module": "M07", "q": "<p>What is a parallelogram?</p>", "a": "<p>A quadrilateral where opposite sides are parallel and equal.</p>"}, {"key": "M07:1", "module": "M07", "q": "<p>What is the relationship between the radius and diameter of a circle?</p>", "a": "<p>$d = 2r$</p>"}, {"key": "M07:2", "module": "M07", "q": "<p>What is a tangent to a circle?</p>", "a": "<p>A line that touches the circle at exactly one point.</p>"}, {"key": "M07:3", "module": "M07", "q": "<p>What does it mean for two triangles to be similar?</p>", "a": "<p>Their corresponding angles are equal, and corresponding sides are proportional.</p>"}, {"key": "M07:4", "module": "M07", "q": "<p>What is the sum of angles in a quadrilateral?</p>", "a": "<p>360 degrees.</p>"}, {"key": "M07:5", "module": "M07", "q": "<p>If two triangles have the same three angles, are they congruent?</p>", "a": "<p>Not necessarily, they are similar (AA Similarity).</p>"}, {"key": "M11:0", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>State precisely when a fraction has a terminating decimal expansion.</p>", "a": "<p>When, reduced to lowest terms, its denominator has no prime factors other than 2 and 5.</p>"}, {"key": "M11:1", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>Why is a repeating decimal always rational?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the repeating block lets you write it as a finite geometric series, whose sum is a ratio of integers.</p>"}, {"key": "M11:2", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>What is the remainder when p(x) is divided by (x - a), and why does that matter?</p>", "a": "<p>It is p(a). It matters because it turns a division into a single evaluation.</p>"}, {"key": "M11:3", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>Give the factor theorem in both directions.</p>", "a": "<p>(x - a) divides p(x) if and only if p(a) = 0. A root gives a factor, and a factor gives a root.</p>"}, {"key": "M11:4", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>For a quadratic ax^2 + bx + c with roots alpha and beta, what are their sum and product?</p>", "a": "<p>The sum is -b/a and the product is c/a.</p>"}, {"key": "M11:5", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>Sketch the proof that the square root of 2 is irrational.</p>", "a": "<p>Assume it is p/q in lowest terms, square to get p^2 = 2q^2, deduce p is even, substitute to deduce q is even, and contradict lowest terms.</p>"}, {"key": "M11:6", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>What is the period of the decimal expansion of 1/p for a prime p other than 2 or 5, and what does it divide?</p>", "a": "<p>It is the smallest k with 10^k congruent to 1 modulo p, and it always divides p - 1.</p>"}, {"key": "M11:7", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>Why does cancelling (x-1) from (x^2-1)/(x-1) need a caveat?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the original expression is undefined at x = 1 while x + 1 is defined there, so the simplification holds only for x not equal to 1.</p>"}, {"key": "M11:8", "module": "M11", "q": "<p>How do you rationalise 1/(sqrt(a) + sqrt(b))?</p>", "a": "<p>Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate sqrt(a) - sqrt(b), which makes the denominator the integer a - b.</p>"}, {"key": "M12:0", "module": "M12", "q": "<p>What does a single linear equation in two variables represent geometrically?</p>", "a": "<p>A straight line, that is, the infinite set of (x, y) pairs satisfying it.</p>"}, {"key": "M12:1", "module": "M12", "q": "<p>What are the three possible outcomes for a pair of linear equations, and what are they called?</p>", "a": "<p>One crossing point (consistent, unique), no crossing (inconsistent, parallel), or the same line twice (dependent, infinitely many solutions).</p>"}, {"key": "M12:2", "module": "M12", "q": "<p>What quantity decides whether a 2x2 system has a unique solution, and what is its geometric meaning?</p>", "a": "<p>The determinant a1<em>b2 - a2</em>b1. It is the area of the parallelogram spanned by the coefficient vectors, so a zero determinant means the two directions have collapsed into one.</p>"}, {"key": "M12:3", "module": "M12", "q": "<p>Why can the determinant alone not distinguish parallel from coincident lines?</p>", "a": "<p>Because it uses only the coefficients of x and y. Whether the lines coincide depends on the constant terms as well.</p>"}, {"key": "M12:4", "module": "M12", "q": "<p>State the section formula and say which point the weight m multiplies.</p>", "a": "<p>The point is ((m<em>x2 + n</em>x1)/(m+n), (m<em>y2 + n</em>y1)/(m+n)). The weight m multiplies the coordinates of the second point.</p>"}, {"key": "M12:5", "module": "M12", "q": "<p>How do you test three points for collinearity using coordinates?</p>", "a": "<p>Compute the triangle's area with the shoelace formula; the points are collinear exactly when it is zero.</p>"}, {"key": "M12:6", "module": "M12", "q": "<p>The distance formula is really which older theorem?</p>", "a": "<p>Pythagoras, applied to the right triangle whose legs are the horizontal and vertical gaps between the points.</p>"}, {"key": "M12:7", "module": "M12", "q": "<p>How do you handle a system like 2/x + 3/y = 13?</p>", "a": "<p>Substitute u = 1/x and v = 1/y to get a linear system, solve it, then invert u and v to recover x and y.</p>"}, {"key": "M13:0", "module": "M13", "q": "<p>What is the discriminant and what does each of its three cases mean?</p>", "a": "<p>b^2 - 4ac. Positive means two distinct real roots, zero means one repeated real root, negative means two complex conjugate roots.</p>"}, {"key": "M13:1", "module": "M13", "q": "<p>Which step in completing the square is the one that matters, and why that number?</p>", "a": "<p>Adding (b/2a)^2 to both sides, because that is exactly the number that turns the left side into a perfect square.</p>"}, {"key": "M13:2", "module": "M13", "q": "<p>State Vieta's relations for a quadratic.</p>", "a": "<p>The roots sum to -b/a and multiply to c/a.</p>"}, {"key": "M13:3", "module": "M13", "q": "<p>Why can the textbook quadratic formula lose all precision?</p>", "a": "<p>When b^2 is much larger than 4ac, sqrt(D) is nearly equal to |b|, so one of the numerators subtracts two nearly equal numbers and the leading digits cancel.</p>"}, {"key": "M13:4", "module": "M13", "q": "<p>Give the numerically stable way to compute both roots.</p>", "a": "<p>Let q = -(b + sign(b)*sqrt(D))/2. Then the roots are q/a and c/q. The first never cancels, and the second uses division rather than subtraction.</p>"}, {"key": "M13:5", "module": "M13", "q": "<p>For real coefficients, why do complex roots always come in pairs?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the only complex part comes from the square root of a negative discriminant, which enters as plus or minus the same imaginary quantity, giving conjugates.</p>"}, {"key": "M13:6", "module": "M13", "q": "<p>A word problem gives roots 4 and -9 for a length. What do you do?</p>", "a": "<p>Discard -9. The equation answers a question about numbers; checking which roots are physically admissible is a separate step you must perform.</p>"}, {"key": "M13:7", "module": "M13", "q": "<p>What does a zero discriminant look like on the graph?</p>", "a": "<p>The parabola touches the horizontal axis at exactly one point, its vertex, without crossing.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:0", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>Why do the trigonometric ratios depend only on the angle and not the triangle's size?</p>", "a": "<p>Because two right triangles sharing an acute angle are similar, and similar triangles have proportional sides.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:1", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>Derive sin 30 and cos 30 from a picture.</p>", "a": "<p>Drop a perpendicular in an equilateral triangle of side 2. It gives a right triangle with hypotenuse 2, base 1 and height root 3, so sin 30 = 1/2 and cos 30 = root 3 over 2.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:2", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>Why is sin^2 + cos^2 = 1 not a separate fact to memorise?</p>", "a": "<p>It is Pythagoras for the triangle, divided through by the square of the hypotenuse.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:3", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>State the addition formulas and the trick for getting the signs right.</p>", "a": "<p>sin(A+B) = sinA cosB + cosA sinB and cos(A+B) = cosA cosB - sinA sinB. They are the entries of the product of two rotation matrices, which fixes the minus sign.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:4", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>What is a radian, and why not just use degrees?</p>", "a": "<p>The angle subtending an arc equal to the radius. Radians are required for the derivative of sine to be cosine and for sin(x)/x to tend to 1.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:5", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>Give the principal-value ranges of arcsin, arccos and arctan.</p>", "a": "<p>[-pi/2, pi/2], [0, pi], and the open interval (-pi/2, pi/2).</p>"}, {"key": "M15:6", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>Why does arcsin(x) + arccos(x) always equal pi/2?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the two angles are complementary: if sin A = x then cos(pi/2 - A) = x, and both results lie in their principal branches.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:7", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>When is the sine rule ambiguous, and what do you use instead?</p>", "a": "<p>In the SSA case, where arcsin cannot distinguish an angle from its supplement. Use the cosine rule, which has a unique solution.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:8", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>How accurate is sin x = x, and how does the error scale?</p>", "a": "<p>The error is about x^3/6, so dividing the angle by 10 divides the error by 1000.</p>"}, {"key": "M15:9", "module": "M15", "q": "<p>Why is atan2 preferred to acos in geometry code?</p>", "a": "<p>acos has an infinite derivative at plus or minus 1, so it is ill-conditioned for angles near 0 and pi, while atan2 is well conditioned and resolves the quadrant.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:0", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>What is the difference between the codomain and the range?</p>", "a": "<p>The codomain is the declared target set; the range is the set of values actually attained. Surjective means they coincide.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:1", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>Why does a set with n elements have 2^n subsets?</p>", "a": "<p>Each element is independently in or out, giving a bijection between subsets and binary strings of length n.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:2", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>State both De Morgan laws in one sentence.</p>", "a": "<p>Complementing a union gives the intersection of the complements, and complementing an intersection gives the union of the complements.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:3", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>What three properties make an equivalence relation, and what do they buy you?</p>", "a": "<p>Reflexive, symmetric and transitive. Together they partition the set into disjoint equivalence classes covering everything.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:4", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>Give a relation that is symmetric and transitive but not reflexive.</p>", "a": "<p>The empty relation on a non-empty set. The usual \"proof\" that symmetry plus transitivity implies reflexivity needs an element to be related to something first.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:5", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>Sketch Cantor's diagonal argument.</p>", "a": "<p>Assume the reals in [0,1] are listed. Build a number differing from the k-th listed number in its k-th digit. It is in [0,1] but on no row, contradicting completeness of the list.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:6", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>Are the rationals countable? What about the reals?</p>", "a": "<p>The rationals are countable, by enumerating pairs along diagonals. The reals are not, by the diagonal argument.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:7", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>When does a function have an inverse?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly when it is a bijection: injective so the inverse is well defined, and surjective so the inverse is defined everywhere on the codomain.</p>"}, {"key": "M16:8", "module": "M16", "q": "<p>What is the floor of -2.3, and why do people get it wrong?</p>", "a": "<p>Minus 3. The floor is the greatest integer not exceeding the value, which is not the same as truncating towards zero.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:0", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>When is the implication p -&gt; q false?</p>", "a": "<p>Only when p is true and q is false. In every other case it is true, including vacuously when p is false.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:1", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>Which of converse, inverse and contrapositive is equivalent to the original implication?</p>", "a": "<p>Only the contrapositive, not q -&gt; not p. The converse and inverse are equivalent to each other but not to the original.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:2", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>How do you negate \"for all x, P(x)\"?</p>", "a": "<p>\"There exists an x with not P(x)\". The quantifier flips and the negation moves inside.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:3", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>State modus ponens and modus tollens, and name the two fallacies that mimic them.</p>", "a": "<p>From p-&gt;q and p conclude q; from p-&gt;q and not q conclude not p. The fallacies are affirming the consequent (p-&gt;q, q, so p) and denying the antecedent (p-&gt;q, not p, so not q).</p>"}, {"key": "M17:4", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>Why does induction need both a base case and an inductive step?</p>", "a": "<p>The step only propagates truth; without a true starting point it propagates nothing. \"n = n+1\" satisfies a step and is false everywhere.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:5", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>What is an invariant and why is it so powerful?</p>", "a": "<p>A property preserved by every permitted move. It settles every reachable state at once, including the infinitely many you could never enumerate.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:6", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>How does the colouring argument show the mutilated chessboard cannot be tiled?</p>", "a": "<p>Every domino covers one square of each colour, so a tiling needs equal counts. Removing two same-coloured corners leaves 32 and 30, so no tiling exists.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:7", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>How many counterexamples are needed to refute a universal statement?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly one.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:8", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>What does structural induction look like for a binary tree?</p>", "a": "<p>Prove the property for the empty tree or a leaf, then assume it for both subtrees and prove it for a node built from them.</p>"}, {"key": "M17:9", "module": "M17", "q": "<p>Why is \"I tested a thousand cases\" not a proof?</p>", "a": "<p>Because a universal claim covers cases you did not test. Testing can refute a claim with one counterexample but can never confirm one.</p>"}, {"key": "V01:0", "module": "V01", "q": "<p>What is the core epistemological difference between a mathematical theorem and a scientific theory?</p>", "a": "<p>A theorem is deductively proven from axioms and cannot be falsified by empirical observation. A scientific theory is an empirical model supported by observations that remains fundamentally falsifiable.</p>"}, {"key": "V01:1", "module": "V01", "q": "<p>What is Hume's problem of induction?</p>", "a": "<p>The logical impossibility of justifying general universal claims about unobserved instances based solely on finite past observations.</p>"}, {"key": "V01:2", "module": "V01", "q": "<p>What does George Box's aphorism \"All models are wrong, but some are useful\" mean?</p>", "a": "<p>Every model is an abstraction that discards real-world complexity; its value lies not in literal physical identity with reality, but in useful predictive power.</p>"}, {"key": "V01:3", "module": "V01", "q": "<p>Why does Karl Popper argue that falsifiability, rather than verification, demarcates science?</p>", "a": "<p>Because no finite number of confirming observations can prove a universal statement true, but a single counterexample can prove it false.</p>"}, {"key": "V01:4", "module": "V01", "q": "<p>What are the four stages of the mathematical modelling cycle?</p>", "a": "<p>Formulate (abstract reality into math), Solve (calculate properties), Interpret (translate to real-world context), and Validate (compare against empirical observations).</p>"}, {"key": "V01:5", "module": "V01", "q": "<p>Why does passing 10,000 automated tests fail to equal a proof of program correctness?</p>", "a": "<p>Tests evaluate only a finite subset of inputs; unless the input space is exhaustively checked or formally verified with invariants, untested inputs may contain defect-triggering states.</p>"}, {"key": "M18:0", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>What is the single idea that turns a permutation count into a combination count?</p>", "a": "<p>The division principle: the ordered count sees each unordered selection exactly r! times, so divide by r!.</p>"}, {"key": "M18:1", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>Why is 0! defined as 1?</p>", "a": "<p>Because there is exactly one arrangement of nothing, and because the permutation and combination formulas require it at their boundaries.</p>"}, {"key": "M18:2", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>Give the combinatorial proof of Pascal's identity.</p>", "a": "<p>Count the r-subsets of an n-set by whether they contain element n. If they do, the rest is an (r-1)-subset of n-1; if not, it is an r-subset of n-1. The cases are disjoint and exhaustive.</p>"}, {"key": "M18:3", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>Why are the binomial coefficients combinations?</p>", "a": "<p>Expanding (a+b)^n means picking a or b from each of n brackets. The coefficient of a^(n-r) b^r counts which r brackets gave the b, which is C(n,r).</p>"}, {"key": "M18:4", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>State the pigeonhole principle in its sharper form.</p>", "a": "<p>Placing n items into m boxes forces some box to hold at least the ceiling of n/m.</p>"}, {"key": "M18:5", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>How many ways to put n identical items into k distinct boxes, empties allowed?</p>", "a": "<p>C(n+k-1, k-1), by placing k-1 bars among n stars.</p>"}, {"key": "M18:6", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>What is a derangement, and what does D(n)/n! tend to?</p>", "a": "<p>A permutation with no fixed point. The ratio tends to 1/e, about 0.3679, and converges very fast.</p>"}, {"key": "M18:7", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>When is the binomial series for (1+x)^n valid for non-integer n?</p>", "a": "<p>Only for |x| &lt; 1. Outside that radius the series diverges even though the function is perfectly well defined.</p>"}, {"key": "M18:8", "module": "M18", "q": "<p>Why can computing C(n,r) as n!/(r!(n-r)!) overflow unnecessarily?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the intermediate factorials are enormous even when the answer is small. Multiplying and dividing alternately keeps the running value near the final size.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:0", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>What does conditioning on B do to the sample space?</p>", "a": "<p>It narrows it to B, and renormalises by dividing by P(B) so the new probabilities sum to 1.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:1", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>Why are P(A|B) and P(B|A) different?</p>", "a": "<p>They divide the same joint probability by different things. When P(A) and P(B) differ greatly, so do the two conditionals.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:2", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>State Bayes' theorem and name its four parts.</p>", "a": "<p>Posterior equals prior times likelihood divided by evidence, where the evidence is the total probability of the observation.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:3", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>A test is 99% sensitive with a 5% false-positive rate, applied to a 1%-prevalent disease. What is P(disease | positive)?</p>", "a": "<p>About 1 in 6, because 99 true positives are swamped by 495 false ones out of 10,000 people.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:4", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>Are mutually exclusive events independent?</p>", "a": "<p>No, the opposite. Disjoint events of positive probability are maximally dependent, since one occurring rules out the other.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:5", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>Which adds without conditions, expectation or variance?</p>", "a": "<p>Expectation always adds. Variance adds only when the variables are independent.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:6", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>Give the mean and variance of a binomial, and say where independence is needed.</p>", "a": "<p>np and np(1-p). The mean follows from linearity alone; the variance needs the trials to be independent.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:7", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>What is the relation between Var(X) and E[X^2]?</p>", "a": "<p>Var(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2, so the two square-quantities differ by exactly the variance.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:8", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>For a rare condition, is it better to improve sensitivity or reduce the false-positive rate?</p>", "a": "<p>Reduce the false-positive rate. The posterior is governed by prevalence times sensitivity against the false-positive rate, and sensitivity is already near its ceiling.</p>"}, {"key": "M27:9", "module": "M27", "q": "<p>How do precision and recall relate to conditional probability?</p>", "a": "<p>Recall is P(flagged | bad) and precision is P(bad | flagged) \u2014 the two directions of the same conditional, and not interchangeable.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:0", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>What is the geometric meaning of multiplying by a complex number?</p>", "a": "<p>Scale by its modulus and rotate by its argument. Moduli multiply and arguments add.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:1", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>Why is the real part of (a+bi)(c+di) equal to ac - bd?</p>", "a": "<p>Expanding gives a bd term multiplied by i squared, which is -1, so it subtracts.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:2", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>Why does the minus sign in complex multiplication match the minus sign in cos(A+B)?</p>", "a": "<p>Because they are the same fact: complex multiplication is the rotation matrix, and the addition formulas are its entries.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:3", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>State Euler's formula and what it says geometrically.</p>", "a": "<p>e^(i theta) = cos theta + i sin theta: the point at angle theta on the unit circle.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:4", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>How many n-th roots does a non-zero complex number have, and how are they arranged?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly n, equally spaced by 2*pi/n in angle, all with the same modulus.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:5", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>Why do the n-th roots of unity sum to zero for n at least 2?</p>", "a": "<p>They are a geometric progression with ratio omega, and the GP sum is (omega^n - 1)/(omega - 1), whose numerator vanishes.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:6", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>Why must you use atan2 rather than atan(b/a) for the argument?</p>", "a": "<p>Because atan loses the quadrant: two numbers in opposite quadrants give the same ratio b/a.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:7", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>Why is sqrt(a)*sqrt(b) = sqrt(ab) not valid for complex numbers?</p>", "a": "<p>The square root is two-valued, and no choice of branch preserves the identity everywhere. It is proved only for non-negative reals.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:8", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>What is the connection between complex numbers and the Fourier transform?</p>", "a": "<p>The DFT multiplies the data by powers of a root of unity, so one complex exponential carries an oscillation's amplitude and phase together.</p>"}, {"key": "M19:9", "module": "M19", "q": "<p>A real cosine of amplitude 3 is transformed. Why does the bin show 1.5?</p>", "a": "<p>Because a real cosine is the sum of two counter-rotating complex exponentials, so its energy splits between bins k and N-k.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:0", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>What is the one rule that makes solving an inequality different from solving an equation?</p>", "a": "<p>Multiplying or dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:1", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>State the corner-point theorem and the reason it is true.</p>", "a": "<p>If a linear program has an optimum, some corner attains it. Every feasible point is a weighted average of corners, and a linear objective at an average is the average of the objectives, which cannot exceed the best corner.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:2", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>Why is the feasible region always convex?</p>", "a": "<p>It is an intersection of half-planes, each convex, and intersections of convex sets are convex.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:3", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>Name the three possible outcomes of a linear program.</p>", "a": "<p>Optimal, infeasible (no feasible point), or unbounded (the objective improves forever).</p>"}, {"key": "M20:4", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>Does an unbounded feasible region imply an unbounded optimum?</p>", "a": "<p>No. Minimising x+y over the non-negative quadrant has an infinite region and an optimum at the origin.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:5", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>What does the simplex method do, and why does it not get stuck?</p>", "a": "<p>It walks from corner to adjacent corner, improving each step. Convexity plus a linear objective means any local optimum is global.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:6", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>State weak duality and say what it is good for.</p>", "a": "<p>Any dual-feasible point gives an upper bound on the primal maximum. It certifies optimality without solving the problem.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:7", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>What do the dual variables mean in practice?</p>", "a": "<p>They are shadow prices: the improvement in the optimum per extra unit of each resource.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:8", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>Why is the greedy \"make whatever earns most\" plan usually wrong?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the constraints interact. Using all of one resource on the most profitable product typically leaves another resource idle.</p>"}, {"key": "M20:9", "module": "M20", "q": "<p>What happens to linear programming when the variables must be whole numbers?</p>", "a": "<p>It becomes integer programming, which is NP-hard. Rounding the LP answer may be infeasible or far from optimal.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:0", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>What does the dot product measure, and what does the cross product measure?</p>", "a": "<p>The dot product is a scalar measuring agreement, giving angles and projections. The cross product is a vector perpendicular to both, whose length is the area of the parallelogram they span.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:1", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>How do you find the distance from a point to a line or plane?</p>", "a": "<p>Project the vector from any point on the line or plane onto the unit normal. In coordinates this is |A x0 + B y0 + C| over the length of the normal.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:2", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>What single number distinguishes the four conic sections?</p>", "a": "<p>The eccentricity: 0 is a circle, below 1 an ellipse, exactly 1 a parabola, above 1 a hyperbola.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:3", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>For an ellipse, what is constant at every point of the curve?</p>", "a": "<p>The sum of the distances to the two foci, equal to 2a. For a hyperbola it is the difference instead.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:4", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>Where are the foci of an ellipse and of a hyperbola?</p>", "a": "<p>At plus and minus c on the major axis, with c = sqrt(a^2 - b^2) for the ellipse and sqrt(a^2 + b^2) for the hyperbola.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:5", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>What does a zero scalar triple product tell you?</p>", "a": "<p>The three vectors are coplanar, so the parallelepiped they span has zero volume.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:6", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>What are skew lines and how do you detect them?</p>", "a": "<p>Lines in space that are neither parallel nor intersecting. Their directions have a non-zero cross product and the joining vector has a non-zero component along it.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:7", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>Why is the cross product anticommutative?</p>", "a": "<p>Because swapping the two vectors reverses the orientation of the parallelogram, so the perpendicular direction flips.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:8", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>Why do professional geometry routines avoid slopes?</p>", "a": "<p>Slopes are undefined for vertical lines and the angle formula divides by zero exactly when lines are perpendicular. Direction and normal vectors have no such special cases.</p>"}, {"key": "M21:9", "module": "M21", "q": "<p>State Lagrange's identity and what it says.</p>", "a": "<p>|a x b|^2 + (a . b)^2 = |a|^2 |b|^2. The two products split the information in a pair of vectors between them.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:0", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>What does a limit deliberately ignore, and why does that matter?</p>", "a": "<p>The value at the point itself. That is what lets a limit describe 0/0 cases, where the function is undefined exactly where you care.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:1", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>Give the three conditions for continuity at a point.</p>", "a": "<p>f(a) exists, the limit as x approaches a exists, and the two are equal.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:2", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>Why is sin(x)/x -&gt; 1 true only in radians?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the proof compares the sector's area to two triangles, and the sector area is x/2 only when x is measured in radians.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:3", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>State the product and quotient rules.</p>", "a": "<p>(fg)' = f'g + fg', and (f/g)' = (f'g - fg')/g^2. The quotient's minus sign is the usual casualty.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:4", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>Why does differentiability imply continuity but not conversely?</p>", "a": "<p>Because f(x)-f(a) is the difference quotient times (x-a), which tends to a finite number times zero. The converse fails at a corner such as |x| at 0.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:5", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>What is the chain rule and why does it matter beyond calculus?</p>", "a": "<p>(g(f(x)))' = g'(f(x)) f'(x). It is the mechanism of backpropagation through a composition of layers.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:6", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>What is the order of accuracy of the forward and central difference quotients?</p>", "a": "<p>First order in h for forward, second order for central, because the odd Taylor terms cancel in the symmetric version.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:7", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>Why is the smallest step size not the best for numerical differentiation?</p>", "a": "<p>Truncation error shrinks with h but rounding error grows as h shrinks, since you subtract nearly equal numbers. The sum has an interior minimum.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:8", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>State the Mean Value Theorem in plain words.</p>", "a": "<p>If a journey averaged a certain speed, at some instant it was travelling at exactly that speed.</p>"}, {"key": "M22:9", "module": "M22", "q": "<p>How do you differentiate x to the power x?</p>", "a": "<p>Logarithmic differentiation: take logs to get log y = x log x, differentiate implicitly, and obtain x^x (1 + log x).</p>"}, {"key": "M23:0", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>Why does the derivative vanish at an interior maximum?</p>", "a": "<p>The one-sided difference quotients have opposite signs, so the limit must be both at most and at least zero.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:1", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>What is the complete recipe for the absolute maximum on a closed interval?</p>", "a": "<p>Evaluate the function at every critical point inside the interval and at both endpoints, then take the largest.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:2", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>When is the second derivative test inconclusive, and what do you do then?</p>", "a": "<p>When f''(c) = 0. Fall back to the first derivative test and look at the sign change of f'.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:3", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>What distinguishes a point of inflexion from a point where f'' = 0?</p>", "a": "<p>An inflexion requires the second derivative to change sign. x^4 at 0 has f'' = 0 and no inflexion.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:4", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>State Newton's iteration and its geometric meaning.</p>", "a": "<p>x_{n+1} = x_n - f(x_n)/f'(x_n): follow the tangent at the current guess down to the axis.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:5", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>What does quadratic convergence mean in practice?</p>", "a": "<p>The error squares each step, so the number of correct digits roughly doubles per iteration.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:6", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>Give two ways Newton's method can fail.</p>", "a": "<p>A vanishing derivative at an iterate, and a multiple root, where convergence drops from quadratic to linear. It can also cycle or diverge from a poor start.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:7", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>How accurate is the tangent-line approximation?</p>", "a": "<p>Its error is of order h squared, so halving the step quarters the error.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:8", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>What does convexity buy you in optimisation?</p>", "a": "<p>Every local minimum is global, so a method that goes downhill cannot get stuck in the wrong place.</p>"}, {"key": "M23:9", "module": "M23", "q": "<p>Why does \"increasing implies f' &gt; 0\" need care?</p>", "a": "<p>x^3 is strictly increasing yet has a zero derivative at the origin. The correct statement is f' &gt;= 0.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:0", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>State both parts of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.</p>", "a": "<p>Part 1: the derivative of the integral from a to x of f is f(x). Part 2: the integral from a to b equals F(b) - F(a) for any antiderivative F.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:1", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>Why does the power rule for integration need a special case at n = -1?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the rule divides by n+1, which is zero there. The logarithm fills that gap.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:2", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>What happens to the limits when you substitute u = g(x) in a definite integral?</p>", "a": "<p>They become g(a) and g(b). Leaving them unchanged computes a different integral.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:3", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>Where does integration by parts come from?</p>", "a": "<p>Integrating the product rule and rearranging. It is the product rule read backwards.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:4", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>Does the integral give you the area under a curve?</p>", "a": "<p>It gives the <em>signed</em> area. For enclosed area you must integrate the absolute value, or split at the zeros.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:5", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>What are the error orders of the trapezoid and Simpson rules?</p>", "a": "<p>Order h^2 for the trapezoid, order h^4 for Simpson, whose error involves the fourth derivative.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:6", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>Why can you not measure Simpson's error order on a cubic?</p>", "a": "<p>Because Simpson is exact for every polynomial of degree at most 3, so the error is identically zero and reveals nothing.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:7", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>Give three elementary functions with no elementary antiderivative.</p>", "a": "<p>e^(-x^2), sin(x)/x, and 1/log(x). This is Liouville's theorem, not a lack of cleverness.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:8", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>Why does the Fundamental Theorem's proof need the Mean Value Theorem?</p>", "a": "<p>To conclude that two antiderivatives differ by a constant, since a function with zero derivative on an interval must be constant.</p>"}, {"key": "M24:9", "module": "M24", "q": "<p>What is the practical meaning of integrating a sampled signal?</p>", "a": "<p>Accumulation: total energy from power samples, distance from velocity samples, bytes from bandwidth samples. You are computing a Riemann sum.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:0", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>What does a differential equation describe, and why is that useful?</p>", "a": "<p>A relation between a function and its derivatives. It is useful because physical laws state rates, not the quantities themselves.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:1", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>How many arbitrary constants does the general solution of an n-th order equation have?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly n, and n initial or boundary conditions are needed to pin down a particular solution.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:2", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>Where does the integrating factor come from?</p>", "a": "<p>From requiring that multiplying by mu makes the left side exactly (mu y)', which forces mu' = mu P and so mu = exp(integral of P).</p>"}, {"key": "M25:3", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>In Newton's law of cooling, what decays exponentially?</p>", "a": "<p>The excess temperature over the surroundings, not the temperature itself. So the body approaches ambient without ever reaching it.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:4", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>State the stability condition for explicit Euler on y' = lambda y with lambda negative.</p>", "a": "<p>The step must satisfy |1 + h*lambda| &lt;= 1, that is h &lt; 2/|lambda|. Beyond that the solution explodes while the true one decays.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:5", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>What is the difference between accuracy and stability?</p>", "a": "<p>Accuracy is how close one step is to the truth; stability is whether errors grow or shrink as steps accumulate. A method can be high order and unstable.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:6", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>What is a stiff equation and what do you do about it?</p>", "a": "<p>One where stability, not accuracy, forces a tiny step, usually because of widely separated time scales. Use an implicit method such as backward Euler.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:7", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>Why does RK4 lose energy on an orbit while leapfrog does not?</p>", "a": "<p>RK4 preserves no geometric structure, so its errors accumulate systematically. Leapfrog is symplectic: it exactly conserves a nearby shadow energy, so the true energy only oscillates.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:8", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>Leapfrog is only second order and RK4 is fourth. Why would anyone choose leapfrog?</p>", "a": "<p>For a long simulation, bounded energy error matters more than per-step accuracy. RK4's drift grows without limit; leapfrog's does not.</p>"}, {"key": "M25:9", "module": "M25", "q": "<p>Give an equation whose solution blows up in finite time.</p>", "a": "<p>y' = y^2 with y(0) = 1 has solution 1/(1-t), which escapes at t = 1.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:0", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>Why is matrix multiplication defined by the row-times-column rule?</p>", "a": "<p>Because it is forced by requiring that the matrix product correspond to composing the two transformations.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:1", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>Why is AB generally different from BA?</p>", "a": "<p>Because doing transformation B then A is generally not the same as doing A then B. Order matters for transformations.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:2", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>What does the determinant mean geometrically?</p>", "a": "<p>Its magnitude is the factor by which areas or volumes are scaled, and its sign says whether orientation is preserved.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:3", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>What does a zero determinant tell you?</p>", "a": "<p>The transformation collapses space into a lower dimension, so the matrix has no inverse and the linear system has no unique solution.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:4", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>What is the relation between the adjoint and the inverse?</p>", "a": "<p>A times adj(A) equals det(A) times the identity, so the inverse is adj(A) divided by det(A).</p>"}, {"key": "M26:5", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>State Cramer's rule and its practical limitation.</p>", "a": "<p>x_i is det(A_i)/det(A), where A_i replaces column i with the right-hand side. It needs n+1 determinants, so it is unusable beyond tiny systems.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:6", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>Why do elementary row operations not change the solution set?</p>", "a": "<p>Each one is reversible, so any solution of the new system corresponds to one of the old. That is why scaling by zero is forbidden.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:7", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>What is (AB)^-1, and why?</p>", "a": "<p>B^-1 A^-1. To undo \"do B then A\" you must undo A first.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:8", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>Is a small determinant a reliable sign that a matrix is nearly singular?</p>", "a": "<p>No. Scaling a matrix changes the determinant by k^n without changing how invertible it is. Use the condition number instead.</p>"}, {"key": "M26:9", "module": "M26", "q": "<p>Why should you rarely compute an explicit matrix inverse?</p>", "a": "<p>Because solving the system directly by elimination is faster and more accurate than forming the inverse and multiplying.</p>"}, {"key": "V02:0", "module": "V02", "q": "<p>What are the four measurement scales defined by S.S. Stevens?</p>", "a": "<p>Nominal (labels), Ordinal (ranks), Interval (equal intervals, arbitrary zero), and Ratio (equal intervals, true zero).</p>"}, {"key": "V02:1", "module": "V02", "q": "<p>Why is calculating the arithmetic mean of Likert ratings invalid?</p>", "a": "<p>Because Likert items are ordinal; arithmetic means assume equal intervals between categories, which is psychologically false and mathematically non-invariant under monotonic transformation.</p>"}, {"key": "V02:2", "module": "V02", "q": "<p>What is the difference between systematic error and random error?</p>", "a": "<p>Systematic error is repeatable bias that shifts the mean away from truth; random error fluctuates symmetrically around the mean with expected value zero.</p>"}, {"key": "V02:3", "module": "V02", "q": "<p>State Goodhart's Law.</p>", "a": "<p>When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure, because agents optimize the proxy metric rather than the underlying construct.</p>"}, {"key": "V02:4", "module": "V02", "q": "<p>What is construct validity?</p>", "a": "<p>The degree to which a test or operational metric genuinely measures the theoretical construct it claims to measure.</p>"}, {"key": "V02:5", "module": "V02", "q": "<p>What does the Buckingham Pi theorem guarantee in dimensional analysis?</p>", "a": "<p>Any physically meaningful equation involving $n$ physical variables can be rewritten in terms of $n - k$ independent dimensionless parameters, where $k$ is the number of fundamental dimensions.</p>"}, {"key": "V03:0", "module": "V03", "q": "<p>What is the Horvitz-Thompson estimator?</p>", "a": "<p>An unbiased estimator of a population total that weights each sampled unit by the reciprocal of its inclusion probability: $\\hat{Y} = \\sum_{i \\in S} y_i / \\pi_i$.</p>"}, {"key": "V03:1", "module": "V03", "q": "<p>What is survivorship bias?</p>", "a": "<p>A logical error where a sample is conditioned on passing a survival filter, causing researchers to overlook non-surviving instances and misjudge causality.</p>"}, {"key": "V03:2", "module": "V03", "q": "<p>Why does stratified sampling reduce variance compared to simple random sampling?</p>", "a": "<p>Because it eliminates between-strata variance from the sampling error by fixing sample allocations within each homogeneous stratum.</p>"}, {"key": "V03:3", "module": "V03", "q": "<p>What does the Design Effect (Deff) quantify in cluster sampling?</p>", "a": "<p>The ratio of the variance of an estimator under a cluster design to the variance under simple random sampling with the same sample size: $\\text{Deff} = 1 + (M - 1)\\rho$.</p>"}, {"key": "V03:4", "module": "V03", "q": "<p>How does non-response bias distort customer satisfaction surveys?</p>", "a": "<p>Dissatisfied users frequently abandon the service without answering surveys, while highly motivated users respond, creating a bimodal or artificially positive illusion.</p>"}, {"key": "V03:5", "module": "V03", "q": "<p>Why does increasing sample size fail to fix selection bias?</p>", "a": "<p>Sample size reduces standard error (variance) toward zero, concentrating the estimate around the biased expected value rather than the true population parameter.</p>"}, {"key": "D01:0", "module": "D01", "q": "<p>What is the formal definition of Big-O notation?</p>", "a": "<p>f(n) is in O(g(n)) if there exist constants c &gt; 0 and n0 &gt; 0 such that f(n) &lt;= c * g(n) for all n &gt;= n0.</p>"}, {"key": "D01:1", "module": "D01", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Big-O, Big-Omega, and Big-Theta?</p>", "a": "<p>Big-O is an asymptotic upper bound, Big-Omega is an asymptotic lower bound, and Big-Theta is an asymptotically tight bound (both upper and lower).</p>"}, {"key": "D01:2", "module": "D01", "q": "<p>What is the Master Theorem for divide-and-conquer recurrences?</p>", "a": "<p>A formula solving T(n) = a*T(n/b) + Theta(n^k log^p n) by comparing log_b(a) with k to determine whether leaves, internal levels, or the root dominates work.</p>"}, {"key": "D01:3", "module": "D01", "q": "<p>Why does dynamic array doubling have O(1) amortized append complexity?</p>", "a": "<p>Because resizing at powers of two copies at most 2N total elements across N appends, amortizing total work to 3 operations per append.</p>"}, {"key": "D01:4", "module": "D01", "q": "<p>How can you verify an algorithm's complexity empirically?</p>", "a": "<p>By plotting log(N) versus log(runtime) across input sizes; the slope of the linear regression line directly reveals the polynomial exponent k.</p>"}, {"key": "D01:5", "module": "D01", "q": "<p>What is the difference between aggregate and potential amortized analysis?</p>", "a": "<p>The aggregate method divides total sequence work by N; the potential method defines a state potential function Phi(D) tracking accumulated credits across operations.</p>"}, {"key": "D02:0", "module": "D02", "q": "<p>What is Kadane's algorithm?</p>", "a": "<p>An optimal O(N) dynamic programming algorithm that finds the contiguous subarray with the maximum sum in a single pass.</p>"}, {"key": "D02:1", "module": "D02", "q": "<p>How does the Difference Array technique achieve O(1) range updates?</p>", "a": "<p>By adding value to D[L] and subtracting value from D[R+1], so that a single prefix sum pass cascades the update across [L, R].</p>"}, {"key": "D02:2", "module": "D02", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of 2D prefix sum submatrix queries?</p>", "a": "<p>O(1) time after an initial O(R * C) precomputation pass.</p>"}, {"key": "D02:3", "module": "D02", "q": "<p>How do you rotate an N x N matrix 90 degrees clockwise in-place?</p>", "a": "<p>First transpose the matrix (swap matrix[i][j] with matrix[j][i]), then reverse each row.</p>"}, {"key": "D02:4", "module": "D02", "q": "<p>When is the two-pointer technique applicable?</p>", "a": "<p>When searching for pairs in sorted arrays or partitioning elements based on monotonic conditions.</p>"}, {"key": "D02:5", "module": "D02", "q": "<p>What is the sliding window technique?</p>", "a": "<p>Maintaining a contiguous subarray window that expands or contracts to track running statistics in linear O(N) time.</p>"}, {"key": "D04:0", "module": "D04", "q": "<p>What is a Monotonic Stack?</p>", "a": "<p>A stack whose elements are maintained in strictly increasing or decreasing order by popping elements that violate the invariant.</p>"}, {"key": "D04:1", "module": "D04", "q": "<p>What problem does a Monotonic Stack solve in O(N) time?</p>", "a": "<p>Finding the Next Greater Element or Nearest Smaller Element for every item in an array.</p>"}, {"key": "D04:2", "module": "D04", "q": "<p>How does a Circular Queue handle wrap-around?</p>", "a": "<p>By advancing head and tail pointers using modulo capacity: (tail + 1) % capacity.</p>"}, {"key": "D04:3", "module": "D04", "q": "<p>What is Dijkstra's Shunting-Yard algorithm?</p>", "a": "<p>A stack-based algorithm that parses infix mathematical expressions into postfix Reverse Polish Notation according to operator precedence.</p>"}, {"key": "D04:4", "module": "D04", "q": "<p>Why does a Monotonic Deque solve Sliding Window Maximum in O(N) time?</p>", "a": "<p>Because every index is added to the deque once and removed from the front or back at most once, bounding total operations to 2N.</p>"}, {"key": "D04:5", "module": "D04", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of pushing to a Monotonic Stack across an entire array?</p>", "a": "<p>Amortized O(1) per element, yielding O(N) total time for an array of size N.</p>"}, {"key": "D05:0", "module": "D05", "q": "<p>What is the primary benefit of sentinel (dummy) nodes in a linked list?</p>", "a": "<p>Sentinels eliminate null-pointer checks for edge insertions and deletions at the head and tail, ensuring every valid node has non-null <code>prev</code> and <code>next</code> references.</p>"}, {"key": "D05:1", "module": "D05", "q": "<p>Why does Floyd's cycle detection guarantee collision if a cycle exists?</p>", "a": "<p>Once both pointers enter the cycle of length $C$, their relative distance increments by 1 step per clock tick, guaranteeing that distance modulo $C$ reaches 0 within at most $C$ steps.</p>"}, {"key": "D05:2", "module": "D05", "q": "<p>How do you find the entrance of a cycle after fast and slow pointers collide?</p>", "a": "<p>Leave one pointer at the collision point, move the other to the list head, and advance both at 1 step per tick. They collide at the cycle entrance after $\\mu$ steps.</p>"}, {"key": "D05:3", "module": "D05", "q": "<p>What two data structures combine to form an O(1) LRU cache?</p>", "a": "<p>A hash map for $O(1)$ key-to-node lookup and a doubly-linked list with sentinels for $O(1)$ node unlinking and splicing at the most-recently-used head.</p>"}, {"key": "D05:4", "module": "D05", "q": "<p>How does an LFU (Least Frequently Used) cache differ from LRU?</p>", "a": "<p>LRU evicts based on access recency; LFU evicts based on access frequency counts, using a frequency map where each frequency links to its own LRU doubly-linked list.</p>"}, {"key": "D05:5", "module": "D05", "q": "<p>How do generators and iterators relate to linked structures?</p>", "a": "<p>Generators yield items lazily on demand without materializing the full sequence in memory, mimicking forward-only singly-linked list traversal while consuming $O(1)$ space.</p>"}, {"key": "D06:0", "module": "D06", "q": "<p>What is the theoretical comparison sort lower bound and why does it exist?</p>", "a": "<p>$\\Omega(N \\log N)$; any comparison algorithm is a binary decision tree with at least $N!$ leaves, requiring tree height $h \\ge \\log_2(N!) \u0007pprox N \\log_2 N - N \\log_2 e$.</p>"}, {"key": "D06:1", "module": "D06", "q": "<p>What makes Dijkstra's 3-way Dutch National Flag partitioning superior to Lomuto?</p>", "a": "<p>It groups all elements equal to the pivot in the middle in a single pass, guaranteeing $O(N)$ runtime on arrays containing identical elements.</p>"}, {"key": "D06:2", "module": "D06", "q": "<p>What are the bounds for binary search on the answer?</p>", "a": "<p>The lower bound is the minimum conceivable answer and the upper bound is the maximum conceivable answer, forming a monotonic boolean predicate range <code>[F, F, ..., T, T]</code>.</p>"}, {"key": "D06:3", "module": "D06", "q": "<p>Why does Merge Sort require O(N) auxiliary space?</p>", "a": "<p>Merging two sorted subarrays requires a temporary buffer to place elements in sorted order without overwriting uninspected elements in the source array.</p>"}, {"key": "D06:4", "module": "D06", "q": "<p>What is an inversion and how fast can inversions be counted?</p>", "a": "<p>An inversion is a pair $(i, j)$ with $i &lt; j$ and $A[i] &gt; A[j]$; it can be counted in $O(N \\log N)$ time by accumulating remaining left-subarray elements during Merge Sort.</p>"}, {"key": "D06:5", "module": "D06", "q": "<p>What is Quickselect's time complexity?</p>", "a": "<p>Average $O(N)$ time and $O(1)$ space because it recurses only into the partition containing the target rank $K$, forming the geometric sum $N + N/2 + N/4 + \\dots = 2N$.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:0", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>State the handshake lemma and one immediate corollary.</p>", "a": "<p>The degrees sum to twice the number of edges. Therefore the number of odd-degree vertices is always even.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:1", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>When does a connected graph have an Euler circuit, and when an Euler path?</p>", "a": "<p>A circuit exactly when every degree is even; a path exactly when zero or two vertices have odd degree.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:2", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>Why did Euler's argument settle K\u00f6nigsberg?</p>", "a": "<p>All four landmasses had odd degree, and at most two odd vertices are permitted, so no traversal exists for anyone.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:3", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>How do Euler and Hamiltonian problems differ computationally?</p>", "a": "<p>Euler is decided in linear time by a degree check; deciding Hamiltonicity is NP-complete.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:4", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>Give three equivalent definitions of a tree.</p>", "a": "<p>Connected and acyclic; connected with n-1 edges; exactly one path between every pair of vertices.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:5", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>What characterises a bipartite graph?</p>", "a": "<p>It is 2-colourable, equivalently it contains no odd cycle.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:6", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>State Euler's formula for planar graphs and one consequence.</p>", "a": "<p>V - E + F = 2 for a connected planar drawing. A consequence is E &lt;= 3V - 6, which rules out K5.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:7", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>How do you solve a linear recurrence with constant coefficients?</p>", "a": "<p>Substitute r^n to get the characteristic equation, find its roots, and fit a combination of r_i^n to the initial conditions.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:8", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>What does \"formal\" mean in formal power series?</p>", "a": "<p>Convergence is irrelevant. The series is a container for the sequence, and x is bookkeeping, so divergent series are perfectly usable.</p>"}, {"key": "M28:9", "module": "M28", "q": "<p>What do the Catalan numbers count, and what is the closed form?</p>", "a": "<p>Balanced bracket strings, binary trees, polygon triangulations and much else. C_n = C(2n,n)/(n+1).</p>"}, {"key": "M29:0", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>When does a number have a multiplicative inverse modulo n?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly when it is coprime to n. The extended Euclidean algorithm constructs the inverse from Bezout's identity.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:1", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>Why can you not always cancel a factor from a congruence?</p>", "a": "<p>Because cancellation is multiplication by an inverse, which exists only when the factor is coprime to the modulus.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:2", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>State Fermat's little theorem and say where primality is used in its proof.</p>", "a": "<p>For prime p not dividing a, a^(p-1) = 1 mod p. Primality is used to conclude that p dividing a product divides a factor.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:3", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>What is a Carmichael number and why does it matter?</p>", "a": "<p>A composite that passes the Fermat test for every coprime base. It shows the Fermat test cannot be repaired by trying more bases. The smallest is 561.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:4", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>How does Miller-Rabin improve on the Fermat test?</p>", "a": "<p>It tracks the square roots of 1 encountered while squaring up to a^(n-1). A prime has only +-1 as square roots of 1; a composite may have others.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:5", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>Why must you reduce modulo n at every step of exponentiation?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the intermediate powers grow astronomically. Reducing keeps every value below n^2 and makes the computation feasible.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:6", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>State the Chinese Remainder Theorem.</p>", "a": "<p>For pairwise coprime moduli, a system of congruences has a unique solution modulo their product.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:7", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>What is Euler's totient and what theorem uses it?</p>", "a": "<p>The count of integers up to n coprime to n. Euler's theorem says a^phi(n) = 1 mod n whenever a is coprime to n.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:8", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>Why do hash tables often use a prime modulus?</p>", "a": "<p>A power-of-two modulus keeps only the low bits, so keys differing in high bits all collide. A prime mixes every bit into the index.</p>"}, {"key": "M29:9", "module": "M29", "q": "<p>Which error does the Luhn check fail to catch, and what fixes it?</p>", "a": "<p>The adjacent transposition 09 to 90. Verhoeff's scheme, based on the dihedral group D5, catches all adjacent transpositions.</p>"}, {"key": "D03:0", "module": "D03", "q": "<p>What is the difference between separate chaining and open addressing?</p>", "a": "<p>Separate chaining stores collisions in external linked lists; open addressing probes for the next available slot within the primary array.</p>"}, {"key": "D03:1", "module": "D03", "q": "<p>Why are tombstones necessary in linear probing hash tables?</p>", "a": "<p>To prevent probe chains from terminating prematurely when searching for elements inserted after a deleted key.</p>"}, {"key": "D03:2", "module": "D03", "q": "<p>What is a Bloom filter and what guarantees does it make?</p>", "a": "<p>A space-efficient probabilistic set that guarantees zero false negatives, though it may produce false positives.</p>"}, {"key": "D03:3", "module": "D03", "q": "<p>How does RandomizedSet achieve O(1) deletion?</p>", "a": "<p>By swapping the element to be deleted with the last element of a dynamic array, updating its index in a hash map, and popping the last element.</p>"}, {"key": "D03:4", "module": "D03", "q": "<p>What is the Rabin-Karp algorithm?</p>", "a": "<p>A string-searching algorithm that uses polynomial rolling hashes to compare substring hashes in O(1) time per window step.</p>"}, {"key": "D03:5", "module": "D03", "q": "<p>What is consistent hashing?</p>", "a": "<p>A hashing technique mapping keys and servers onto a virtual ring so that adding/removing nodes rehashes only a minimal fraction of keys.</p>"}, {"key": "P03:0", "module": "P03", "q": "<p>What is an abstract data type (ADT)?</p>", "a": "<p>A data type defined by its behavior (operations and contracts) from the point of view of a user, independent of implementation.</p>"}, {"key": "P03:1", "module": "P03", "q": "<p>What is the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)?</p>", "a": "<p>If S is a subtype of T, objects of type T may be replaced with objects of type S without altering the correctness of the program.</p>"}, {"key": "P03:2", "module": "P03", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Subtype and Parametric polymorphism?</p>", "a": "<p>Subtype polymorphism relies on subtyping and dynamic dispatch; parametric polymorphism allows generic code across arbitrary type variables.</p>"}, {"key": "P03:3", "module": "P03", "q": "<p>Why is composition preferred over inheritance?</p>", "a": "<p>Composition avoids the fragile base class problem, loose couples components, and allows flexible behavior swapping at runtime.</p>"}, {"key": "P03:4", "module": "P03", "q": "<p>What is a class invariant?</p>", "a": "<p>A condition that must always be true for every valid instance of a class, preserved before and after every public method invocation.</p>"}, {"key": "P03:5", "module": "P03", "q": "<p>How do Result types improve on exceptions?</p>", "a": "<p>Result types make failure paths explicit in the function signature, forcing callers to handle errors at compile-time.</p>"}, {"key": "P04:0", "module": "P04", "q": "<p>What is De Morgan's Law?</p>", "a": "<p>not (A and B) = (not A) or (not B), and not (A or B) = (not A) and (not B).</p>"}, {"key": "P04:1", "module": "P04", "q": "<p>What does it mean that NAND is a universal gate?</p>", "a": "<p>Any boolean logic function or digital circuit can be constructed exclusively using two-input NAND gates.</p>"}, {"key": "P04:2", "module": "P04", "q": "<p>What is the difference between a half adder and a full adder?</p>", "a": "<p>A half adder adds two bits; a full adder adds two bits plus an incoming carry bit (Cin).</p>"}, {"key": "P04:3", "module": "P04", "q": "<p>What is a multiplexer (MUX)?</p>", "a": "<p>A digital switch that directs one of 2^N input lines to a single output line based on N selector lines.</p>"}, {"key": "P04:4", "module": "P04", "q": "<p>What is clock setup time (t_setup)?</p>", "a": "<p>The minimum time an input signal must remain stable before the clock edge to avoid metastability.</p>"}, {"key": "P04:5", "module": "P04", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Moore and Mealy state machines?</p>", "a": "<p>Moore FSM outputs depend solely on the current state; Mealy FSM outputs depend on both current state and current inputs.</p>"}, {"key": "P24:0", "module": "P24", "q": "<p>What lessons did the Therac-25 radiation disaster teach computer science?</p>", "a": "<p>Software is life-critical engineering requiring formal safety interlocks, rigorous unit testing, and management accountability; software cannot blindly replace physical safety relays.</p>"}, {"key": "P24:1", "module": "P24", "q": "<p>What is differential privacy and how does the Laplace mechanism work?</p>", "a": "<p>A mathematical guarantee that query outputs do not reveal whether any individual record was present, achieved by adding Laplace noise calibrated to sensitivity / epsilon.</p>"}, {"key": "P24:2", "module": "P24", "q": "<p>What is the Disparate Impact Four-Fifths Rule in algorithmic fairness?</p>", "a": "<p>A selection standard stating that an automated system has adverse disparate impact if the selection rate of an unprivileged group is less than 80% (0.80) of the privileged group.</p>"}, {"key": "P24:3", "module": "P24", "q": "<p>What are the key obligations under India's DPDP Act 2023?</p>", "a": "<p>Verifiable consent, purpose limitation, data minimization, user right of erasure, and mandatory incident notification to CERT-In and the Data Protection Board.</p>"}, {"key": "P24:4", "module": "P24", "q": "<p>What is the difference between permissive and copyleft open-source licenses?</p>", "a": "<p>Permissive licenses (MIT, Apache) allow closed-source proprietary redistribution with attribution; copyleft licenses (GPL, AGPL) legally compel derivative works to remain open source.</p>"}, {"key": "P24:5", "module": "P24", "q": "<p>Why is algorithmic bias not solved simply by removing protected attributes like gender or race?</p>", "a": "<p>Machine learning models easily reconstruct protected attributes from correlated proxy features (postal code, school, browsing patterns); proactive fairness audits are required.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:0", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>What makes a set of vectors a basis?</p>", "a": "<p>It is independent and it spans. Every vector then has a unique expression in it, and all bases of a space have the same size.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:1", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>Why did adding a third spice packet change nothing?</p>", "a": "<p>Because it was already in the span of the other two. Only independent vectors enlarge a span.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:2", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>State the rank-nullity theorem and its intuition.</p>", "a": "<p>Rank plus nullity equals the dimension of the domain. The map flattens a subspace to zero and carries the rest across faithfully.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:3", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>What are the three conditions for a subspace?</p>", "a": "<p>It contains the zero vector, and it is closed under addition and under scalar multiplication.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:4", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>What does the rank of a matrix count?</p>", "a": "<p>The number of pivots in its echelon form, equivalently the dimension of its column space, equivalently of its row space.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:5", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>When is a linear system inconsistent?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly when the reduced augmented matrix has a pivot in the right-hand-side column.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:6", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>Why does partial pivoting matter?</p>", "a": "<p>A small pivot produces enormous multipliers that amplify rounding error. Pivoting keeps every multiplier at most 1 and makes elimination stable.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:7", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>Is an inaccurate answer always a sign of an ill-conditioned problem?</p>", "a": "<p>No. A well-conditioned problem solved by an unstable algorithm gives garbage. Conditioning is a property of the problem, stability of the method.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:8", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>Why prefer LU to computing an inverse?</p>", "a": "<p>LU costs about a third as much, is more accurate, and one factorisation serves many right-hand sides. The explicit inverse of a sparse matrix is also usually dense.</p>"}, {"key": "M30:9", "module": "M30", "q": "<p>What does a change of basis do to the matrix of a linear map?</p>", "a": "<p>It conjugates it: A becomes B inverse A B. Similar matrices are the same map in different coordinates.</p>"}, {"key": "V12:0", "module": "V12", "q": "<p>What is the Oracle Problem in software testing?</p>", "a": "<p>The challenge of determining whether the observed output of an executed program on arbitrary inputs is correct without human intervention.</p>"}, {"key": "V12:1", "module": "V12", "q": "<p>What is Metamorphic Testing?</p>", "a": "<p>A testing technique that detects defects by checking whether relations between multiple inputs and outputs hold (e.g. $f(2x) = 2f(x)$) without knowing the true output value.</p>"}, {"key": "V12:2", "module": "V12", "q": "<p>What did the Knight-Leveson experiment prove about N-version programming?</p>", "a": "<p>Independently written implementations fail to achieve statistical independence; human developers suffer from correlated common-mode cognitive errors.</p>"}, {"key": "V12:3", "module": "V12", "q": "<p>What is the Parity Oracle pattern?</p>", "a": "<p>A verification pattern that executes a reference engine, compares side-effects, accepts sound supersets, and reports INCONCLUSIVE when stubs are missing.</p>"}, {"key": "V12:4", "module": "V12", "q": "<p>What is the difference between verification and validation (Boehm)?</p>", "a": "<p>Verification checks conformity to specification ('building it right'); validation checks fitness for user purpose ('building the right thing').</p>"}, {"key": "V12:5", "module": "V12", "q": "<p>What is differential testing?</p>", "a": "<p>Providing identical inputs to multiple independent implementations of the same specification and flagging any discrepancy in their outputs as a potential defect.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:0", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>Why can the mean be a bad summary?</p>", "a": "<p>A heavy tail drags it above almost every observation. For skewed data the median and high quantiles describe reality; the mean describes nobody.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:1", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>When does the law of large numbers fail?</p>", "a": "<p>When the mean does not exist, as for a Pareto with alpha at most 1 or a Cauchy. The sample mean then never settles.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:2", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>What does the central limit theorem require?</p>", "a": "<p>Independent identically distributed variables with FINITE variance. The shape of the original distribution is irrelevant.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:3", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>Does zero correlation imply independence?</p>", "a": "<p>No. Correlation measures linear association only. X uniform on {-1,0,1} and Y = X^2 have zero covariance and Y is a function of X.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:4", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>Give the standard error of a sample mean and its practical consequence.</p>", "a": "<p>sigma over root n. To halve the error you need four times the data.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:5", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>State Markov's and Chebyshev's inequalities and what each assumes.</p>", "a": "<p>Markov: P(X &gt;= a) &lt;= E[X]/a, needing only non-negativity. Chebyshev: P(|X-mu| &gt;= k sigma) &lt;= 1/k^2, needing a finite variance. Chebyshev follows from Markov applied to the squared deviation.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:6", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>What makes Hoeffding stronger than Chebyshev?</p>", "a": "<p>It assumes bounded variables and in exchange gives an exponentially small bound rather than a polynomial one.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:7", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>How does the inverse CDF method work, and when can you not use it?</p>", "a": "<p>If U is uniform then F inverse of U has CDF F. It needs an invertible CDF, which the normal lacks \u2014 hence Box-Muller.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:8", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>Why is Monte Carlo integration used in high dimensions?</p>", "a": "<p>Its error falls like 1/sqrt(n) independently of dimension, while grid methods need exponentially many points.</p>"}, {"key": "M34:9", "module": "M34", "q": "<p>What is special about the exponential distribution?</p>", "a": "<p>It is memoryless: having waited tells you nothing about the remaining wait. It is the only continuous distribution with that property.</p>"}, {"key": "V04:0", "module": "V04", "q": "<p>What does Mean Squared Error (MSE) equal in terms of bias and variance?</p>", "a": "<p>$\\text{MSE}(\\hat{\\theta}) = \\text{Var}(\\hat{\\theta}) + \\text{Bias}^2(\\hat{\\theta})$.</p>"}, {"key": "V04:1", "module": "V04", "q": "<p>Why does the Wald confidence interval for a proportion fail near $p=0$ and $p=1$?</p>", "a": "<p>Because it estimates standard error using $\\hat{p}(1-\\hat{p})$; when $k=0$, $\\hat{p}=0$, collapsing the interval to $[0, 0]$ with zero width and zero coverage.</p>"}, {"key": "V04:2", "module": "V04", "q": "<p>How does the Wilson score interval resolve the Wald interval's failure?</p>", "a": "<p>It inverts the score test by using the hypothesized parameter $p_0(1-p_0)$ in the denominator, solving a quadratic equation that yields valid positive width even when $k=0$.</p>"}, {"key": "V04:3", "module": "V04", "q": "<p>What is the exact coverage guarantee of the Clopper-Pearson interval?</p>", "a": "<p>It guarantees that the true coverage probability is strictly greater than or equal to $1 - \\alpha$ for all parameter values, achieved by inverting Binomial tail probabilities using Beta quantiles.</p>"}, {"key": "V04:4", "module": "V04", "q": "<p>Why must clustered data be bootstrapped by resampling clusters rather than individual units?</p>", "a": "<p>Because resampling individual observations assumes independent errors, ignoring intra-cluster correlation and producing artificially narrow, undercovering confidence intervals.</p>"}, {"key": "V04:5", "module": "V04", "q": "<p>What is the difference between a standard deviation and a standard error?</p>", "a": "<p>Standard deviation measures the dispersion of values in a population; standard error measures the dispersion of a sample statistic across repeated samples.</p>"}, {"key": "V07:0", "module": "V07", "q": "<p>Why can raw agreement be high when neither rater is paying attention?</p>", "a": "<p>When one label is very common, two raters who both use it most of the time agree by chance. Chance agreement is the sum over labels of (A's share) \u00d7 (B's share).</p>"}, {"key": "V07:1", "module": "V07", "q": "<p>Write Cohen's kappa.</p>", "a": "<p>$\\kappa = (p_o - p_e)/(1 - p_e)$, with $p_o$ the observed agreement and $p_e = \\sum_i p_{i\\cdot} p_{\\cdot i}$ the chance agreement from the two raters' own marginals.</p>"}, {"key": "V07:2", "module": "V07", "q": "<p>What do kappa = 1, 0 and -1 mean?</p>", "a": "<p>1 is perfect agreement, 0 is agreement exactly at chance level, and negative means systematically worse than chance. It reaches -1 only when $p_e = 1/2$ and $p_o = 0$.</p>"}, {"key": "V07:3", "module": "V07", "q": "<p>Two raters are each 90% accurate. Why is their kappa 0.64 at a 50% base rate but about 0.25 at 5%?</p>", "a": "<p>Their observed agreement ($r^2 + (1-r)^2 = 0.82$) does not depend on prevalence, but chance agreement does. At 5% prevalence both say \"no\" so often that chance already explains most of the agreement.</p>"}, {"key": "V07:4", "module": "V07", "q": "<p>Why must a labeller be validated on a held-out set?</p>", "a": "<p>If you tuned or selected it on the gold set, its gold score is biased upward (the best of many noisy scores). Only items that played no part in building it give an honest estimate.</p>"}, {"key": "V07:5", "module": "V07", "q": "<p>With two raters, what does Fleiss' kappa equal?</p>", "a": "<p>Scott's pi, which uses one pooled set of category shares for both raters, not Cohen's kappa, which uses each rater's own shares.</p>"}, {"key": "V07:6", "module": "V07", "q": "<p>How do you bootstrap a kappa correctly?</p>", "a": "<p>Resample items with replacement, keeping each item's pair of labels together. Recompute kappa each time and take the percentile interval. Resampling the raters separately destroys the pairing and drives kappa to about 0.</p>"}, {"key": "V07:7", "module": "V07", "q": "<p>What is the difference between reliability and validity for a labeller?</p>", "a": "<p>Reliability means it gives the same answer again (self-agreement). Validity means its answers match the truth. A labeller can be perfectly reliable and consistently wrong.</p>"}, {"key": "V05:0", "module": "V05", "q": "<p>What is the formal definition of a p-value?</p>", "a": "<p>The probability, under the assumption that the null hypothesis $H_0$ is true, of observing a test statistic at least as extreme as the observed statistic.</p>"}, {"key": "V05:1", "module": "V05", "q": "<p>What is a Type I error vs a Type II error?</p>", "a": "<p>Type I error is a false positive (rejecting $H_0$ when it is true); Type II error is a false negative (failing to reject $H_0$ when the alternative is true).</p>"}, {"key": "V05:2", "module": "V05", "q": "<p>Why does the Bonferroni correction control the Family-Wise Error Rate (FWER)?</p>", "a": "<p>By Boole's inequality (union bound), $\\mathbb{P}(\\bigcup_i E_i) \\le \\sum_i \\mathbb{P}(E_i) = m (\\alpha/m) = \\alpha$.</p>"}, {"key": "V05:3", "module": "V05", "q": "<p>How does the False Discovery Rate (FDR) differ from the Family-Wise Error Rate (FWER)?</p>", "a": "<p>FWER controls the probability of making even one false positive (stringent); FDR controls the expected proportion of false positives among all declared discoveries (practical for large $m$).</p>"}, {"key": "V05:4", "module": "V05", "q": "<p>Why is a standard t-test incapable of proving that two systems perform identically?</p>", "a": "<p>Because failing to reject the null hypothesis merely indicates insufficient evidence to distinguish them, not proof of zero difference; equivalence testing (TOST) is required.</p>"}, {"key": "V05:5", "module": "V05", "q": "<p>What is 'the garden of forking paths' in data analysis?</p>", "a": "<p>The phenomenon where data-dependent choices of preprocessing, metrics, and subgroup filters inflate false discovery rates even without conscious intent to p-hack.</p>"}, {"key": "V06:0", "module": "V06", "q": "<p>What is statistical power?</p>", "a": "<p>The probability of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis when the alternative hypothesis is true ($1 - \\beta$).</p>"}, {"key": "V06:1", "module": "V06", "q": "<p>What is Cohen's d vs Cohen's h?</p>", "a": "<p>Cohen's d measures standardized mean differences between continuous variables ($d = \\Delta / \\sigma$); Cohen's h measures differences between proportions using the arcsin transformation ($h = 2 \\arcsin\\sqrt{p_1} - 2 \\arcsin\\sqrt{p_2}$).</p>"}, {"key": "V06:2", "module": "V06", "q": "<p>What is the Winner's Curse in scientific experiments?</p>", "a": "<p>The phenomenon where statistically significant results from low-powered studies systematically overestimate the true effect size because only extreme positive noise crosses the significance threshold.</p>"}, {"key": "V06:3", "module": "V06", "q": "<p>What is the Minimum Detectable Effect (MDE)?</p>", "a": "<p>The smallest true effect size that an experiment with sample size $n$ has a specified probability (e.g. 80%) of detecting at significance level $\\alpha$.</p>"}, {"key": "V06:4", "module": "V06", "q": "<p>What does Vargha-Delaney $A_{12} = 0.70$ mean?</p>", "a": "<p>There is a 70% probability that a randomly drawn observation from group 1 will be strictly greater than a randomly drawn observation from group 2 (with ties counting as 0.5).</p>"}, {"key": "V06:5", "module": "V06", "q": "<p>Why does an underpowered experiment waste engineering resources?</p>", "a": "<p>Because it has a high probability of missing real improvements (Type II error) and an extreme probability of exaggerating noise if significance is achieved (Winner's Curse).</p>"}, {"key": "V08:0", "module": "V08", "q": "<p>What is the Back-Door Criterion?</p>", "a": "<p>A graphical criterion in causal DAGs: a set of variables $Z$ blocks all confounding paths between treatment $X$ and outcome $Y$ without containing descendants of $X$.</p>"}, {"key": "V08:1", "module": "V08", "q": "<p>What is a collider in a causal DAG?</p>", "a": "<p>A node where two arrows converge ($A \\to C \\leftarrow B$). Conditioning on a collider opens the path, creating spurious association between $A$ and $B$.</p>"}, {"key": "V08:2", "module": "V08", "q": "<p>State Simpson's Paradox.</p>", "a": "<p>A statistical phenomenon where a trend or correlation observed in several distinct groups reverses when the groups are aggregated.</p>"}, {"key": "V08:3", "module": "V08", "q": "<p>What is Sample Ratio Mismatch (SRM)?</p>", "a": "<p>A statistically significant difference between the intended and observed sample allocation ratio in an A/B test, indicating broken randomisation or attrition bias.</p>"}, {"key": "V08:4", "module": "V08", "q": "<p>What is the core identifying assumption of Difference-in-Differences (DiD)?</p>", "a": "<p>The parallel trends assumption: in the absence of treatment, the average change in the treatment group would have been identical to the change in the control group.</p>"}, {"key": "V08:5", "module": "V08", "q": "<p>What is regression to the mean?</p>", "a": "<p>The statistical tendency for extreme random scores on an initial test to move closer to the true population average upon subsequent re-testing.</p>"}, {"key": "V11:0", "module": "V11", "q": "<p>What is Bayes' Rule for probability densities?</p>", "a": "<p>$p(\\theta \\mid D) = \\frac{p(D \\mid \\theta) p(\\theta)}{p(D)} = \\frac{p(D \\mid \\theta) p(\\theta)}{\\int p(D \\mid \\theta&#x27;) p(\\theta&#x27;) d\\theta&#x27;}$.</p>"}, {"key": "V11:1", "module": "V11", "q": "<p>What is the base-rate fallacy?</p>", "a": "<p>The tendency to judge the probability of a hypothesis based solely on test evidence while ignoring the prior prevalence (base rate) of the hypothesis in the population.</p>"}, {"key": "V11:2", "module": "V11", "q": "<p>What is a conjugate prior?</p>", "a": "<p>A prior distribution family that, when combined with a specific likelihood function, yields a posterior distribution belonging to the exact same algebraic family.</p>"}, {"key": "V11:3", "module": "V11", "q": "<p>What does Laplace's rule of succession state?</p>", "a": "<p>Starting from a flat uniform prior, the expected probability of success on trial $n+1$ after $k$ successes is $\\frac{k + 1}{n + 2}$.</p>"}, {"key": "V11:4", "module": "V11", "q": "<p>How is a Bayesian credible interval interpreted?</p>", "a": "<p>Given the observed data and prior assumptions, there is a $(1-\\alpha)$ probability that the true parameter lies within the interval.</p>"}, {"key": "V11:5", "module": "V11", "q": "<p>What is Expected Loss in a Bayesian A/B test?</p>", "a": "<p>The expected margin by which variant B is worse than variant A, computed as $\\mathbb{E}[\\max(p_A - p_B, 0)]$.</p>"}, {"key": "D07:0", "module": "D07", "q": "<p>What is the AVL tree balance factor invariant and how is it restored?</p>", "a": "<p>$|BF(u)| \\le 1$ where $BF(u) = \text{height}(left) - \text{height}(right)$; restored via single (LL, RR) or double (LR, RL) rotations in $O(1)$ time.</p>"}, {"key": "D07:1", "module": "D07", "q": "<p>Why are B+trees preferred over B-trees for database storage engines?</p>", "a": "<p>B+trees store all record data in leaf nodes and link leaves sequentially, allowing fast contiguous range scans without traversing internal nodes.</p>"}, {"key": "D07:2", "module": "D07", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of searching or inserting a word of length L in a Trie?</p>", "a": "<p>$O(L)$ time, completely independent of the total number of words $N$ stored in the trie.</p>"}, {"key": "D07:3", "module": "D07", "q": "<p>Why does recursive BST validation require range parameters (low, high)?</p>", "a": "<p>Because every node in a left subtree must be smaller than ALL its ancestors, not just its immediate parent.</p>"}, {"key": "D07:4", "module": "D07", "q": "<p>What is a Fenwick Tree (Binary Indexed Tree) and what is its query time?</p>", "a": "<p>A tree-like array structure that supports prefix sum queries and point updates in $O(\\log N)$ time using bitwise least significant bits (<code>i &amp; (-i)</code>).</p>"}, {"key": "D07:5", "module": "D07", "q": "<p>How does tree serialization with pre-order traversal reconstruct unambiguous tree topology?</p>", "a": "<p>By recording explicit null markers (<code>#</code>) for missing children, every internal node is guaranteed to have exactly two child tokens in the stream.</p>"}, {"key": "D08:0", "module": "D08", "q": "<p>What is the array index relationship for parent and children in a binary heap?</p>", "a": "<p>For node $i$: $    ext{parent} = \\lfloor (i-1)/2\nfloor$, $   ext{left} = 2i + 1$, $  ext{right} = 2i + 2$.</p>"}, {"key": "D08:1", "module": "D08", "q": "<p>Why does Floyd's bottom-up heapify take O(N) rather than O(N log N)?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the work per node is proportional to height $h$ above leaves, and the number of nodes decreases exponentially with height, forming the convergent sum $\\sum h/2^h = 2$.</p>"}, {"key": "D08:2", "module": "D08", "q": "<p>How does the dual-heap median tracker guarantee O(1) median lookup?</p>", "a": "<p>By splitting numbers into a max-heap (lower half) and min-heap (upper half) of equal size, the median is always at the top of one or both heaps.</p>"}, {"key": "D08:3", "module": "D08", "q": "<p>How does a sweep-line algorithm determine minimum meeting rooms?</p>", "a": "<p>By decomposing meetings into start ($+1$) and end ($-1$) events, sorting by time, and tracking the maximum running prefix sum of active rooms.</p>"}, {"key": "D08:4", "module": "D08", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of merging K sorted lists of total size N?</p>", "a": "<p>$O(N \\log K)$ by maintaining a min-heap containing the current head element of each of the $K$ lists.</p>"}, {"key": "D08:5", "module": "D08", "q": "<p>Why is decreasing a key in a standard binary heap inefficient?</p>", "a": "<p>Because locating the element in the heap array takes an $O(N)$ linear search unless an auxiliary index mapping <code>id -&gt; heap_pos</code> is maintained.</p>"}, {"key": "D09:0", "module": "D09", "q": "<p>Why does BFS guarantee shortest paths in unweighted graphs?</p>", "a": "<p>Because vertices are discovered in non-decreasing order of distance from source: all vertices at distance $k$ are processed before any at $k+1$.</p>"}, {"key": "D09:1", "module": "D09", "q": "<p>What do the three colors (WHITE, GRAY, BLACK) signify in directed DFS?</p>", "a": "<p>WHITE = unvisited, GRAY = currently on the active recursion call stack, BLACK = fully explored with all descendants finished.</p>"}, {"key": "D09:2", "module": "D09", "q": "<p>What indicates a cycle during directed DFS?</p>", "a": "<p>Encountering an edge leading to a GRAY vertex (a back-edge to an active ancestor on the call stack).</p>"}, {"key": "D09:3", "module": "D09", "q": "<p>What condition proves an undirected graph is bipartite?</p>", "a": "<p>The graph is 2-colorable with zero adjacent nodes sharing the same color, which is mathematically equivalent to having no odd-length cycles.</p>"}, {"key": "D09:4", "module": "D09", "q": "<p>How does Kahn's algorithm detect cycles in a directed graph?</p>", "a": "<p>If the number of vertices dequeued with in-degree 0 is less than total vertices $|V|$, the remaining vertices form a directed cycle.</p>"}, {"key": "D09:5", "module": "D09", "q": "<p>What does <code>low[u]</code> represent in Tarjan's SCC algorithm?</p>", "a": "<p>The lowest discovery time reachable from $u$ through its subtree and at most one back-edge to an active stack ancestor.</p>"}, {"key": "D10:0", "module": "D10", "q": "<p>Why does Dijkstra's algorithm fail on graphs with negative edge weights?</p>", "a": "<p>Because Dijkstra assumes that once a vertex is popped from the priority queue, its distance is final and can never be improved by extending a longer path.</p>"}, {"key": "D10:1", "module": "D10", "q": "<p>How does Bellman-Ford detect negative weight cycles?</p>", "a": "<p>By running a $|V|$-th relaxation pass: if any distance can still be decreased after $|V|-1$ passes, a reachable negative cycle must exist.</p>"}, {"key": "D10:2", "module": "D10", "q": "<p>What is the Cut Property for Minimum Spanning Trees?</p>", "a": "<p>For any partition of vertices into two sets $S$ and $V \\setminus S$, the minimum weight edge crossing the cut must belong to an MST.</p>"}, {"key": "D10:3", "module": "D10", "q": "<p>What two optimizations give Union-Find its inverse Ackermann O(alpha(N)) runtime?</p>", "a": "<p>Path compression (flattens trees during <code>find</code>) and union by rank (attaches shorter trees under taller trees during <code>union</code>).</p>"}, {"key": "D10:4", "module": "D10", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of Kruskal's algorithm?</p>", "a": "<p>$O(|E| \\log |E|)$ dominated by sorting the edges by weight.</p>"}, {"key": "D10:5", "module": "D10", "q": "<p>What does the Floyd-Warshall algorithm compute and in what time complexity?</p>", "a": "<p>All-pairs shortest paths for every pair of vertices in $O(|V|^3)$ time using 3 nested loops.</p>"}, {"key": "D11:0", "module": "D11", "q": "<p>What is the Greedy-Choice Property?</p>", "a": "<p>The property that a globally optimal solution can be arrived at by making locally optimal (greedy) choices without ever backtracking.</p>"}, {"key": "D11:1", "module": "D11", "q": "<p>What are the core steps of an Exchange Argument proof?</p>", "a": "<p>Assume an optimal solution $O$ differs from greedy $G$, exchange the differing element in $O$ with the greedy element, and prove the new solution remains valid and equally optimal.</p>"}, {"key": "D11:2", "module": "D11", "q": "<p>Why does the greedy choice fail on the 0/1 Knapsack problem?</p>", "a": "<p>Because items cannot be fractionally divided to fill remaining capacity, leaving empty slack that lowers total value.</p>"}, {"key": "D11:3", "module": "D11", "q": "<p>What does it mean for a code to be prefix-free in Huffman coding?</p>", "a": "<p>No valid character code word is a prefix of any other code word, enabling unambiguous instant decoding without delimiter symbols.</p>"}, {"key": "D11:4", "module": "D11", "q": "<p>In Interval Scheduling, what attribute should you sort by to maximize activities?</p>", "a": "<p>Earliest finish time (end time), because finishing early leaves maximal remaining time open for subsequent activities.</p>"}, {"key": "D11:5", "module": "D11", "q": "<p>What algebraic structure guarantees that a greedy algorithm produces an optimal solution?</p>", "a": "<p>A Matroid, which satisfies hereditary independent set axioms and the independent set exchange property.</p>"}, {"key": "D12:0", "module": "D12", "q": "<p>What are the two mandatory properties required for Dynamic Programming to apply?</p>", "a": "<p>Optimal Substructure (optimal global solution composed of optimal subproblem solutions) and Overlapping Subproblems (subproblems recur repeatedly).</p>"}, {"key": "D12:1", "module": "D12", "q": "<p>What is the difference between memoization and tabulation?</p>", "a": "<p>Memoization is top-down (recursive, stores results on return); Tabulation is bottom-up (iterative, fills table starting from base cases).</p>"}, {"key": "D12:2", "module": "D12", "q": "<p>Why is 0/1 Knapsack called 'pseudo-polynomial'?</p>", "a": "<p>Because its runtime $O(N \\cdot W)$ is polynomial in the numeric value of $W$, but exponential in the bit-length of the input $b = \\log_2 W$.</p>"}, {"key": "D12:3", "module": "D12", "q": "<p>How does space optimization work for 2D DP when dp[i] only depends on dp[i-1]?</p>", "a": "<p>By keeping only two rows (current and previous) or iterating right-to-left in a single 1D array, reducing space from $O(M \\cdot N)$ to $O(N)$.</p>"}, {"key": "D12:4", "module": "D12", "q": "<p>What are the three transitions in the Levenshtein Edit Distance recurrence?</p>", "a": "<p>Insert <code>dp[i][j-1] + 1</code>, Delete <code>dp[i-1][j] + 1</code>, and Replace <code>dp[i-1][j-1] + cost</code> (where cost is 0 if characters match, 1 otherwise).</p>"}, {"key": "D12:5", "module": "D12", "q": "<p>How do you reconstruct the actual items chosen in 0/1 Knapsack from the DP table?</p>", "a": "<p>Start at $(N, W)$ and backtrack: if $dp[i][w]\ne dp[i-1][w]$, item $i-1$ was selected; deduct its weight from $w$ and step to $i-1$.</p>"}, {"key": "D14:0", "module": "D14", "q": "<p>What is the core idea of Backtracking?</p>", "a": "<p>Exploring an implicit search tree and abandoning (pruning) partial candidate solutions as soon as a constraint is violated.</p>"}, {"key": "D14:1", "module": "D14", "q": "<p>What does Alpha-Beta pruning prune?</p>", "a": "<p>Branches where the current player already has a guaranteed better move, or the opponent would never allow the game state to occur (beta &lt;= alpha).</p>"}, {"key": "D14:2", "module": "D14", "q": "<p>How does Meet-in-the-Middle reduce time complexity?</p>", "a": "<p>Splits a problem of size N into two halves of size N/2, reducing O(2^N) brute-force to O(N * 2^(N/2)).</p>"}, {"key": "D14:3", "module": "D14", "q": "<p>What is the Minimum Remaining Values (MRV) heuristic?</p>", "a": "<p>A variable selection strategy in constraint satisfaction that picks the unassigned variable with the fewest valid options remaining.</p>"}, {"key": "D14:4", "module": "D14", "q": "<p>Why does Iterative Deepening DFS (IDDFS) use little memory?</p>", "a": "<p>It retains the O(d) linear memory consumption of DFS while guaranteeing shortest path discovery like BFS in unweighted trees.</p>"}, {"key": "D14:5", "module": "D14", "q": "<p>What is the condition to prune in Alpha-Beta search?</p>", "a": "<p>Break the branch evaluation as soon as beta &lt;= alpha.</p>"}, {"key": "D18:0", "module": "D18", "q": "<p>What are the 5 phases of a 45-minute technical interview?</p>", "a": "<ol>\n<li>Clarify &amp; edge cases (0-5m), 2. Baseline &amp; optimize (5-15m), 3. Clean coding (15-35m), 4. Dry-run testing (35-42m), 5. Reflection (42-45m).</li>\n</ol>"}, {"key": "D18:1", "module": "D18", "q": "<p>Why should you always state a brute-force approach first?</p>", "a": "<p>It establishes a guaranteed working baseline, proves you understand the problem, and gives a benchmark to optimize against.</p>"}, {"key": "D18:2", "module": "D18", "q": "<p>What Python standard library module provides O(1) FIFO operations?</p>", "a": "<p>collections.deque (provides O(1) append, appendleft, pop, and popleft).</p>"}, {"key": "D18:3", "module": "D18", "q": "<p>What does heapq in Python implement by default?</p>", "a": "<p>A min-heap where the smallest element is always maintained at index 0.</p>"}, {"key": "D18:4", "module": "D18", "q": "<p>How should you respond when an interviewer points out a bug?</p>", "a": "<p>Acknowledge the feedback calmly, trace the execution with an edge case, identify the exact root cause, and apply a clean fix.</p>"}, {"key": "D18:5", "module": "D18", "q": "<p>What is the primary indicator of L5 (Senior) engineering interview performance?</p>", "a": "<p>Proactive ownership: clarifying ambiguity independently, designing robust edge cases, communicating trade-offs, and verifying correctness.</p>"}, {"key": "S16:0", "module": "S16", "q": "<p>What is a Kotlin Platform Type (T!)?</p>", "a": "<p>A type originating from Java code without nullability annotations, which Kotlin treats with relaxed compile-time checks, risking runtime NPEs.</p>"}, {"key": "S16:1", "module": "S16", "q": "<p>What is the difference between apply and let?</p>", "a": "<p>apply passes the context object as this and returns the object itself; let passes it and returns the result of the lambda.</p>"}, {"key": "S16:2", "module": "S16", "q": "<p>Why are reified generics only possible with inline functions?</p>", "a": "<p>Because inlining copies function bytecode directly to the call site where the concrete class type is known, bypassing JVM type erasure.</p>"}, {"key": "S16:3", "module": "S16", "q": "<p>What is the advantage of Kotlin Sequences over Collections?</p>", "a": "<p>Sequences evaluate lazily element-by-element without allocating intermediate lists, optimizing multi-step pipelines with short-circuiting.</p>"}, {"key": "S16:4", "module": "S16", "q": "<p>What does declaration-site covariance (out T) mean?</p>", "a": "<p>The type parameter can only be produced (read-only), allowing a subtype container to be assigned to a supertype container.</p>"}, {"key": "S16:5", "module": "S16", "q": "<p>How does Kotlin achieve compile-time null safety?</p>", "a": "<p>By distinguishing non-nullable types (String) from nullable types (String?), forcing developers to handle nulls via safe calls or Elvis operators.</p>"}, {"key": "L01:0", "module": "L01", "q": "<p>What is Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM)?</p>", "a": "<p>Optimizing model parameters to minimize average loss over the training dataset as a proxy for true distribution risk.</p>"}, {"key": "L01:1", "module": "L01", "q": "<p>What is the bias-variance trade-off?</p>", "a": "<p>Underfitting models suffer from high bias (rigid assumptions); overfitting models suffer from high variance (sensitive to training noise).</p>"}, {"key": "L01:2", "module": "L01", "q": "<p>What is data leakage?</p>", "a": "<p>When information from the test or validation set contaminates model training or preprocessing, producing falsely optimistic evaluations.</p>"}, {"key": "L01:3", "module": "L01", "q": "<p>What does the No Free Lunch theorem state?</p>", "a": "<p>No single machine learning algorithm outperforms all others when averaged across all possible data distributions.</p>"}, {"key": "L01:4", "module": "L01", "q": "<p>Why should numerical missingness be paired with an indicator column?</p>", "a": "<p>Imputing a median fills the missing slot, but adding <code>_is_missing</code> preserves the predictive information that the value was absent.</p>"}, {"key": "L01:5", "module": "L01", "q": "<p>Why is a test set evaluated only once at the conclusion of a project?</p>", "a": "<p>Repeated testing on a test set converts it into a de facto validation set, leaking information into model selection decisions.</p>"}, {"key": "P05:0", "module": "P05", "q": "<p>What is the Iron Law of Processor Performance?</p>", "a": "<p>CPU Time = Instruction Count * CPI * Clock Period.</p>"}, {"key": "P05:1", "module": "P05", "q": "<p>What is AMAT in memory systems?</p>", "a": "<p>Average Memory Access Time = Hit Time + (Miss Rate * Miss Penalty).</p>"}, {"key": "P05:2", "module": "P05", "q": "<p>What are the 4 C's of cache misses?</p>", "a": "<p>Compulsory (cold start), Capacity (cache too small), Conflict (collision in set), Coherence (multicore invalidation).</p>"}, {"key": "P05:3", "module": "P05", "q": "<p>What is the difference between RISC and CISC?</p>", "a": "<p>RISC uses simple, fixed-length load-store instructions optimized for single-cycle pipelining; CISC uses complex, variable-length multi-cycle instructions.</p>"}, {"key": "P05:4", "module": "P05", "q": "<p>What is a load-use data hazard?</p>", "a": "<p>When an instruction immediately reads a register being loaded from memory, requiring a 1-cycle pipeline bubble even with forwarding.</p>"}, {"key": "P05:5", "module": "P05", "q": "<p>How do Spectre and Meltdown exploit speculative execution?</p>", "a": "<p>By executing unauthorized loads past mispredicted branches; microarchitectural cache timing side channels leak the loaded secrets.</p>"}, {"key": "P06:0", "module": "P06", "q": "<p>What is the difference between kernel mode and user mode?</p>", "a": "<p>Kernel mode has unrestricted hardware access; user mode operates with restricted CPU privileges and isolated memory to prevent system crashes.</p>"}, {"key": "P06:1", "module": "P06", "q": "<p>What is the convoy effect in FCFS scheduling?</p>", "a": "<p>Short processes waiting a long time behind a single CPU-intensive process, dragging down average waiting time.</p>"}, {"key": "P06:2", "module": "P06", "q": "<p>Why does SRTF achieve minimal average waiting time?</p>", "a": "<p>By prioritizing tasks with the least remaining work, it minimizes the queue length and total accumulated wait time.</p>"}, {"key": "P06:3", "module": "P06", "q": "<p>How does Linux CFS ensure fairness?</p>", "a": "<p>By tracking virtual runtime (vruntime) scaled by task priority weight, always selecting the task with minimum vruntime.</p>"}, {"key": "P06:4", "module": "P06", "q": "<p>What is the purpose of priority boosting in MLFQ?</p>", "a": "<p>To prevent starvation by periodically moving all processes back to the highest-priority queue.</p>"}, {"key": "P06:5", "module": "P06", "q": "<p>What is the difference between fork() and exec()?</p>", "a": "<p>fork() clones the calling process address space; exec() replaces the address space with a new executable binary program.</p>"}, {"key": "P08:0", "module": "P08", "q": "<p>What is a race condition?</p>", "a": "<p>A bug where program correctness depends unpredictably on the relative scheduling or timing of concurrent execution threads.</p>"}, {"key": "P08:1", "module": "P08", "q": "<p>What are the four Coffman deadlock conditions?</p>", "a": "<p>Mutual Exclusion, Hold and Wait, No Preemption, and Circular Wait; all four must hold simultaneously for a deadlock to exist.</p>"}, {"key": "P08:2", "module": "P08", "q": "<p>How does Dijkstra's Banker's Algorithm avoid deadlocks?</p>", "a": "<p>By simulating resource allocation to verify whether a safe execution sequence exists before granting resource requests.</p>"}, {"key": "P08:3", "module": "P08", "q": "<p>What is false sharing in multicore systems?</p>", "a": "<p>Performance degradation when threads on different cores update independent variables residing on the same 64-byte CPU cache line.</p>"}, {"key": "P08:4", "module": "P08", "q": "<p>How does an SPSC lock-free ring buffer work?</p>", "a": "<p>Uses separate atomic head and tail pointers with power-of-two masking to enqueue and dequeue without mutex contention.</p>"}, {"key": "P08:5", "module": "P08", "q": "<p>What is the happens-before relationship?</p>", "a": "<p>A formal partial ordering defining when memory writes by one thread are guaranteed to be visible to reads by another thread.</p>"}, {"key": "P07:0", "module": "P07", "q": "<p>What is the primary function of the MMU?</p>", "a": "<p>The Memory Management Unit translates virtual addresses to physical RAM addresses using page tables and the TLB.</p>"}, {"key": "P07:1", "module": "P07", "q": "<p>What is B\u00e9l\u00e1dy's anomaly?</p>", "a": "<p>The counter-intuitive phenomenon where increasing the number of page frames results in more page faults under FIFO page replacement.</p>"}, {"key": "P07:2", "module": "P07", "q": "<p>What is a stack algorithm in page replacement?</p>", "a": "<p>An algorithm (like LRU) where the set of pages in memory for N frames is always a subset of pages for N+1 frames, preventing B\u00e9l\u00e1dy's anomaly.</p>"}, {"key": "P07:3", "module": "P07", "q": "<p>How does mmap work?</p>", "a": "<p>It maps file pages directly into a process's virtual address space backed by the kernel page cache, enabling zero-copy I/O.</p>"}, {"key": "P07:4", "module": "P07", "q": "<p>Why is fsync required after write()?</p>", "a": "<p>write() only buffers data in volatile kernel RAM; fsync forces the storage controller to commit dirty buffers to non-volatile flash.</p>"}, {"key": "P07:5", "module": "P07", "q": "<p>How does RAID 5 achieve fault tolerance?</p>", "a": "<p>By striping data across N disks and storing distributed XOR parity, allowing any single failed disk to be mathematically reconstructed.</p>"}, {"key": "P09:0", "module": "P09", "q": "<p>What is the difference between TCP and UDP?</p>", "a": "<p>TCP provides reliable, ordered, connection-oriented byte streams with congestion control; UDP provides lightweight, unordered, datagram-oriented transmission.</p>"}, {"key": "P09:1", "module": "P09", "q": "<p>What is the Bandwidth-Delay Product (BDP)?</p>", "a": "<p>The maximum amount of data in flight required to fully saturate a link: Bandwidth * RTT.</p>"}, {"key": "P09:2", "module": "P09", "q": "<p>What is Additive Increase Multiplicative Decrease (AIMD)?</p>", "a": "<p>The congestion control algorithm where cwnd increases linearly by 1 MSS per RTT and halves multiplicatively upon packet loss.</p>"}, {"key": "P09:3", "module": "P09", "q": "<p>What causes Bufferbloat?</p>", "a": "<p>Overly large packet buffers in routers that fill up and introduce seconds of latency without improving network throughput.</p>"}, {"key": "P09:4", "module": "P09", "q": "<p>How does HTTP/3 differ from HTTP/2?</p>", "a": "<p>HTTP/3 replaces TCP with QUIC over UDP, eliminating transport Head-of-Line blocking and enabling 0-RTT handshakes.</p>"}, {"key": "P09:5", "module": "P09", "q": "<p>What is the role of DNS?</p>", "a": "<p>Translates human-readable domain names into 32-bit IPv4 or 128-bit IPv6 network addresses.</p>"}, {"key": "P10:0", "module": "P10", "q": "<p>What is Kerckhoffs's Principle?</p>", "a": "<p>A cryptographic system should remain secure even if everything about the system except the key is public knowledge.</p>"}, {"key": "P10:1", "module": "P10", "q": "<p>What is the difference between encryption and hashing?</p>", "a": "<p>Encryption is a two-way reversible process using a key; hashing is a one-way irreversible transformation into a fixed-size digest.</p>"}, {"key": "P10:2", "module": "P10", "q": "<p>Why is textbook RSA insecure without padding?</p>", "a": "<p>It is deterministic and multiplicatively homomorphic, enabling ciphertext comparison and signature forgery; OAEP padding is required.</p>"}, {"key": "P10:3", "module": "P10", "q": "<p>How does Diffie-Hellman negotiate a shared secret?</p>", "a": "<p>By exchanging modular powers g^a and g^b mod p; both compute (g^b)^a = (g^a)^b = g^ab mod p without revealing private exponents.</p>"}, {"key": "P10:4", "module": "P10", "q": "<p>What is the danger of the JWT 'none' algorithm attack?</p>", "a": "<p>If an auth server accepts 'none', attackers can tamper with the payload and strip the signature entirely, forging admin tokens.</p>"}, {"key": "P10:5", "module": "P10", "q": "<p>What is a timing attack on string comparison?</p>", "a": "<p>Measuring microsecond differences in comparison latency to deduce secret token bytes one by one based on early loop termination.</p>"}, {"key": "P11:0", "module": "P11", "q": "<p>What did Edgar F. Codd's 1970 paper introduce to computer science?</p>", "a": "<p>The relational model of data, abstracting physical disk representations into mathematical relations governed by first-order predicate logic.</p>"}, {"key": "P11:1", "module": "P11", "q": "<p>What are the fundamental operators of relational algebra?</p>", "a": "<p>Selection (sigma), Projection (pi), Cartesian Product (times), Natural Join (bowtie), Union (cup), Difference (setminus), and Rename (rho).</p>"}, {"key": "P11:2", "module": "P11", "q": "<p>What are the four ACID properties guaranteed by database transactions?</p>", "a": "<p>Atomicity (all-or-nothing), Consistency (integrity constraints preserved), Isolation (serializable execution), and Durability (committed changes survive crashes).</p>"}, {"key": "P11:3", "module": "P11", "q": "<p>What is the primary difference between a B+ Tree and an LSM Tree?</p>", "a": "<p>B+ Trees perform in-place updates optimizing for fast random reads and range queries; LSM Trees write append-only to memory memtables flushed to SSTables, optimizing write throughput.</p>"}, {"key": "P11:4", "module": "P11", "q": "<p>What is the purpose of Two-Phase Locking (2PL)?</p>", "a": "<p>To guarantee conflict serializability by ensuring a transaction cannot acquire any new locks once it releases its first lock (growing phase followed by shrinking phase).</p>"}, {"key": "P11:5", "module": "P11", "q": "<p>What constitutes Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)?</p>", "a": "<p>A schema is in BCNF if for every non-trivial functional dependency X -&gt; Y, X is a superkey of the relation.</p>"}, {"key": "P12:0", "module": "P12", "q": "<p>What is the difference between a DFA and an NFA?</p>", "a": "<p>A DFA has exactly one deterministic transition per state and alphabet symbol; an NFA can have zero, one, or multiple transitions including epsilon transitions.</p>"}, {"key": "P12:1", "module": "P12", "q": "<p>What does the Rabin-Scott subset construction prove?</p>", "a": "<p>That every non-deterministic finite automaton (NFA) has an equivalent deterministic finite automaton (DFA), proving NFAs add no computational power over DFAs.</p>"}, {"key": "P12:2", "module": "P12", "q": "<p>What does the Pumping Lemma for regular languages demonstrate?</p>", "a": "<p>It establishes a necessary condition for any regular language, allowing proof by contradiction that languages with unbounded memory (like 0^n 1^n) are not regular.</p>"}, {"key": "P12:3", "module": "P12", "q": "<p>What is Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS)?</p>", "a": "<p>An algorithmic complexity attack where backtracking regex engines take exponential time O(2^n) on nested repetitions, exhausting CPU resources.</p>"}, {"key": "P12:4", "module": "P12", "q": "<p>How does Thompson's algorithm avoid backtracking in regular expressions?</p>", "a": "<p>It tracks the set of all simultaneously active NFA states in parallel, processing each input character in linear time O(m * n).</p>"}, {"key": "P12:5", "module": "P12", "q": "<p>What is DFA minimization and what does the Myhill-Nerode theorem state?</p>", "a": "<p>DFA minimization merges equivalent states into the unique minimal DFA with the fewest possible states for that language.</p>"}, {"key": "P15:0", "module": "P15", "q": "<p>Why is Pratt parsing preferred over Yacc/Bison for expressions?</p>", "a": "<p>Pratt parsing is intuitive, hand-written, cache-friendly, runs in O(N) time, and generates clean compiler error messages without table conflicts.</p>"}, {"key": "P15:1", "module": "P15", "q": "<p>What are the null denotation (nud) and left denotation (led) in Pratt parsing?</p>", "a": "<p>nud parses tokens at the start of an expression (literals, unary prefix operators); led parses infix/postfix operators taking the left-hand AST as argument.</p>"}, {"key": "P15:2", "module": "P15", "q": "<p>How does a Pratt parser implement right-associative operators like exponentiation?</p>", "a": "<p>By calling the recursive expression parser with a right binding power of lbp - 1, allowing subsequent identical operators to bind first.</p>"}, {"key": "P15:3", "module": "P15", "q": "<p>What does an LL(1) grammar require of its FIRST and FOLLOW sets?</p>", "a": "<p>For any production A -&gt; alpha | beta, FIRST(alpha) and FIRST(beta) must be disjoint; if alpha derives epsilon, FIRST(beta) and FOLLOW(A) must be disjoint.</p>"}, {"key": "P15:4", "module": "P15", "q": "<p>What are Parsing Expression Grammars (PEGs) and why are they unambiguous?</p>", "a": "<p>PEGs use prioritized ordered choice (e1 / e2) that always selects the first matching branch, eliminating non-deterministic ambiguity.</p>"}, {"key": "P15:5", "module": "P15", "q": "<p>What is the infamous JavaScript/C operator precedence gotcha involving bitwise operators?</p>", "a": "<p>Bitwise operators (&amp;, |) bind looser than equality (==), so a &amp; 1 === 0 evaluates as a &amp; (1 === 0).</p>"}, {"key": "P18:0", "module": "P18", "q": "<p>What is referential transparency?</p>", "a": "<p>The property where any expression can be replaced by its evaluated value without changing program behavior, guaranteeing freedom from hidden side effects.</p>"}, {"key": "P18:1", "module": "P18", "q": "<p>What is the difference between a product type and a sum type?</p>", "a": "<p>A product type (struct, tuple) holds all of its fields simultaneously (|A x B| = |A| * |B|), while a sum type (tagged union, enum) holds exactly one of several alternative variants (|A + B| = |A| + |B|).</p>"}, {"key": "P18:2", "module": "P18", "q": "<p>What are the two core operations of a monad?</p>", "a": "<p>Unit (return / pure), which wraps a raw value into the monadic container, and Bind (flatMap / &gt;&gt;=), which chains a function returning a monad while handling intermediate state or error logic.</p>"}, {"key": "P18:3", "module": "P18", "q": "<p>How do persistent data structures make immutability computationally efficient?</p>", "a": "<p>Through structural sharing: creating a new version of a collection reuses unmodified nodes of tree or DAG structures, achieving O(1) or O(log n) update time without defensive full-copying.</p>"}, {"key": "P18:4", "module": "P18", "q": "<p>What is an affine type system and how does Rust use it?</p>", "a": "<p>An affine type system enforces that every resource is used at most once. Rust uses it to track ownership and move semantics, statically guaranteeing memory safety and data-race freedom at compile time without a garbage collector.</p>"}, {"key": "P18:5", "module": "P18", "q": "<p>How does Dart 3 implement algebraic data types and pattern matching?</p>", "a": "<p>Using sealed class hierarchies to define closed sum types, allowing switch statements and expressions to be checked by the compiler for exhaustive coverage without default fallback cases.</p>"}, {"key": "S01:0", "module": "S01", "q": "<p>What is Sound Null Safety in Dart?</p>", "a": "<p>A type system guarantee that non-nullable types cannot evaluate to null at runtime, eliminating NullPointerExceptions at compile time.</p>"}, {"key": "S01:1", "module": "S01", "q": "<p>How does Dart's event loop prioritize tasks?</p>", "a": "<p>Microtasks are always processed first; the microtask queue must be completely empty before the next event from the event queue executes.</p>"}, {"key": "S01:2", "module": "S01", "q": "<p>What is an Isolate in Dart?</p>", "a": "<p>An independent worker thread with its own private heap memory that communicates exclusively via message passing across Ports.</p>"}, {"key": "S01:3", "module": "S01", "q": "<p>Why does Dart use JIT in debug and AOT in release mode?</p>", "a": "<p>JIT provides sub-second stateful hot reload during development; AOT compiles fast, predictable native machine code for production release.</p>"}, {"key": "S01:4", "module": "S01", "q": "<p>What are Dart 3 sealed classes?</p>", "a": "<p>Class hierarchies that cannot be extended outside their defining library, enabling compile-time exhaustive pattern matching in switch statements.</p>"}, {"key": "S01:5", "module": "S01", "q": "<p>What does const canonicalization do?</p>", "a": "<p>Ensures that objects constructed with identical compile-time const expressions share the exact same memory identity in the heap.</p>"}, {"key": "P19:0", "module": "P19", "q": "<p>What is afferent vs efferent coupling and the instability metric?</p>", "a": "<p>Afferent coupling (Ca) measures incoming dependencies, efferent coupling (Ce) measures outgoing dependencies. Instability I = Ce / (Ca + Ce), where 0 is maximally stable and 1 is maximally unstable.</p>"}, {"key": "P19:1", "module": "P19", "q": "<p>What is a Git merge-base?</p>", "a": "<p>The Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) commit between two branch heads in the Git commit Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG), used as the base for three-way merging.</p>"}, {"key": "P19:2", "module": "P19", "q": "<p>What is the Testing Pyramid?</p>", "a": "<p>A software verification model recommending a large base of fast isolated unit tests (~70%), a medium layer of integration tests (~20%), and a small top layer of end-to-end tests (~10%).</p>"}, {"key": "P19:3", "module": "P19", "q": "<p>What is Hyrum's Law?</p>", "a": "<p>The empirical law stating that with enough users of an API, every observable system behavior (intended or not) will be depended upon by someone, making changes risky.</p>"}, {"key": "P19:4", "module": "P19", "q": "<p>What is blast radius in software architecture?</p>", "a": "<p>The set of all upstream components, services, or modules transitively affected when a specific symbol or service is modified or fails.</p>"}, {"key": "P19:5", "module": "P19", "q": "<p>What is the core philosophy of a blameless postmortem?</p>", "a": "<p>Treating human error as a symptom of systemic and architectural failure rather than personal incompetence, focusing on corrective actions and safeguard mechanisms.</p>"}, {"key": "P22:0", "module": "P22", "q": "<p>What does Fitts's law model in user interfaces?</p>", "a": "<p>The time required to move to a target: MT = a + b * log2(1 + D/W). Making buttons larger (W) and closer (D) reduces interaction time.</p>"}, {"key": "P22:1", "module": "P22", "q": "<p>What does Hick's law state regarding menu design?</p>", "a": "<p>Decision time scales logarithmically with the number of choices: T = b * log2(n + 1). Grouping or pruning options reduces cognitive load.</p>"}, {"key": "P22:2", "module": "P22", "q": "<p>What are the WCAG 2.1 Level AA color contrast requirements?</p>", "a": "<p>A minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:0:1 for large text (&gt;= 18pt or bold &gt;= 14pt) and active graphical UI components.</p>"}, {"key": "P22:3", "module": "P22", "q": "<p>Why is the minimum recommended touch target size 48x48dp?</p>", "a": "<p>It matches the physical human fingertip pad (~9mm), preventing high miss rates and motor frustration on touchscreens.</p>"}, {"key": "P22:4", "module": "P22", "q": "<p>What is the System Usability Scale (SUS) and what score is average?</p>", "a": "<p>A 10-item standard usability questionnaire yielding a score from 0 to 100. A score of 68 represents the 50th percentile (average baseline).</p>"}, {"key": "P22:5", "module": "P22", "q": "<p>What are Material Design window size classes?</p>", "a": "<p>Standard responsive breakpoints: Compact (&lt; 600dp, phones), Medium (600-840dp, foldables/tablets), and Expanded (&gt;= 840dp, desktop/large tablets).</p>"}, {"key": "M31:0", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>What is an eigenvector, in one sentence?</p>", "a": "<p>A direction the matrix does not turn, only scales, by the factor called the eigenvalue.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:1", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>When can a matrix be diagonalised?</p>", "a": "<p>When it has a full set of independent eigenvectors. Distinct eigenvalues guarantee this; repeated ones may or may not.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:2", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>State the spectral theorem.</p>", "a": "<p>A real symmetric matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors, so A = Q Lambda Q transpose.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:3", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>What does the SVD say geometrically?</p>", "a": "<p>Every matrix is a rotation, then a scaling along perpendicular axes, then another rotation.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:4", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>What is the relation between singular values and the eigenvalues of A transpose A?</p>", "a": "<p>The singular values are the square roots of those eigenvalues, and the right singular vectors are their eigenvectors.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:5", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>State the Eckart-Young theorem.</p>", "a": "<p>Truncating the SVD to the largest k singular values gives the best rank-k approximation, with spectral error equal to sigma_{k+1}.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:6", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>What is the condition number and what does it tell you?</p>", "a": "<p>The ratio of largest to smallest singular value. It bounds how much a relative error in the data is amplified in the answer.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:7", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>Why avoid forming A transpose A for least squares?</p>", "a": "<p>Because it squares the condition number, losing twice as many digits. Use QR or the SVD instead.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:8", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>When does power iteration fail?</p>", "a": "<p>When there is no strictly dominant eigenvalue, or the starting vector has no component along the dominant eigenvector.</p>"}, {"key": "M31:9", "module": "M31", "q": "<p>Why is the SVD a better rank test than counting pivots?</p>", "a": "<p>Pivot counting needs an exact zero test, which is meaningless in floating point. Singular values degrade gracefully, so you can see how close to rank-deficient a matrix is.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:0", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>Why do two numbers determine infinitely many directional slopes?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the directional derivative is the dot product of the gradient with the unit direction, so one vector fixes them all.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:1", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>In which direction does the gradient point, and what is its magnitude?</p>", "a": "<p>Steepest ascent, with magnitude equal to that steepest slope. Perpendicular to it, the slope is zero.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:2", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>Does having all partial derivatives imply differentiability?</p>", "a": "<p>No. A function can have both partials at a point and not even be continuous there.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:3", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>What is the multivariate chain rule in one line?</p>", "a": "<p>The Jacobian of a composition is the product of the Jacobians, evaluated at the right points.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:4", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>Why is reverse-mode differentiation cheap for a scalar loss?</p>", "a": "<p>Because bracketing the Jacobian product from the output backwards keeps every intermediate a vector, so one pass gives all the partial derivatives.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:5", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>How does the Hessian classify a critical point?</p>", "a": "<p>All eigenvalues positive is a minimum, all negative a maximum, mixed signs a saddle, any zero inconclusive.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:6", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>Why are saddles the dominant critical point in high dimensions?</p>", "a": "<p>A minimum needs every one of n eigenvalues to be positive, which becomes exponentially unlikely as n grows.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:7", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>State the Lagrange condition and its intuition.</p>", "a": "<p>grad f = lambda grad g at the optimum. If the objective's gradient had any component along the constraint surface you could slide that way and improve.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:8", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>What must you multiply by when changing variables in a multiple integral?</p>", "a": "<p>The absolute value of the Jacobian determinant, the local volume scale factor. For polar coordinates it is r.</p>"}, {"key": "M32:9", "module": "M32", "q": "<p>How is the Gaussian integral evaluated, and why?</p>", "a": "<p>Square it to get a double integral, switch to polar coordinates, and the Jacobian factor r makes the integrand elementary. The one-dimensional problem has no elementary antiderivative.</p>"}, {"key": "L02:0", "module": "L02", "q": "<p>What is the normal equations formula for linear regression?</p>", "a": "<p>$\\hat{w} = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T y$, representing the orthogonal projection of target $y$ onto the feature subspace.</p>"}, {"key": "L02:1", "module": "L02", "q": "<p>Why are least squares and logistic regression losses convex?</p>", "a": "<p>Their Hessian matrices are positive semi-definite everywhere, guaranteeing no local minima traps.</p>"}, {"key": "L02:2", "module": "L02", "q": "<p>How does L2 Ridge regularization differ from L1 Lasso?</p>", "a": "<p>Ridge shrinks weights toward zero via Gaussian prior; Lasso produces exact zeros via Laplace prior, performing feature selection.</p>"}, {"key": "L02:3", "module": "L02", "q": "<p>Why is feature scaling essential before gradient descent?</p>", "a": "<p>Features with mismatched variances produce elongated elliptical loss contours, causing gradient descent to oscillate rather than converge.</p>"}, {"key": "L02:4", "module": "L02", "q": "<p>What is the sigmoid function?</p>", "a": "<p>$\\sigma(z) = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-z}}$, which maps any real number to a valid probability in $(0, 1)$.</p>"}, {"key": "L02:5", "module": "L02", "q": "<p>What is the relationship between Maximum Likelihood Estimation and cross-entropy?</p>", "a": "<p>Maximizing log-likelihood under a Bernoulli or Multinomial distribution is mathematically identical to minimizing cross-entropy loss.</p>"}, {"key": "L03:0", "module": "L03", "q": "<p>How does a decision tree select split points?</p>", "a": "<p>It evaluates candidate thresholds across all features to find the split that maximizes Gini impurity or Shannon entropy reduction.</p>"}, {"key": "L03:1", "module": "L03", "q": "<p>Why does bagging reduce variance without increasing bias?</p>", "a": "<p>Averaging decorrelated estimators preserves the individual expectation (bias) while dividing independent variance by the ensemble size.</p>"}, {"key": "L03:2", "module": "L03", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Precision and Recall?</p>", "a": "<p>Precision measures how many positive predictions were correct; Recall measures how many actual positive instances were found.</p>"}, {"key": "L03:3", "module": "L03", "q": "<p>What does ROC-AUC represent probabilistically?</p>", "a": "<p>The probability that a randomly chosen positive instance receives a higher prediction score than a randomly chosen negative instance.</p>"}, {"key": "L03:4", "module": "L03", "q": "<p>How does K-Means assign cluster centroids?</p>", "a": "<p>Lloyd's algorithm alternates between assigning points to the nearest Euclidean centroid and updating centroids to the mean of assigned points.</p>"}, {"key": "L03:5", "module": "L03", "q": "<p>What is permutation feature importance?</p>", "a": "<p>Measuring the drop in model validation performance when values of a specific feature column are randomly shuffled.</p>"}, {"key": "L04:0", "module": "L04", "q": "<p>What is automatic differentiation?</p>", "a": "<p>A family of techniques that evaluates exact mathematical derivatives of computer programs by applying the chain rule to primitive operations.</p>"}, {"key": "L04:1", "module": "L04", "q": "<p>Why does deep learning use reverse mode instead of forward mode?</p>", "a": "<p>Because neural networks map millions of inputs to a single scalar loss; reverse mode computes all gradients in a single backward pass.</p>"}, {"key": "L04:2", "module": "L04", "q": "<p>What are Dual numbers in forward-mode autodiff?</p>", "a": "<p>Numbers of the form $u + \\dot{u}\\epsilon$ where $\\epsilon^2 = 0$, propagating function values and derivatives simultaneously.</p>"}, {"key": "L04:3", "module": "L04", "q": "<p>What is gradient checking?</p>", "a": "<p>Verifying analytical autodiff gradients by comparing them against two-sided numerical central difference approximations.</p>"}, {"key": "L04:4", "module": "L04", "q": "<p>Why must gradients be accumulated with <code>+=</code> during backpropagation?</p>", "a": "<p>When a node's output is consumed by multiple children in a graph, its total derivative is the sum of partial derivatives from all paths.</p>"}, {"key": "L04:5", "module": "L04", "q": "<p>What is expression swell in symbolic differentiation?</p>", "a": "<p>The exponential growth of symbolic algebraic expressions when differentiating nested composite functions without subexpression sharing.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:0", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>State the completeness axiom and name one thing it fails for.</p>", "a": "<p>Every non-empty set of reals bounded above has a least upper bound in the reals. The rationals fail it.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:1", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>Give the epsilon-N definition of convergence, and say why the quantifier order matters.</p>", "a": "<p>For every epsilon there exists N such that beyond N the terms are within epsilon. N may depend on epsilon; demanding one N for all cases is a different, stronger statement.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:2", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>What is a Cauchy sequence, and what does it have to do with completeness?</p>", "a": "<p>One whose terms eventually bunch within any tolerance, with no reference to a limit. In the reals, Cauchy and convergent are equivalent; that equivalence <em>is</em> completeness.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:3", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>Do vanishing terms guarantee a convergent series?</p>", "a": "<p>No. The harmonic series has terms tending to zero and diverges.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:4", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>For which p does the sum of 1/n^p converge?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly for p greater than 1. At p = 1 it is the harmonic series.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:5", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>What is the difference between continuity and uniform continuity?</p>", "a": "<p>For continuity delta may depend on the point; for uniform continuity one delta must work everywhere. Heine-Cantor says they coincide on a closed bounded interval.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:6", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>Why is x^n on [0,1] not uniformly convergent?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the trouble spot moves: at x = (1/2)^(1/n) the value is exactly 1/2 for every n, so the supremum distance never shrinks.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:7", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>What does Riemann's rearrangement theorem say?</p>", "a": "<p>A conditionally convergent series can be rearranged to converge to any real number, or to diverge. Only absolute convergence makes the sum order-independent.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:8", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>What does the Lagrange remainder add to a Taylor polynomial?</p>", "a": "<p>A bound on the error, in terms of a higher derivative at some intermediate point. It turns an approximation into a guarantee.</p>"}, {"key": "M33:9", "module": "M33", "q": "<p>Distinguish O, Theta, little-o and the tilde.</p>", "a": "<p>O is an upper bound, Theta a tight bound, little-o a strict upper bound (ratio to zero), and the tilde asymptotic equality (ratio to one).</p>"}, {"key": "M35:0", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>Why is 0.1 not exactly representable?</p>", "a": "<p>A binary fraction terminates only when the denominator is a power of two. One tenth has a factor of 5, so it recurs in binary just as a third recurs in decimal.</p>"}, {"key": "M35:1", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>What is machine epsilon, and how does it differ from an ulp?</p>", "a": "<p>Epsilon is the gap between 1.0 and the next double, 2^-52. An ulp is the LOCAL gap, which scales with magnitude: near 2^k it is 2^(k-52).</p>"}, {"key": "M35:2", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>What is catastrophic cancellation?</p>", "a": "<p>Subtracting two nearly equal numbers. The agreeing leading digits annihilate and the pre-existing error is promoted to the front. The subtraction itself is usually exact.</p>"}, {"key": "M35:3", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>Give three stable replacements for cancelling expressions.</p>", "a": "<p>1 - cos x becomes 2 sin^2(x/2); e^x - 1 becomes expm1; log(1+x) becomes log1p. Also the conjugate trick for sqrt(x+1) - sqrt(x).</p>"}, {"key": "M35:4", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>What is the difference between conditioning and stability?</p>", "a": "<p>Conditioning is a property of the PROBLEM \u2014 how much it amplifies input error. Stability is a property of the ALGORITHM \u2014 whether it adds error the problem did not require.</p>"}, {"key": "M35:5", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>Why does Kahan summation work?</p>", "a": "<p><code>(t - s) - y</code> is computed exactly by Sterbenz's lemma, so it recovers the rounding error, which is then fed back into the next addend. The error bound becomes independent of n.</p>"}, {"key": "M35:6", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>Give the convergence orders of bisection, Newton and secant.</p>", "a": "<p>Bisection 1 (halves each step, ~50 for full precision, always converges); Newton 2 (digits double, no guarantee); secant the golden ratio, about 1.618.</p>"}, {"key": "M35:7", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>When does Newton's method lose its quadratic order?</p>", "a": "<p>At a multiple root, where f'(r) = 0. Convergence degrades to linear.</p>"}, {"key": "M35:8", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>What is Runge's phenomenon?</p>", "a": "<p>Interpolating 1/(1+25x^2) at equispaced nodes on [-1,1] DIVERGES near the endpoints as the degree grows. Chebyshev nodes, which cluster at the ends, converge.</p>"}, {"key": "M35:9", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>Why does money use decimals rather than floats?</p>", "a": "<p>Money is defined in decimal units and must be exact. Binary floats cannot represent 0.01, so the error accumulates across many transactions into a real discrepancy.</p>"}, {"key": "M35:10", "module": "M35", "q": "<p>How should floats be compared?</p>", "a": "<p>With a mixed tolerance, <code>abs(a-b) &lt;= atol + rtol*abs(b)</code>. A purely relative test fails near zero; a purely absolute one fails at large magnitudes.</p>"}, {"key": "V14:0", "module": "V14", "q": "<p>What is the difference between code coverage and test adequacy?</p>", "a": "<p>Code coverage measures what source lines were executed; test adequacy (such as mutation score) measures whether tests verify behavior and detect faults.</p>"}, {"key": "V14:1", "module": "V14", "q": "<p>What is a test smell?</p>", "a": "<p>A structural pattern in test code (like missing assertions, swallowed exceptions, or tautologies) indicating poor verification quality.</p>"}, {"key": "V14:2", "module": "V14", "q": "<p>What is MC/DC coverage?</p>", "a": "<p>Modified Condition/Decision Coverage: each boolean condition is shown to independently affect the decision outcome while other conditions stay constant.</p>"}, {"key": "V14:3", "module": "V14", "q": "<p>What does the Coupling Effect hypothesis state?</p>", "a": "<p>Complex software faults are coupled to simple syntactic faults; tests that kill simple first-order mutants catch complex multi-line bugs.</p>"}, {"key": "V14:4", "module": "V14", "q": "<p>What is an equivalent mutant?</p>", "a": "<p>A mutated program variant that is semantically identical to the original program, meaning no test input can ever distinguish them.</p>"}, {"key": "V14:5", "module": "V14", "q": "<p>What causes test flakiness?</p>", "a": "<p>Non-determinism, race conditions, unseeded random generators, time-dependent logic, and shared mutable state across test executions.</p>"}, {"key": "V17:0", "module": "V17", "q": "<p>What is the evidence hierarchy in software engineering?</p>", "a": "<p>Direct execution and observation &gt; test suite runner output &gt; static source code reading &gt; unverified assertions.</p>"}, {"key": "V17:1", "module": "V17", "q": "<p>Why is <code>set -o pipefail</code> critical in CI scripts?</p>", "a": "<p>It prevents succeeding pipe commands from masking the non-zero exit codes of upstream crashed processes.</p>"}, {"key": "V17:2", "module": "V17", "q": "<p>What is the difference between a SUPPORTED and an UNDETERMINED claim?</p>", "a": "<p>SUPPORTED has direct empirical execution evidence matching within tolerance; UNDETERMINED lacks execution or test verification.</p>"}, {"key": "V17:3", "module": "V17", "q": "<p>What does Brier score measure?</p>", "a": "<p>The mean squared error between subjective confidence probabilities and actual binary outcomes, quantifying calibration.</p>"}, {"key": "V17:4", "module": "V17", "q": "<p>What is the Straw Man fallacy in technical reviews?</p>", "a": "<p>Refuting an exaggerated, distorted caricature of an alternative proposal rather than addressing its actual merits.</p>"}, {"key": "V17:5", "module": "V17", "q": "<p>What is a reproducibility package?</p>", "a": "<p>A complete bundle containing source code, pinned dependency manifests, dataset seeds, and execution scripts enabling 1-click replication.</p>"}, {"key": "V13:0", "module": "V13", "q": "<p>What is property-based testing?</p>", "a": "<p>A testing methodology where universal algebraic invariants are checked across hundreds of automatically generated pseudo-random inputs.</p>"}, {"key": "V13:1", "module": "V13", "q": "<p>What is shrinking in QuickCheck?</p>", "a": "<p>The automated process of iteratively simplifying a failing counterexample to find the minimal input that still reproduces the failure.</p>"}, {"key": "V13:2", "module": "V13", "q": "<p>What is a round-trip property?</p>", "a": "<p>An invariant stating that encoding followed by decoding returns the original value: $\\text{decode}(\\text{encode}(x)) = x$.</p>"}, {"key": "V13:3", "module": "V13", "q": "<p>What is coverage-guided fuzzing?</p>", "a": "<p>A dynamic testing technique that instruments program branches and prioritizes mutant inputs that discover new control-flow edges.</p>"}, {"key": "V13:4", "module": "V13", "q": "<p>What is differential fuzzing?</p>", "a": "<p>Providing identical fuzzed inputs to two independent implementations and flagging any divergence in their output behavior.</p>"}, {"key": "V13:5", "module": "V13", "q": "<p>Why are random seeds critical in fuzzing?</p>", "a": "<p>They ensure that a random test run can be reproduced bit-for-bit in debugging and continuous integration.</p>"}, {"key": "V09:0", "module": "V09", "q": "<p>What is the primary danger of optional stopping (peeking) in A/B testing?</p>", "a": "<p>Continuous data monitoring inflates Type I error because random walk noise eventually crosses the nominal critical boundary even under a true null.</p>"}, {"key": "V09:1", "module": "V09", "q": "<p>What does CUPED stand for and what does it achieve?</p>", "a": "<p>Controlled-experiment Using Pre-Experiment Data; it uses pre-experiment covariates to reduce metric variance by factor $1 - \\rho^2$, shrinking required sample size.</p>"}, {"key": "V09:2", "module": "V09", "q": "<p>What is an interaction effect in a factorial design?</p>", "a": "<p>When the effect of one independent variable on the outcome depends upon the specific level of another independent variable.</p>"}, {"key": "V09:3", "module": "V09", "q": "<p>What is the difference between internal validity and external validity?</p>", "a": "<p>Internal validity asks whether the experimental treatment genuinely caused the outcome within the study; external validity asks whether the conclusion holds in other populations and environments.</p>"}, {"key": "V09:4", "module": "V09", "q": "<p>Why is permuted block randomisation preferred over pure coin-flip assignment?</p>", "a": "<p>It ensures exact balance in sample sizes between treatment arms across time and within prognostic strata, preventing temporal confounding.</p>"}, {"key": "V09:5", "module": "V09", "q": "<p>How does a Pocock boundary control the error rate across multiple interim looks?</p>", "a": "<p>It raises the critical significance threshold (e.g. from 1.96 to 2.36) so that the cumulative probability of a false rejection across all planned looks remains exactly $\\alpha$.</p>"}, {"key": "V10:0", "module": "V10", "q": "<p>What is Coordinated Omission in latency benchmarking?</p>", "a": "<p>A measurement flaw where a slow response stalls a closed-loop load generator, preventing subsequent requests from entering the system and hiding queuing delay from latency percentiles.</p>"}, {"key": "V10:1", "module": "V10", "q": "<p>Why must speedup ratios be summarized with the geometric mean rather than the arithmetic mean?</p>", "a": "<p>The geometric mean is inversion consistent ($G(A/B) = 1 / G(B/A)$), preventing arbitrary re-normalization tricks from making two identical systems look mutually superior.</p>"}, {"key": "V10:2", "module": "V10", "q": "<p>What is the difference between latency and throughput?</p>", "a": "<p>Latency is the duration required to complete a single unit of work; throughput is the rate at which units of work are completed over time.</p>"}, {"key": "V10:3", "module": "V10", "q": "<p>Why are interleaved paired runs necessary when comparing two software versions?</p>", "a": "<p>They cancel out time-dependent environmental drift such as thermal CPU throttling, background OS tasks, and cache state pollution.</p>"}, {"key": "V10:4", "module": "V10", "q": "<p>What is dead-code elimination in microbenchmarks?</p>", "a": "<p>An optimization where the compiler notices a calculated result is never consumed externally and deletes the entire benchmarked loop, measuring zero nanoseconds.</p>"}, {"key": "V10:5", "module": "V10", "q": "<p>Why are p95 and p99 percentiles preferred over mean and standard deviation for latency?</p>", "a": "<p>Latency distributions are heavily skewed and multi-modal; standard deviation assumes normal symmetry and obscures catastrophic tail latency spikes.</p>"}, {"key": "V15:0", "module": "V15", "q": "<p>What is a Hoare triple?</p>", "a": "<p>A formal specification $\\{P\\}\\; C \\;\\{Q\\}$ asserting that if precondition $P$ holds, executing $C$ leads to postcondition $Q$.</p>"}, {"key": "V15:1", "module": "V15", "q": "<p>What is the difference between partial and total correctness?</p>", "a": "<p>Partial correctness proves correctness if execution terminates; total correctness additionally proves that execution terminates.</p>"}, {"key": "V15:2", "module": "V15", "q": "<p>What is a loop invariant?</p>", "a": "<p>A logical assertion that holds before loop entry and is preserved after every iteration of the loop body.</p>"}, {"key": "V15:3", "module": "V15", "q": "<p>What is a loop variant?</p>", "a": "<p>An integer expression that strictly decreases with every loop iteration and is bounded below, proving termination.</p>"}, {"key": "V15:4", "module": "V15", "q": "<p>What is the assignment axiom in Hoare logic?</p>", "a": "<p>$\\{Q[e/x]\\}\\; x := e \\;\\{Q\\}$: the precondition is formed by substituting $e$ for $x$ in postcondition $Q$.</p>"}, {"key": "V15:5", "module": "V15", "q": "<p>What is Weakest Precondition (wp)?</p>", "a": "<p>The most general predicate $P$ such that executing command $C$ is guaranteed to terminate in a state satisfying postcondition $Q$.</p>"}, {"key": "D13:0", "module": "D13", "q": "<p>What defines the order of evaluation in Interval DP?</p>", "a": "<p>Intervals must be evaluated in increasing order of length L = j - i + 1 so smaller subproblems are memoized before larger spans.</p>"}, {"key": "D13:1", "module": "D13", "q": "<p>What does a bitmask state represent in Held-Karp TSP?</p>", "a": "<p>An integer whose set bits indicate the subset of visited vertices, paired with the index of the currently active vertex.</p>"}, {"key": "D13:2", "module": "D13", "q": "<p>Why does bursting balloons require thinking backwards?</p>", "a": "<p>Identifying the last balloon popped ensures that the subproblems to its left and right do not interact, preserving optimal substructure.</p>"}, {"key": "D13:3", "module": "D13", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of Held-Karp TSP with N vertices?</p>", "a": "<p>O(2^N * N^2) time and O(2^N * N) space, compared to O(N!) brute force.</p>"}, {"key": "D13:4", "module": "D13", "q": "<p>How does Tree DP propagate state values?</p>", "a": "<p>Via post-order traversal (DFS), computing leaf states first and aggregating child states into parent decisions.</p>"}, {"key": "D13:5", "module": "D13", "q": "<p>What is Digit DP primarily used for?</p>", "a": "<p>Counting integers in a range [A, B] satisfying digit-level constraints using state (index, tight_bound, sum/mask).</p>"}, {"key": "D15:0", "module": "D15", "q": "<p>What does the KMP prefix function pi[i] store?</p>", "a": "<p>The length of the longest proper prefix of pattern[0..i] that is also a suffix of pattern[0..i].</p>"}, {"key": "D15:1", "module": "D15", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of KMP search?</p>", "a": "<p>O(N + M) worst-case time, where N is text length and M is pattern length.</p>"}, {"key": "D15:2", "module": "D15", "q": "<p>What is the Z-array definition?</p>", "a": "<p>Z[i] is the length of the longest substring starting at s[i] that matches a prefix of string s.</p>"}, {"key": "D15:3", "module": "D15", "q": "<p>How does Rabin-Karp achieve O(1) rolling hash updates?</p>", "a": "<p>Subtracts the outgoing character's high-order value, multiplies by base, and adds the incoming character modulo prime.</p>"}, {"key": "D15:4", "module": "D15", "q": "<p>How many expansion centers exist for palindromes in a string of length N?</p>", "a": "<p>2N - 1 centers (N single characters and N - 1 adjacent character pairs).</p>"}, {"key": "D15:5", "module": "D15", "q": "<p>What is the Myers diff algorithm based on?</p>", "a": "<p>Finding a shortest path in an edit graph from (0,0) to (N,M) using breadth-first greedy search.</p>"}, {"key": "D16:0", "module": "D16", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Las Vegas and Monte Carlo algorithms?</p>", "a": "<p>Las Vegas algorithms always return the correct answer with variable runtime; Monte Carlo algorithms run in fixed time with a small probability of error.</p>"}, {"key": "D16:1", "module": "D16", "q": "<p>Why does the Fisher-Yates shuffle produce an unbiased permutation?</p>", "a": "<p>It swaps each element i with an index chosen uniformly from [i, N-1], yielding exactly N! equally probable execution trees.</p>"}, {"key": "D16:2", "module": "D16", "q": "<p>What does Reservoir Sampling guarantee?</p>", "a": "<p>Every item in a stream of unknown length N has an exact equal probability k / N of being included in the final sample.</p>"}, {"key": "D16:3", "module": "D16", "q": "<p>Why can Count-Min Sketch never underestimate a frequency?</p>", "a": "<p>Counters are only incremented (+1), never decremented; hash collisions only add to the count, so min(counters) &gt;= true_count.</p>"}, {"key": "D16:4", "module": "D16", "q": "<p>What is the collision probability in MinHash equal to?</p>", "a": "<p>The exact Jaccard similarity of the two sets: Pr[min(h(A)) == min(h(B))] = |A \u2229 B| / |A \u222a B|.</p>"}, {"key": "D16:5", "module": "D16", "q": "<p>What memory does HyperLogLog require to estimate cardinalities into billions?</p>", "a": "<p>Approximately 1.5 kilobytes, achieving standard error around 1.04 / sqrt(m).</p>"}, {"key": "D17:0", "module": "D17", "q": "<p>What does the Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorem state?</p>", "a": "<p>The maximum amount of flow from source to sink equals the minimum total capacity of edges that, if removed, disconnect source from sink.</p>"}, {"key": "D17:1", "module": "D17", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of the Edmonds-Karp algorithm?</p>", "a": "<p>O(V * E^2), using BFS to always choose the shortest augmenting path.</p>"}, {"key": "D17:2", "module": "D17", "q": "<p>How is a min-cut partition (S, T) identified after max-flow finishes?</p>", "a": "<p>S is the set of vertices reachable from the source s in the residual graph; T is all other vertices.</p>"}, {"key": "D17:3", "module": "D17", "q": "<p>How is bipartite matching reduced to a max-flow problem?</p>", "a": "<p>Add super-source s to left nodes (cap 1), left to right nodes (cap 1), right nodes to super-sink t (cap 1).</p>"}, {"key": "D17:4", "module": "D17", "q": "<p>What does Hall's Marriage Theorem state?</p>", "a": "<p>A bipartite graph G = (L \u222a R, E) has a matching covering L iff for every subset W \u2286 L, |N(W)| &gt;= |W|.</p>"}, {"key": "D17:5", "module": "D17", "q": "<p>How does the Project Selection problem model infinite capacity edges?</p>", "a": "<p>Dependencies have infinite capacity from project to equipment, preventing the min-cut from severing the dependency.</p>"}, {"key": "D21:0", "module": "D21", "q": "<p>What does the STAR framework stand for?</p>", "a": "<p>Situation, Task, Action, Result: the structured narrative model for senior engineering leadership interviews.</p>"}, {"key": "D21:1", "module": "D21", "q": "<p>What did Google's Project Aristotle discover?</p>", "a": "<p>Psychological safety is the single most critical determinant of high-performing engineering teams.</p>"}, {"key": "D21:2", "module": "D21", "q": "<p>What is the SBI feedback framework?</p>", "a": "<p>Situation-Behavior-Impact: an objective model for delivering constructive, behavior-anchored technical feedback.</p>"}, {"key": "D21:3", "module": "D21", "q": "<p>What formula defines RICE prioritization?</p>", "a": "<p>RICE = (Reach * Impact * Confidence) / Effort, optionally adjusted by risk penalties.</p>"}, {"key": "D21:4", "module": "D21", "q": "<p>What is the primary principle of a blameless post-mortem?</p>", "a": "<p>Retrospective incident analysis focusing on systemic vulnerabilities, automation, and safeguards rather than individual human fault.</p>"}, {"key": "D21:5", "module": "D21", "q": "<p>What does 'Influence without authority' mean for an L5 engineer?</p>", "a": "<p>Guiding cross-functional engineering consensus through empirical data, prototypes, and trade-off matrices rather than positional authority.</p>"}, {"key": "P16:0", "module": "P16", "q": "<p>What is the difference between small-step and big-step operational semantics?</p>", "a": "<p>Small-step semantics formalizes computation step-by-step as transition relations; big-step semantics relates expressions directly to their final evaluated values.</p>"}, {"key": "P16:1", "module": "P16", "q": "<p>What is a closure in programming language semantics?</p>", "a": "<p>A first-class function paired with its enclosing lexical environment frame, preserving access to captured variables across function lifetimes.</p>"}, {"key": "P16:2", "module": "P16", "q": "<p>What are the falsy values in ECMAScript (JavaScript)?</p>", "a": "<p>false, 0, -0, 0n, empty string '', null, undefined, and NaN. All other values (including [] and {}) are truthy.</p>"}, {"key": "P16:3", "module": "P16", "q": "<p>Why does [] == ![] evaluate to true in JavaScript?</p>", "a": "<p>![] evaluates to false; [] coerses via ToPrimitive to '' which coerses via ToNumber to 0; false coerses to 0; and 0 == 0 is true.</p>"}, {"key": "P16:4", "module": "P16", "q": "<p>What is beta-reduction in the lambda calculus?</p>", "a": "<p>The fundamental rule of function application replacing formal parameters with argument expressions: (\\x. e) v -&gt; e[x := v].</p>"}, {"key": "P16:5", "module": "P16", "q": "<p>What is differential testing of an interpreter?</p>", "a": "<p>Running identical randomized or suite inputs through a new interpreter and a trusted reference implementation to detect discrepancies.</p>"}, {"key": "S08:0", "module": "S08", "q": "<p>What is SQLite WAL mode?</p>", "a": "<p>Write-Ahead Logging mode where writes append to a separate WAL log, allowing concurrent readers to read without being blocked by active writers.</p>"}, {"key": "S08:1", "module": "S08", "q": "<p>Why use atomic file writes via rename?</p>", "a": "<p>POSIX atomic rename guarantees that if a crash occurs mid-write, the original file remains uncorrupted on disk.</p>"}, {"key": "S08:2", "module": "S08", "q": "<p>What is the Outbox Pattern in offline mobile apps?</p>", "a": "<p>Locally persisting pending mutations in an Outbox database table inside the same transaction as entity updates, ensuring reliable sync upon reconnection.</p>"}, {"key": "S08:3", "module": "S08", "q": "<p>How does optimistic concurrency detect sync conflicts?</p>", "a": "<p>By checking if the server's current entity version or ETag matches the client's base version before applying an update.</p>"}, {"key": "S08:4", "module": "S08", "q": "<p>What is Android Scoped Storage?</p>", "a": "<p>A privacy model introduced in Android 10 that isolates app-specific files and requires MediaStore or SAF for shared media access.</p>"}, {"key": "S08:5", "module": "S08", "q": "<p>Why is transaction batching essential for SQLite write performance?</p>", "a": "<p>Individual inserts trigger costly physical flash storage syncs; wrapping them in a transaction amortizes the sync cost into a single disk flush.</p>"}, {"key": "S09:0", "module": "S09", "q": "<p>What are the three essential mobile HTTP timeouts?</p>", "a": "<p>Connect timeout (socket/TLS setup), Read timeout (gap between incoming packets), and Write timeout (upload packet transmission).</p>"}, {"key": "S09:1", "module": "S09", "q": "<p>Why add random jitter to exponential backoff?</p>", "a": "<p>To prevent synchronized client retries from overwhelming recovering servers with a thundering herd spike.</p>"}, {"key": "S09:2", "module": "S09", "q": "<p>What is a Circuit Breaker in mobile networking?</p>", "a": "<p>A client-side state machine that fast-fails requests when server errors exceed a threshold, preventing battery drain and radio waste.</p>"}, {"key": "S09:3", "module": "S09", "q": "<p>How does HTTP ETag conditional revalidation work?</p>", "a": "<p>Client sends If-None-Match with cached ETag; server returns 304 Not Modified without payload body if unchanged, saving bandwidth.</p>"}, {"key": "S09:4", "module": "S09", "q": "<p>What is the single-flight pattern for token refresh?</p>", "a": "<p>Ensuring that when multiple concurrent requests encounter 401 Unauthorized, only one refresh call is dispatched while other requests await the new token.</p>"}, {"key": "S09:5", "module": "S09", "q": "<p>What is debouncing in mobile search inputs?</p>", "a": "<p>Delaying network transmission until the user has stopped typing for a defined duration (e.g. 300ms), cancelling obsolete in-flight requests.</p>"}, {"key": "P13:0", "module": "P13", "q": "<p>What is the Chomsky hierarchy of formal grammars?</p>", "a": "<p>A four-level hierarchy: Type 3 (Regular, finite automata), Type 2 (Context-Free, pushdown automata), Type 1 (Context-Sensitive, LBA), and Type 0 (Unrestricted, Turing machines).</p>"}, {"key": "P13:1", "module": "P13", "q": "<p>What is the Church-Turing thesis?</p>", "a": "<p>The conjecture that any function that can be computed by an effective algorithmic procedure can be computed by a Turing machine.</p>"}, {"key": "P13:2", "module": "P13", "q": "<p>What is the difference between decidable and Turing-recognizable languages?</p>", "a": "<p>Decidable languages have a Turing machine that halts on every input (accept or reject); recognizable languages may run forever on strings not in the language.</p>"}, {"key": "P13:3", "module": "P13", "q": "<p>How does Alan Turing prove that the Halting Problem is undecidable?</p>", "a": "<p>Via Cantor's diagonal argument, by constructing a program D that halts if and only if a hypothetical halting solver predicts that D loops.</p>"}, {"key": "P13:4", "module": "P13", "q": "<p>What does Rice's Theorem state?</p>", "a": "<p>Any non-trivial semantic property of the language recognized by a Turing machine is undecidable.</p>"}, {"key": "P13:5", "module": "P13", "q": "<p>Why is perfect static analysis impossible?</p>", "a": "<p>By Rice's Theorem, no automated tool can decide semantic runtime properties (like null pointer dereferences or terminations) without false positives or false negatives.</p>"}, {"key": "P14:0", "module": "P14", "q": "<p>What is the formal definition of class P and class NP?</p>", "a": "<p>P is the class of decision problems solvable in deterministic polynomial time; NP is the class of decision problems verifiable in deterministic polynomial time given a polynomial-size certificate.</p>"}, {"key": "P14:1", "module": "P14", "q": "<p>What is the Cook-Levin Theorem?</p>", "a": "<p>The fundamental theorem of complexity theory proving that the Boolean Satisfiability problem (SAT) is NP-complete by encoding Turing machine execution into CNF clauses.</p>"}, {"key": "P14:2", "module": "P14", "q": "<p>What is the difference between NP-complete and NP-hard?</p>", "a": "<p>NP-complete problems must belong to NP and be NP-hard; NP-hard problems are at least as hard as any problem in NP, but do not necessarily reside in NP (e.g. Halting Problem).</p>"}, {"key": "P14:3", "module": "P14", "q": "<p>What does Karp's 1972 paper demonstrate?</p>", "a": "<p>It proved that 21 fundamental problems across graph theory, integer programming, and combinatorics (3-SAT, Clique, Vertex Cover, TSP) are all NP-complete via polynomial reductions.</p>"}, {"key": "P14:4", "module": "P14", "q": "<p>How is 2-SAT solvable in linear time while 3-SAT is NP-complete?</p>", "a": "<p>2-SAT can be formulated as an implication graph where satisfiability corresponds to whether any variable x shares a strongly connected component with ~x, solvable in O(V+E) time.</p>"}, {"key": "P14:5", "module": "P14", "q": "<p>What is Savitch's Theorem in space complexity?</p>", "a": "<p>Savitch's Theorem states that NPSPACE(f(n)) is contained in PSPACE(f(n)^2), proving that non-determinism does not add exponential power in space complexity, and PSPACE = NPSPACE.</p>"}, {"key": "P17:0", "module": "P17", "q": "<p>What is Static Single Assignment (SSA) form?</p>", "a": "<p>An intermediate representation where every variable is assigned exactly once, using phi-functions at merge points to simplify dataflow analysis.</p>"}, {"key": "P17:1", "module": "P17", "q": "<p>What is the difference between AOT and JIT compilation?</p>", "a": "<p>AOT compiles to native machine code prior to execution for zero warmup and predictable latency; JIT compiles dynamically at runtime using profiling feedback.</p>"}, {"key": "P17:2", "module": "P17", "q": "<p>What is the Weak Generational Hypothesis?</p>", "a": "<p>The empirical observation in computer systems that most allocated objects have very short lifespans and die shortly after creation.</p>"}, {"key": "P17:3", "module": "P17", "q": "<p>How does a generational garbage collector use write barriers?</p>", "a": "<p>A write barrier intercepts pointer writes from old-generation objects to young-generation objects, recording them in a remembered set so minor collections don't scan the entire old heap.</p>"}, {"key": "P17:4", "module": "P17", "q": "<p>What is Chaitin's graph coloring register allocation?</p>", "a": "<p>A technique modeling variable live ranges as nodes in an interference graph; a K-coloring of the graph maps variables to K physical registers.</p>"}, {"key": "P17:5", "module": "P17", "q": "<p>What is Tail-Call Optimization (TCO)?</p>", "a": "<p>Reusing the caller's stack frame when a function call is in tail position, eliminating stack frame growth and turning recursion into a loop.</p>"}, {"key": "S04:0", "module": "S04", "q": "<p>What is Zygote in Android?</p>", "a": "<p>The root process that pre-loads ART runtime classes and forks new application processes using Copy-On-Write memory sharing.</p>"}, {"key": "S04:1", "module": "S04", "q": "<p>What is the oom_adj_score?</p>", "a": "<p>A priority score from -1000 to 1000 assigned by ActivityManager; higher scores represent lower importance and are killed first by LMK.</p>"}, {"key": "S04:2", "module": "S04", "q": "<p>What causes an Application Not Responding (ANR) error?</p>", "a": "<p>Blocking the main UI thread for $\\ge 5.0$ seconds during user input dispatch or $\\ge 20$ seconds during broadcast handling.</p>"}, {"key": "S04:3", "module": "S04", "q": "<p>What is a Context Leak?</p>", "a": "<p>When an Activity context is retained by a long-lived static object or singleton after onDestroy(), preventing garbage collection.</p>"}, {"key": "S04:4", "module": "S04", "q": "<p>What survives Android process death?</p>", "a": "<p>SavedStateHandle / onSaveInstanceState bundles (saved by system server) and disk storage. ViewModels do NOT survive process death.</p>"}, {"key": "S04:5", "module": "S04", "q": "<p>What is onTrimMemory()?</p>", "a": "<p>An Android callback notifying running apps of system-wide memory pressure so they can trim image caches before LMK termination strikes.</p>"}, {"key": "S12:0", "module": "S12", "q": "<p>Where should sensitive encryption keys and auth tokens live?</p>", "a": "<p>Inside the hardware-backed Android Keystore or iOS Keychain / Secure Enclave.</p>"}, {"key": "S12:1", "module": "S12", "q": "<p>Why does the Verhoeff algorithm outperform Luhn for national IDs?</p>", "a": "<p>Verhoeff uses the dihedral group D5, catching 100% of single-digit errors and 100% of adjacent transposition errors.</p>"}, {"key": "S12:2", "module": "S12", "q": "<p>Why does RFC 8252 ban embedded WebViews for OAuth?</p>", "a": "<p>Embedded WebViews allow host applications to inspect input fields and steal user passwords; external browser tabs isolate credentials safely.</p>"}, {"key": "S12:3", "module": "S12", "q": "<p>What does PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) prevent?</p>", "a": "<p>Prevents malicious apps on the device from intercepting authorization codes returned from OAuth login redirects.</p>"}, {"key": "S12:4", "module": "S12", "q": "<p>What is the danger of an exported Android component without permissions?</p>", "a": "<p>Any other third-party application on the device can launch the component and hijack internal app functionality.</p>"}, {"key": "S12:5", "module": "S12", "q": "<p>What is PII masking in mobile logging?</p>", "a": "<p>Redacting sensitive personal data (credit cards, national IDs, JWT tokens) from debug and crash logs to prevent privacy leaks.</p>"}, {"key": "S05:0", "module": "S05", "q": "<p>What is Android's main-thread rule?</p>", "a": "<p>Never perform blocking operations (I/O, database, network) on the main thread, and never touch UI components from background threads.</p>"}, {"key": "S05:1", "module": "S05", "q": "<p>How does Android's MessageQueue order messages?</p>", "a": "<p>Chronologically by delivery timestamp (<code>when</code>), not in plain FIFO order.</p>"}, {"key": "S05:2", "module": "S05", "q": "<p>What is Kotlin structured concurrency?</p>", "a": "<p>A concurrency paradigm where parent scopes track child coroutines, automatically cancelling all children if the parent or a sibling fails.</p>"}, {"key": "S05:3", "module": "S05", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Dispatchers.IO and Dispatchers.Default?</p>", "a": "<p>Dispatchers.IO is sized for blocking disk/network tasks (up to 64 threads); Dispatchers.Default is sized for CPU-bound computations (equal to CPU core count).</p>"}, {"key": "S05:4", "module": "S05", "q": "<p>What is WorkManager?</p>", "a": "<p>An Android Jetpack library that guarantees deferrable, constraint-based background execution that survives app restarts and reboots.</p>"}, {"key": "S05:5", "module": "S05", "q": "<p>Why use repeatOnLifecycle with Kotlin Flow?</p>", "a": "<p>To automatically pause flow collection when the UI is invisible (below STARTED) and restart it on return, preventing background battery and CPU waste.</p>"}, {"key": "S06:0", "module": "S06", "q": "<p>What is Binder in Android?</p>", "a": "<p>The Linux kernel driver that provides secure, high-performance Inter-Process Communication (IPC) with hardware-enforced UID authentication.</p>"}, {"key": "S06:1", "module": "S06", "q": "<p>What is the Binder transaction buffer limit?</p>", "a": "<p>1 Megabyte (1024 * 1024 bytes) shared across all concurrent transactions for a given process.</p>"}, {"key": "S06:2", "module": "S06", "q": "<p>What are the four primary Android application components?</p>", "a": "<p>Activity (UI screen), Service (background operation), BroadcastReceiver (system event listener), and ContentProvider (data sharing).</p>"}, {"key": "S06:3", "module": "S06", "q": "<p>What does PendingIntent FLAG_IMMUTABLE do?</p>", "a": "<p>Guarantees that foreign apps receiving the PendingIntent cannot modify its action, data, or target component before execution.</p>"}, {"key": "S06:4", "module": "S06", "q": "<p>What is the difference between standard and singleTop launch modes?</p>", "a": "<p>standard always spawns a new instance; singleTop reuses the instance if it is already at the top of the back stack via onNewIntent().</p>"}, {"key": "S06:5", "module": "S06", "q": "<p>What happens during a TransactionTooLargeException?</p>", "a": "<p>A Binder transaction failed because the Parcel payload plus envelope exceeded the 1 MB buffer limit, crashing the calling process.</p>"}, {"key": "S07:0", "module": "S07", "q": "<p>What is the difference between MethodChannel and EventChannel?</p>", "a": "<p>MethodChannel is for single asynchronous request-response RPC calls; EventChannel is for continuous streams of asynchronous events (like sensors).</p>"}, {"key": "S07:1", "module": "S07", "q": "<p>What is Dart FFI (Foreign Function Interface)?</p>", "a": "<p>A mechanism allowing Dart code to directly invoke C/C++ library functions with zero-copy memory access in the same process.</p>"}, {"key": "S07:2", "module": "S07", "q": "<p>What thread do Platform Channel calls arrive on in native Android?</p>", "a": "<p>The main UI thread by default, requiring background thread dispatching for long operations.</p>"}, {"key": "S07:3", "module": "S07", "q": "<p>What is Pigeon in Flutter?</p>", "a": "<p>A code generator that produces type-safe platform channel wrappers for Dart, Kotlin, and Swift from a single interface definition.</p>"}, {"key": "S07:4", "module": "S07", "q": "<p>What is an Arena in Dart FFI?</p>", "a": "<p>A scoped memory allocator that automatically frees all native allocated memory when execution leaves the code block.</p>"}, {"key": "S07:5", "module": "S07", "q": "<p>What is the JNI local reference table limit in Android?</p>", "a": "<p>512 local reference slots; creating local references in loops without DeleteLocalRef causes process abort.</p>"}, {"key": "P20:0", "module": "P20", "q": "<p>What is the happens-before relation in distributed systems?</p>", "a": "<p>A strict partial order (introduced by Leslie Lamport) defining causal relationships between events across independent processes without physical clocks.</p>"}, {"key": "P20:1", "module": "P20", "q": "<p>What is the Quorum intersection invariant?</p>", "a": "<p>In an N-node replica cluster with write quorum W and read quorum R, R + W &gt; N guarantees that any read quorum intersects any write quorum in at least one node, ensuring fresh data.</p>"}, {"key": "P20:2", "module": "P20", "q": "<p>What does the CAP theorem state?</p>", "a": "<p>In an asynchronous network experiencing network partitions (P), a distributed data store can guarantee either Consistency (linearizability) or Availability, but not both.</p>"}, {"key": "P20:3", "module": "P20", "q": "<p>What are Conflict-Free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)?</p>", "a": "<p>Data structures modeled on join-semilattices where replicas update state locally and merge concurrently, mathematically guaranteeing convergence via commutativity, associativity, and idempotence.</p>"}, {"key": "P20:4", "module": "P20", "q": "<p>Why is 'exactly-once delivery' an illusion in distributed networking?</p>", "a": "<p>Network packet loss and the Two Generals' Problem prove that unacknowledged messages cannot be confirmed; true reliability requires at-least-once delivery paired with idempotent processing.</p>"}, {"key": "P20:5", "module": "P20", "q": "<p>How does Raft ensure consensus safety?</p>", "a": "<p>By guaranteeing that at most one leader can be elected per term via majority voting, and ensuring the leader only appends to logs, never overwriting existing committed entries.</p>"}, {"key": "D19:0", "module": "D19", "q": "<p>What does Brewer's CAP Theorem state?</p>", "a": "<p>In the presence of a network partition (P), a distributed system must choose between Consistency (C) and Availability (A).</p>"}, {"key": "D19:1", "module": "D19", "q": "<p>What fraction of keys are remapped when a node is added in Consistent Hashing?</p>", "a": "<p>Expected 1 / (N + 1) keys, compared to ~N / (N + 1) in traditional modulo hashing.</p>"}, {"key": "D19:2", "module": "D19", "q": "<p>What is the structure of a Twitter Snowflake 64-bit ID?</p>", "a": "<p>1 unused sign bit, 41-bit timestamp delta (69 years), 5-bit datacenter ID, 5-bit worker ID, 12-bit sequence counter.</p>"}, {"key": "D19:3", "module": "D19", "q": "<p>What is tail latency amplification?</p>", "a": "<p>When a single user request fans out to M independent backends, the probability of hitting a 99th percentile delay escalates to 1 - (1 - 0.01)^M.</p>"}, {"key": "D19:4", "module": "D19", "q": "<p>How does a Token Bucket differ from a Leaky Bucket?</p>", "a": "<p>Token Bucket allows traffic bursts up to bucket capacity; Leaky Bucket forces egress at a strictly constant smooth rate.</p>"}, {"key": "D19:5", "module": "D19", "q": "<p>What is the difference between RPO and RTO in disaster recovery?</p>", "a": "<p>RPO (Recovery Point Objective) is the maximum acceptable data loss in time; RTO (Recovery Time Objective) is the maximum acceptable downtime.</p>"}, {"key": "D20:0", "module": "D20", "q": "<p>What is the core philosophy of Offline-First mobile architecture?</p>", "a": "<p>The local embedded database (SQLite/Realm) is the primary single source of truth for the UI; network is an asynchronous sync channel.</p>"}, {"key": "D20:1", "module": "D20", "q": "<p>What problem does Full Jitter solve in exponential backoff?</p>", "a": "<p>It breaks the synchronized 'thundering herd' shockwaves of retrying clients, smoothing the traffic into a manageable Poisson arrival distribution.</p>"}, {"key": "D20:2", "module": "D20", "q": "<p>What does the Outbox Pattern guarantee in mobile apps?</p>", "a": "<p>At-least-once delivery of user mutations by storing them in a durable local queue alongside the local database transaction.</p>"}, {"key": "D20:3", "module": "D20", "q": "<p>What three properties must a state-based CRDT join operator possess?</p>", "a": "<p>Commutativity (a \u2294 b = b \u2294 a), Associativity ((a \u2294 b) \u2294 c = a \u2294 (b \u2294 c)), and Idempotence (a \u2294 a = a).</p>"}, {"key": "D20:4", "module": "D20", "q": "<p>Why should mobile apps avoid polling?</p>", "a": "<p>Continuous polling prevents the mobile baseband radio and CPU from entering low-power sleep states, draining battery rapidly.</p>"}, {"key": "D20:5", "module": "D20", "q": "<p>What is the role of push notifications in offline sync?</p>", "a": "<p>A lightweight wake-up signal informing the client that new server deltas exist, triggering an on-demand delta pull.</p>"}, {"key": "P21:0", "module": "P21", "q": "<p>What does Amdahl's Law state?</p>", "a": "<p>The maximum speedup of a program on parallel processors is strictly bounded by the reciprocal of its sequential execution fraction: S(P) &lt;= 1/s as P -&gt; infinity.</p>"}, {"key": "P21:1", "module": "P21", "q": "<p>What is the key difference between Amdahl's Law and Gustafson's Law?</p>", "a": "<p>Amdahl assumes a fixed problem size (speedup asymptotes to 1/s), while Gustafson assumes problem size scales with processor count (speedup scales linearly: s + P(1-s)).</p>"}, {"key": "P21:2", "module": "P21", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Work and Span?</p>", "a": "<p>Work (T1) is the total sequential execution time of all operations. Span (T_inf) is the critical path length of the computation DAG. Maximum parallelism is T1 / T_inf.</p>"}, {"key": "P21:3", "module": "P21", "q": "<p>What is the difference between SIMD and SIMT?</p>", "a": "<p>SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) uses wide CPU vector registers (AVX/NEON) to execute one opcode over vector lanes; SIMT (Single Instruction, Multiple Threads) runs lockstep warps on GPUs where independent thread branches can cause divergence.</p>"}, {"key": "P21:4", "module": "P21", "q": "<p>How does Blelloch's parallel prefix scan achieve work-efficiency?</p>", "a": "<p>By executing in two tree phases: an up-sweep reduction tree that computes partial sums, and a down-sweep distribution tree, achieving O(n) work and O(log n) span.</p>"}, {"key": "P21:5", "module": "P21", "q": "<p>What is the Roofline Model in computer architecture?</p>", "a": "<p>A performance model showing whether an algorithm's throughput is bounded by peak compute floating-point operations (GFLOPS) or by memory bandwidth (GB/s * operational intensity).</p>"}, {"key": "P23:0", "module": "P23", "q": "<p>Why are homogeneous coordinates (x, y, z, 1) used in 3D graphics?</p>", "a": "<p>They allow translation, rotation, scaling, and projective transformations to all be unified as single 4x4 matrix multiplications, enabling efficient composition.</p>"}, {"key": "P23:1", "module": "P23", "q": "<p>What is gimbal lock and how do quaternions prevent it?</p>", "a": "<p>Gimbal lock is the loss of one rotational degree of freedom when Euler axes align. Quaternions represent rotations as 4D hypercomplex numbers, providing singularity-free continuous rotation.</p>"}, {"key": "P23:2", "module": "P23", "q": "<p>How does the 2D orientation test determine clockwise vs counter-clockwise turns?</p>", "a": "<p>By taking the 2x2 determinant cross-product (Bx - Ax)(Cy - Ay) - (By - Ay)(Cx - Ax): positive indicates counter-clockwise (left), negative indicates clockwise (right), zero is collinear.</p>"}, {"key": "P23:3", "module": "P23", "q": "<p>What is the time complexity of the Graham scan convex hull algorithm?</p>", "a": "<p>O(n log n) dominated by initial point sorting; the subsequent monotone stack hull construction runs in linear O(n) time.</p>"}, {"key": "P23:4", "module": "P23", "q": "<p>What is the frame budget for a 60Hz display and a 120Hz display?</p>", "a": "<p>16.67ms per frame for 60Hz, and 8.33ms per frame for 120Hz; exceeding this budget causes dropped frames (jank).</p>"}, {"key": "P23:5", "module": "P23", "q": "<p>Why is gamma correction needed in graphics pipelines?</p>", "a": "<p>Human vision perceives light logarithmically; gamma encoding (V^(1/2.2)) compresses linear luminance to allocate more bits to darker tones where human eyes have higher sensitivity.</p>"}, {"key": "S02:0", "module": "S02", "q": "<p>What are Flutter's three trees?</p>", "a": "<p>Widget Tree (immutable configuration), Element Tree (structural lifecycle and state), and RenderObject Tree (layout and painting).</p>"}, {"key": "S02:1", "module": "S02", "q": "<p>What does 'constraints go down, sizes go up' mean?</p>", "a": "<p>Parents pass BoxConstraints down to children; children determine their dimensions within those constraints and report their Size back up.</p>"}, {"key": "S02:2", "module": "S02", "q": "<p>What is the frame budget for smooth 60 Hz and 120 Hz rendering?</p>", "a": "<p>16.67 milliseconds per frame at 60 Hz; 8.33 milliseconds per frame at 120 Hz.</p>"}, {"key": "S02:3", "module": "S02", "q": "<p>How does RepaintBoundary eliminate raster jank?</p>", "a": "<p>By isolating rapidly repainting subtrees into an offscreen display list cache, preventing full-screen redraws.</p>"}, {"key": "S02:4", "module": "S02", "q": "<p>Why does Impeller eliminate shader compilation jank?</p>", "a": "<p>Impeller precompiles all shaders ahead-of-time (AOT) into native Metal or Vulkan code, avoiding runtime compilation pauses.</p>"}, {"key": "S02:5", "module": "S02", "q": "<p>What is the Gesture Arena?</p>", "a": "<p>Flutter's touch arbitration mechanism that collects competing recognizers and resolves a single winner when gestures exceed movement thresholds.</p>"}, {"key": "S03:0", "module": "S03", "q": "<p>What is the difference between ephemeral state and app state?</p>", "a": "<p>Ephemeral state is local to a single widget (e.g. current tab); app state is shared across multiple screens (e.g. user session).</p>"}, {"key": "S03:1", "module": "S03", "q": "<p>How does InheritedWidget optimize subtree updates?</p>", "a": "<p>Descendant elements subscribe to the inherited widget; when state changes, only subscribed elements are marked dirty.</p>"}, {"key": "S03:2", "module": "S03", "q": "<p>Why must you check <code>mounted</code> after an <code>await</code> in Flutter?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the user might have navigated away while waiting for the async call; calling <code>setState()</code> on an unmounted element throws an error.</p>"}, {"key": "S03:3", "module": "S03", "q": "<p>What is the difference between <code>go()</code> and <code>push()</code> in go_router?</p>", "a": "<p><code>go()</code> updates the router URI and switches shell branches cleanly; <code>push()</code> imperatively adds a route onto the topmost stack.</p>"}, {"key": "S03:4", "module": "S03", "q": "<p>What is the State object lifecycle sequence?</p>", "a": "<p>createState -&gt; initState -&gt; didChangeDependencies -&gt; build -&gt; didUpdateWidget -&gt; dispose.</p>"}, {"key": "S03:5", "module": "S03", "q": "<p>What is unidirectional data flow in BLoC / Redux?</p>", "a": "<p>Events flow into a ViewModel/Reducer, which emits new immutable States observed by the UI, preventing circular dependencies.</p>"}, {"key": "S14:0", "module": "S14", "q": "<p>What is Server-Driven UI (SDUI)?</p>", "a": "<p>An architecture where the backend delivers declarative JSON schemas defining layout and rules, while native mobile engines render widgets dynamically.</p>"}, {"key": "S14:1", "module": "S14", "q": "<p>What does depends_on specify in form schemas?</p>", "a": "<p>A conditional expression that dynamically determines whether a field is visible to the user based on form state.</p>"}, {"key": "S14:2", "module": "S14", "q": "<p>Why must dynamic expressions use a safe AST subset?</p>", "a": "<p>To prevent Remote Code Execution (RCE) and injection vulnerabilities that arise from raw string evaluation.</p>"}, {"key": "S14:3", "module": "S14", "q": "<p>What is the 'superset, never contradicting' parity criterion?</p>", "a": "<p>Mobile clients may offer additional features (e.g. offline support), but must never contradict server validation decisions.</p>"}, {"key": "S14:4", "module": "S14", "q": "<p>Why do missing stubs make a parity test INCONCLUSIVE?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the true behavior of the un-stubbed API call is unknown, making it impossible to certify whether the client passed or failed.</p>"}, {"key": "S14:5", "module": "S14", "q": "<p>What is the purpose of the client-script validate hook?</p>", "a": "<p>To run client-side business logic and throw errors to block invalid form submissions before sending data to the server.</p>"}, {"key": "S11:0", "module": "S11", "q": "<p>What are the tiers of the mobile test pyramid?</p>", "a": "<p>Unit tests (fast business logic), Widget/Component tests (headless UI with virtual clock), Golden tests (visual screenshot comparison), and E2E tests (real device flows).</p>"}, {"key": "S11:1", "module": "S11", "q": "<p>What is the difference between a mock and a fake?</p>", "a": "<p>A mock verifies method call expectations; a fake is a working in-memory implementation of an interface that maintains real state.</p>"}, {"key": "S11:2", "module": "S11", "q": "<p>Why does Flutter widget testing use a virtual clock?</p>", "a": "<p>tester.pump() advances virtual time instantly without waiting wall-clock seconds, allowing fast, deterministic animation testing.</p>"}, {"key": "S11:3", "module": "S11", "q": "<p>What causes flaky tests on mobile platforms?</p>", "a": "<p>Asynchronous network delays, animation timing variations, non-deterministic thread scheduling, and unseeded random data.</p>"}, {"key": "S11:4", "module": "S11", "q": "<p>How does golden test pixel tolerance prevent false failures?</p>", "a": "<p>By allowing a small Euclidean color distance (\u0394E) to tolerate subtle anti-aliasing and font rasterization differences across GPUs.</p>"}, {"key": "S11:5", "module": "S11", "q": "<p>Why is DAMP preferred over DRY in test code?</p>", "a": "<p>Descriptive And Meaningful Phrases make test failures immediately understandable without jumping through complex shared abstraction layers.</p>"}, {"key": "S13:0", "module": "S13", "q": "<p>Why are mobile rollbacks impossible once a build is downloaded?</p>", "a": "<p>Users have already installed the immutable binary on their physical devices; reverting requires building, testing, and distributing a new version.</p>"}, {"key": "S13:1", "module": "S13", "q": "<p>What is the difference between versionName and versionCode?</p>", "a": "<p>versionName is the human-readable semver string (e.g. '2.4.1'); versionCode is an integer that must monotonically increase on every release.</p>"}, {"key": "S13:2", "module": "S13", "q": "<p>What does R8 do during Android compilation?</p>", "a": "<p>Code shrinking (tree shaking), optimization (inlining), and obfuscation (renaming classes to short tokens).</p>"}, {"key": "S13:3", "module": "S13", "q": "<p>Why must R8 mapping.txt be preserved for every release?</p>", "a": "<p>To deobfuscate production crash stack traces and map obfuscated line numbers back to original source code symbols.</p>"}, {"key": "S13:4", "module": "S13", "q": "<p>How does a staged rollout protect production stability?</p>", "a": "<p>By releasing to a small canary cohort (e.g. 1%) first, allowing developers to monitor crash metrics and halt the release if bugs appear.</p>"}, {"key": "S13:5", "module": "S13", "q": "<p>What is the primary benefit of remote kill switches?</p>", "a": "<p>Instantly deactivating broken or vulnerable features from a cloud dashboard without waiting for app store review and user downloads.</p>"}, {"key": "S10:0", "module": "S10", "q": "<p>What is the frame time budget at 60 Hz and 120 Hz?</p>", "a": "<p>16.66 milliseconds at 60 Hz, and 8.33 milliseconds at 120 Hz.</p>"}, {"key": "S10:1", "module": "S10", "q": "<p>What is the difference between cold start and warm start?</p>", "a": "<p>Cold start launches from scratch without process in memory (&lt;1000ms target); warm start brings an existing process with recreated Activity to the foreground (&lt;500ms target).</p>"}, {"key": "S10:2", "module": "S10", "q": "<p>Why does bitmap decoding consume so much RAM?</p>", "a": "<p>Compressed JPEG/PNG files expand into uncompressed raw pixels in memory: Width \u00d7 Height \u00d7 4 bytes for ARGB_8888 format.</p>"}, {"key": "S10:3", "module": "S10", "q": "<p>How does list virtualization prevent memory exhaustion?</p>", "a": "<p>By instantiating only the widgets currently visible within the screen viewport plus a small overscan buffer, recycling views as they scroll away.</p>"}, {"key": "S10:4", "module": "S10", "q": "<p>What are Baseline Profiles?</p>", "a": "<p>Ahead-Of-Time (AOT) compiled method specifications shipped inside the APK to accelerate cold startup and eliminate JIT compilation jank.</p>"}, {"key": "S10:5", "module": "S10", "q": "<p>Why should saveLayer and Opacity widgets be minimized?</p>", "a": "<p>They allocate offscreen GPU render buffers, triggering additional compositing passes that inflate frame rendering time.</p>"}, {"key": "S17:0", "module": "S17", "q": "<p>What are the three passes of the classic Android View system?</p>", "a": "<p>Measure (determining size), Layout (positioning children), and Draw (rendering pixels to Canvas).</p>"}, {"key": "S17:1", "module": "S17", "q": "<p>What is recomposition in Jetpack Compose?</p>", "a": "<p>The process of re-executing composable functions whose observed state has changed to update the UI tree.</p>"}, {"key": "S17:2", "module": "S17", "q": "<p>Why does passing a standard List cause recomposition jank?</p>", "a": "<p>Standard List is considered unstable by the Compose compiler, preventing the composable from skipping recomposition.</p>"}, {"key": "S17:3", "module": "S17", "q": "<p>How does DiffUtil optimize RecyclerView updates?</p>", "a": "<p>By calculating the minimal edit operations (insertions, deletions, moves) between lists using Myers' diff algorithm instead of redrawing all items.</p>"}, {"key": "S17:4", "module": "S17", "q": "<p>What does LaunchedEffect(key) do when key changes?</p>", "a": "<p>It cancels the currently running coroutine job and launches a new coroutine with the updated key.</p>"}, {"key": "S17:5", "module": "S17", "q": "<p>What is state hoisting in Jetpack Compose?</p>", "a": "<p>Moving state to a caller to make a composable stateless, reusable, and easy to unit test.</p>"}, {"key": "P25:0", "module": "P25", "q": "<p>What are the six phases of the incident response lifecycle in NIST SP 800-61?</p>", "a": "<p>Preparation, Detection and Analysis, Containment, Eradication, Recovery, and Post-Incident Review.</p>"}, {"key": "P25:1", "module": "P25", "q": "<p>What is the mandatory cybersecurity incident reporting deadline under CERT-In Directions 2022?</p>", "a": "<p>All covered cyber incidents (such as ransomware, system compromise, or data breaches) must be reported within 6 hours of noticing.</p>"}, {"key": "P25:2", "module": "P25", "q": "<p>What is required to maintain legal chain of custody for digital forensic evidence?</p>", "a": "<p>An immutable audit log documenting every custodian transfer, timestamps, reasons, and cryptographic hash verification (SHA-256) matching the original image.</p>"}, {"key": "P25:3", "module": "P25", "q": "<p>How does hardware anti-rollback protection prevent downgrade attacks?</p>", "a": "<p>Monotonic eFuses record the minimum permitted security version; attempts to boot older signed firmware with known vulnerabilities are rejected by hardware.</p>"}, {"key": "P25:4", "module": "P25", "q": "<p>What is the purpose of a blameless postmortem in security engineering?</p>", "a": "<p>To analyze systemic architectural weaknesses, latent design defects, and process failures without individual scapegoating, strengthening long-term defense.</p>"}, {"key": "P25:5", "module": "P25", "q": "<p>What is the Single Loss Expectancy (SLE) in quantitative risk assessment?</p>", "a": "<p>The financial loss incurred from a single incident occurrence, calculated as Asset Value multiplied by Exposure Factor (AV x EF).</p>"}, {"key": "M36:0", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>What makes convexity the dividing line in optimisation?</p>", "a": "<p>For a convex function every stationary point is a global minimum, so going downhill cannot strand you in the wrong place. The one-line proof is the first-order condition with the gradient set to zero.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:1", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>What is the largest stable step for gradient descent, and why?</p>", "a": "<p>2/L, where L is the largest Hessian eigenvalue. On a quadratic each eigendirection evolves as (1 - alpha*lambda)^k, and that exceeds 1 in magnitude exactly when alpha &gt; 2/lambda.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:2", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>What determines the SPEED of gradient descent, as opposed to whether it converges?</p>", "a": "<p>The condition number kappa = L/mu. With step 1/L the contraction is 1 - 1/kappa per step, so about kappa iterations are needed.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:3", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>What does momentum buy?</p>", "a": "<p>The rate's dependence drops from kappa to sqrt(kappa). Nesterov's version is optimal among first-order methods.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:4", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>Why does Adam need bias correction, and which way does the error go?</p>", "a": "<p>m and v both start at zero, so both are biased towards zero. The biases do not cancel: the net uncorrected step is sqrt(1-b2^t)/(1-b1^t) times the intended one, about 3.16 times TOO LARGE at t=1. With correction the first step has magnitude exactly alpha.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:5", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>Why does constant-step SGD not converge?</p>", "a": "<p>Gradient noise keeps it in a ball of radius O(alpha * sigma^2) around the optimum. Convergence needs a decreasing step with sum alpha = infinity and sum alpha^2 finite.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:6", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>How does mini-batch size affect gradient noise?</p>", "a": "<p>A batch of b cuts the variance by b and the standard deviation by sqrt(b) \u2014 diminishing returns, the same 1/sqrt(n) law as everywhere else.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:7", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>What is Newton's method's key advantage and its cost?</p>", "a": "<p>It is affine invariant, so the condition number drops out of its rate, and it converges quadratically near the optimum. It costs O(d^3) per step and needs a positive definite Hessian.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:8", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>State the four KKT conditions.</p>", "a": "<p>Stationarity (the Lagrangian's gradient vanishes), primal feasibility, dual feasibility (multipliers non-negative), and complementary slackness (lambda_i * g_i = 0).</p>"}, {"key": "M36:9", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>What does weak duality give you, and what does it assume?</p>", "a": "<p>Every dual feasible point lower-bounds the primal optimum, so the gap is a computable certificate of suboptimality. It assumes NOTHING \u2014 not even convexity.</p>"}, {"key": "M36:10", "module": "M36", "q": "<p>What does backtracking line search replace?</p>", "a": "<p>Knowledge of L. It halves the step until the Armijo sufficient-decrease condition holds, measuring instead of assuming.</p>"}, {"key": "L05:0", "module": "L05", "q": "<p>What is the Universal Approximation Theorem?</p>", "a": "<p>A mathematical theorem stating that a single-hidden-layer feedforward network with non-linear activations can approximate any continuous function on a compact domain to arbitrary precision.</p>"}, {"key": "L05:1", "module": "L05", "q": "<p>Why does ReLU solve vanishing gradients better than sigmoid?</p>", "a": "<p>ReLU has a constant derivative of 1.0 for positive inputs, preventing exponential gradient decay across deep layers.</p>"}, {"key": "L05:2", "module": "L05", "q": "<p>What is He (Kaiming) initialization?</p>", "a": "<p>A weight initialization scheme setting variance to $2 / d_{in}$, derived to maintain activation variance across layers with ReLU activations.</p>"}, {"key": "L05:3", "module": "L05", "q": "<p>How does a residual connection alter gradient flow?</p>", "a": "<p>It adds an identity shortcut $\\mathbf{h} + \\mathcal{F}(\\mathbf{h})$, giving a backward gradient $\nabla \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{I} + \nabla \\mathcal{F})$ that allows error signals to bypass decaying sub-layers.</p>"}, {"key": "L05:4", "module": "L05", "q": "<p>What is the difference between Batch Normalization and Layer Normalization?</p>", "a": "<p>Batch Normalization normalizes across the batch dimension; Layer Normalization normalizes across the feature dimension independently per sample.</p>"}, {"key": "L05:5", "module": "L05", "q": "<p>What are the two moment estimates tracked by the Adam optimizer?</p>", "a": "<p>Exponentially decaying moving averages of past gradients (first moment $m_t$) and squared gradients (second moment $v_t$).</p>"}, {"key": "M37:0", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>What is surprisal, and why must it be a logarithm?</p>", "a": "<p>-log2(p) bits. Additivity over independent events forces Cauchy's functional equation, whose only continuous solutions are logarithms.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:1", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>What does entropy bound?</p>", "a": "<p>The expected length of any uniquely decodable code: L &gt;= H. It is the hard floor on lossless compression of that source.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:2", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>State the relationship between cross-entropy, entropy and KL.</p>", "a": "<p>H(p,q) = H(p) + D(p||q). Since D &gt;= 0, cross-entropy is minimised over q at q = p \u2014 the justification for cross-entropy loss.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:3", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>Why is KL divergence non-negative?</p>", "a": "<p>Jensen's inequality applied to the convex function -log. Equality requires p/q constant, which with both summing to 1 forces p = q.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:4", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>How does mutual information differ from correlation?</p>", "a": "<p>I(X;Y) = 0 if and only if X and Y are INDEPENDENT. Correlation detects only linear association and can be zero for perfectly dependent variables.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:5", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>State Kraft's inequality and what it decides.</p>", "a": "<p>A prefix code with lengths l_i exists iff sum 2^(-l_i) &lt;= 1. It characterises exactly which sets of codeword lengths are possible.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:6", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>State the source coding theorem.</p>", "a": "<p>H &lt;= L* &lt; H + 1 for the best prefix code. Coding blocks of n symbols reduces the overhead to 1/n.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:7", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>In what sense is Huffman optimal, and what beats it?</p>", "a": "<p>It has the minimum expected length among PREFIX codes. Arithmetic coding beats it by not assigning whole numbers of bits per symbol.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:8", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>What is perplexity, and what is its floor?</p>", "a": "<p>2 raised to the cross-entropy. It reads as an effective number of equally likely choices, and is bounded below by 2^H(p), the source's own entropy.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:9", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>What is the capacity of a binary symmetric channel?</p>", "a": "<p>1 - H_b(e), where e is the crossover probability. It is zero at e = 0.5, where the output is independent of the input.</p>"}, {"key": "M37:10", "module": "M37", "q": "<p>What does the data processing inequality say?</p>", "a": "<p>For a chain X -&gt; Y -&gt; Z, I(X;Z) &lt;= I(X;Y). Post-processing can never create information.</p>"}, {"key": "L06:0", "module": "L06", "q": "<p>What is Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE)?</p>", "a": "<p>An iterative data-driven subword tokenization algorithm that merges the most frequent adjacent character pairs into new vocabulary tokens.</p>"}, {"key": "L06:1", "module": "L06", "q": "<p>Why is byte-level BPE superior to character-level BPE?</p>", "a": "<p>Byte-level BPE uses all 256 possible bytes as its base vocabulary, ensuring zero out-of-vocabulary (OOV) tokens for any arbitrary input string.</p>"}, {"key": "L06:2", "module": "L06", "q": "<p>What is the Distributional Hypothesis in linguistics?</p>", "a": "<p>The principle that words appearing in similar linguistic contexts tend to share similar semantic meanings ('you shall know a word by the company it keeps').</p>"}, {"key": "L06:3", "module": "L06", "q": "<p>What does language model Perplexity measure?</p>", "a": "<p>The exponentiated cross-entropy loss, representing the effective number of equally likely tokens the model is choosing among at each step.</p>"}, {"key": "L06:4", "module": "L06", "q": "<p>How does vector arithmetic solve word analogies like King - Man + Woman?</p>", "a": "<p>By translating the vector along semantic difference directions (such as gender or tense) in a learned geometric embedding space.</p>"}, {"key": "L06:5", "module": "L06", "q": "<p>Why do static word2vec embeddings struggle with polysemy?</p>", "a": "<p>Because static embeddings assign exactly one vector per word, conflating distinct word senses into an unweighted geometric average.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:0", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>What are the four group axioms?</p>", "a": "<p>Closure, associativity, an identity, and an inverse for every element. Commutativity is NOT required \u2014 abelian groups are the special case.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:1", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>State Lagrange's theorem and one consequence.</p>", "a": "<p>The order of any subgroup divides the order of the group. Hence the order of every element divides |G|, so a^|G| = e \u2014 which IS Fermat's little theorem in Z_p*.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:2", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>Why does Lagrange's theorem hold?</p>", "a": "<p>The left cosets of H partition G into disjoint blocks all of size |H|, because x -&gt; ax is a bijection from H onto aH.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:3", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>When is Z_n a field?</p>", "a": "<p>Exactly when n is prime. A composite modulus has zero divisors, and a zero divisor can never be invertible.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:4", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>What are addition and multiplication in GF(2^n)?</p>", "a": "<p>Addition is XOR of the bit patterns, with no carries. Multiplication is polynomial multiplication reduced modulo an IRREDUCIBLE polynomial of degree n.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:5", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>What is a CRC, algebraically?</p>", "a": "<p>The remainder of x^r M(x) divided by a generator polynomial G(x) over GF(2). An error E(x) goes undetected exactly when G(x) divides E(x).</p>"}, {"key": "M38:6", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>Why does a plain checksum miss transpositions, whatever its width?</p>", "a": "<p>Because addition is commutative, so the sum cannot see order. It is a structural blindness, not a shortage of bits.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:7", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>Why does the Hamming syndrome identify the error position?</p>", "a": "<p>The columns of H are the binary numerals 1..7, and a single error at position i gives syndrome equal to column i \u2014 the position written in binary.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:8", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>What does a code of minimum distance d guarantee?</p>", "a": "<p>It corrects floor((d-1)/2) errors and detects d-1. Distance 3 corrects one and detects two.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:9", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>What is a homomorphism, and what is its kernel?</p>", "a": "<p>A map preserving the operation: phi(ab) = phi(a)phi(b). The kernel is what maps to the identity; it is a normal subgroup, and G/ker is isomorphic to the image.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:10", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>Why is Shamir's secret sharing information-theoretically secure?</p>", "a": "<p>With t-1 shares, every candidate secret is consistent with exactly one polynomial of degree &lt; t, so all remain equally likely. The posterior equals the prior, whatever computation is available.</p>"}, {"key": "M38:11", "module": "M38", "q": "<p>Why is Schnorr zero-knowledge, and what breaks it?</p>", "a": "<p>A simulator who knows the challenge first can produce an identically distributed transcript without the secret, so the transcript carries no information. Reusing the commitment r with two different challenges reveals x outright.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:0", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>What does Fourier's theorem claim?</p>", "a": "<p>Any reasonable periodic signal is a unique sum of sinusoids, and the representation loses nothing \u2014 you can move between signal and coefficients freely.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:1", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>Why do the DFT coefficients come out unique?</p>", "a": "<p>Orthogonality: the sum over n of exp(2<em>pi</em>i<em>(j-k)</em>n/N) is N when j = k and 0 otherwise, because otherwise it is a geometric series whose numerator vanishes.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:2", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>State the Cooley-Tukey split.</p>", "a": "<p>X[k] = E[k] + w^k O[k] and X[k+N/2] = E[k] - w^k O[k], where E and O are the DFTs of the even and odd indexed samples. It works because w_N^2 = w_(N/2) and w^(k+N/2) = -w^k.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:3", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>Why is the FFT O(N log N)?</p>", "a": "<p>T(N) = 2T(N/2) + O(N), which the Master theorem solves to O(N log N).</p>"}, {"key": "M39:4", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>State the convolution theorem and its catch.</p>", "a": "<p>DFT(x circularly-convolved y) = DFT(x) * DFT(y) pointwise. The catch is that it is CIRCULAR \u2014 linear convolution needs zero-padding to len(a)+len(b)-1.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:5", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>State the Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem.</p>", "a": "<p>A signal with no energy above f_max is perfectly reconstructible from samples at a rate STRICTLY greater than 2*f_max.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:6", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>What is the apparent frequency of an aliased tone?</p>", "a": "<p>|f - fs * round(f/fs)|. A 98 Hz tone sampled at 100 Hz appears at 2 Hz.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:7", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>Why can aliasing not be undone in software?</p>", "a": "<p>Because f and f + m<em>fs give EXACTLY equal sample values \u2014 2</em>pi<em>m</em>n is a whole number of turns. The mapping is not injective, so no inverse exists.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:8", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>What causes spectral leakage?</p>", "a": "<p>A finite recording multiplies the signal by a window, and multiplication in time is convolution in frequency, so each spectral line is smeared by the window's transform.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:9", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>What do window functions trade?</p>", "a": "<p>Main-lobe width (resolution) against sidelobe level (leakage). Rectangular has the narrowest lobe and the worst sidelobes at -13 dB; Hann is twice as wide at -31 dB.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:10", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>Does zero-padding improve frequency resolution?</p>", "a": "<p>No. It interpolates the spectrum onto a finer grid. Resolution is 1/T and only a longer recording improves it.</p>"}, {"key": "M39:11", "module": "M39", "q": "<p>Name the simplest low-pass and high-pass filters and their nulls.</p>", "a": "<p>An L-point moving average is low-pass with nulls at multiples of fs/L. The first difference is high-pass, zero at DC and maximal at Nyquist.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:0", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>What is the Markov property?</p>", "a": "<p>The next state depends only on the current state, not on the path taken to reach it. It is what turns a simulation into linear algebra.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:1", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>What is a stationary distribution, algebraically?</p>", "a": "<p>A probability vector pi with pi P = pi \u2014 a LEFT eigenvector of P for eigenvalue 1. Existence is guaranteed; uniqueness needs irreducibility.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:2", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>What does ergodic mean and what does it buy?</p>", "a": "<p>Irreducible plus aperiodic. It guarantees a unique stationary distribution, reached from every starting state, equal to the long-run fraction of time in each state.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:3", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>Give the gambler's ruin probability for a fair walk and for a biased one.</p>", "a": "<p>Fair: k/N. Biased: (1 - r^k)/(1 - r^N) with r = q/p. A 1% edge turns a 50/50 start on a 100-step board into about 0.12.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:4", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>Why does PageRank need a damping factor?</p>", "a": "<p>The teleport makes the chain irreducible and aperiodic, so a unique stationary distribution exists and power iteration converges. It also fixes dangling nodes and rank sinks.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:5", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>What is the key idea of Metropolis-Hastings?</p>", "a": "<p>Build a chain whose stationary distribution IS the target, accepting with min(1, f(y)/f(x)). The unknown normalising constant cancels in the ratio.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:6", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>Give L, W and the stability condition for an M/M/1 queue.</p>", "a": "<p>rho = lambda/mu must be &lt; 1. L = rho/(1-rho), W = 1/(mu - lambda). The state distribution is geometric: p_n = (1-rho) rho^n.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:7", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>State Little's law and say how general it is.</p>", "a": "<p>L = lambda * W. It holds for ANY stable queueing system \u2014 any arrival process, service distribution or scheduling discipline.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:8", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>Why does latency explode as utilisation approaches 1?</p>", "a": "<p>Sojourn time in units of service time is 1/(1-rho), which is the pole of the geometric series sum n rho^(n-1) = 1/(1-rho)^2. It is a hyperbola, not a line.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:9", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>Does an M/M/1 queue make latency heavy-tailed?</p>", "a": "<p>No. Its sojourn time is exactly exponential at every utilisation \u2014 mean/median stays at 1/ln(2). The queue multiplies the SCALE by 1/(1-rho). A worse SHAPE comes from non-exponential service or from fan-out, where you wait for the max of N.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:10", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>Why does value iteration converge?</p>", "a": "<p>The Bellman operator is a gamma-contraction in the sup norm, so Banach's fixed point theorem gives a unique fixed point reached geometrically. This needs gamma &lt; 1.</p>"}, {"key": "M40:11", "module": "M40", "q": "<p>What does Q-learning learn, and what does it not need?</p>", "a": "<p>It learns Q(s,a) from sampled transitions and does NOT need the transition model P. It needs sufficient exploration and a decreasing step size.</p>"}, {"key": "V16:0", "module": "V16", "q": "<p>What is the SAT problem?</p>", "a": "<p>The decision problem of determining whether there exists a truth assignment that satisfies a Boolean formula in CNF.</p>"}, {"key": "V16:1", "module": "V16", "q": "<p>What does the Tseitin transformation achieve?</p>", "a": "<p>It converts arbitrary Boolean circuits into equisatisfiable CNF in linear time and space $O(n)$ by introducing auxiliary variables for each logic gate.</p>"}, {"key": "V16:2", "module": "V16", "q": "<p>What are the two core deduction rules in DPLL?</p>", "a": "<p>Unit propagation (forced assignment of unit clauses) and pure literal elimination (assigning variables with single polarities).</p>"}, {"key": "V16:3", "module": "V16", "q": "<p>What is Conflict-Driven Clause Learning (CDCL)?</p>", "a": "<p>A SAT algorithm that analyzes conflicts, learns new asserting clauses, and backjumps non-chronologically to avoid repeating mistakes.</p>"}, {"key": "V16:4", "module": "V16", "q": "<p>What is the difference between safety and liveness properties?</p>", "a": "<p>Safety: 'bad things never happen' (disproved by finite trace). Liveness: 'good things eventually happen' (disproved by infinite loop).</p>"}, {"key": "V16:5", "module": "V16", "q": "<p>How does explicit-state model checking find concurrency bugs?</p>", "a": "<p>By using breadth-first search to systematically explore every possible thread scheduling interleaving until all states are validated or an unsafe state is reached.</p>"}, {"key": "V18:0", "module": "V18", "q": "<p>What are the three passes in Keshav's Three-Pass paper reading method?</p>", "a": "<p>Pass 1: Birds-eye view (10 min). Pass 2: Grasp content without details (1 hour). Pass 3: Virtual re-implementation (several hours).</p>"}, {"key": "V18:1", "module": "V18", "q": "<p>What is the ACM definition of Reproducibility?</p>", "a": "<p>An independent team obtains identical results using the original author's artifacts on the same experimental setup.</p>"}, {"key": "V18:2", "module": "V18", "q": "<p>What is the ACM definition of Replicability?</p>", "a": "<p>An independent team obtains consistent findings using completely independent code, fresh data, or different experimental setups.</p>"}, {"key": "V18:3", "module": "V18", "q": "<p>What is the GRIM test?</p>", "a": "<p>A mathematical test checking whether reported means of integer Likert ratings are consistent with integer arithmetic given the reported sample size.</p>"}, {"key": "V18:4", "module": "V18", "q": "<p>How does inverse-variance weighting work in meta-analysis?</p>", "a": "<p>Each study's effect size is weighted by $w_i = 1 / SE_i^2$, giving higher statistical influence to studies with smaller standard errors.</p>"}, {"key": "V18:5", "module": "V18", "q": "<p>What does Higgins' $I^2$ measure in meta-analysis?</p>", "a": "<p>The percentage of variation across study results that is due to true heterogeneity rather than chance sampling error.</p>"}, {"key": "V19:0", "module": "V19", "q": "<p>What is the IMRaD structure in scientific papers?</p>", "a": "<p>Introduction (why), Methods (how), Results (what was found), and Discussion (what it means).</p>"}, {"key": "V19:1", "module": "V19", "q": "<p>Why are Non-Goals mandatory in Google L5 design documents?</p>", "a": "<p>They explicitly delimit scope boundaries and prevent scope creep from derailing system delivery.</p>"}, {"key": "V19:2", "module": "V19", "q": "<p>What is the Flesch Reading Ease score?</p>", "a": "<p>A quantitative readability metric based on average sentence length and syllable count per word; higher scores indicate clearer plain language.</p>"}, {"key": "V19:3", "module": "V19", "q": "<p>What is the CRediT taxonomy?</p>", "a": "<p>A standardized 14-role taxonomy defining contributor authorship credit (e.g. Conceptualization, Software, Supervision).</p>"}, {"key": "V19:4", "module": "V19", "q": "<p>What three elements must appear in every peer-review rebuttal response?</p>", "a": "<p>The reviewer's point, the author's decision with evidence, and the exact manuscript line numbers showing the modification.</p>"}, {"key": "V19:5", "module": "V19", "q": "<p>What makes a chart or figure misleading in technical documents?</p>", "a": "<p>Truncated non-zero y-axes, missing error bars, dual non-aligned axes, and cherry-picked baseline timeframes.</p>"}, {"key": "L11:0", "module": "L11", "q": "<p>What is test set contamination?</p>", "a": "<p>When benchmark test questions or solutions leak into the pre-training or fine-tuning datasets, causing models to memorize answers rather than generalize.</p>"}, {"key": "L11:1", "module": "L11", "q": "<p>Why use the Wilson score interval instead of the Wald interval?</p>", "a": "<p>The Wald interval assumes normal distribution and produces invalid bounds ($&gt;1.0$ or $&lt;0.0$) near extreme accuracies or for small sample sizes; Wilson maintains valid coverage.</p>"}, {"key": "L11:2", "module": "L11", "q": "<p>What is Cohen's kappa?</p>", "a": "<p>A statistical metric measuring agreement between two raters (e.g. human vs LLM judge) normalized by the agreement expected purely by random chance.</p>"}, {"key": "L11:3", "module": "L11", "q": "<p>What is position bias in LLM-as-a-judge?</p>", "a": "<p>The tendency of automated judges to disproportionately favor whichever answer is presented first (as Option A).</p>"}, {"key": "L11:4", "module": "L11", "q": "<p>How does paired bootstrap testing compare two models?</p>", "a": "<p>By resampling test set indices with replacement and calculating the empirical probability that Model B fails to outperform Model A.</p>"}, {"key": "L11:5", "module": "L11", "q": "<p>What is Goodhart's Law in machine learning?</p>", "a": "<p>When a specific benchmark metric becomes the primary optimization target, models overfit to its quirks and the metric loses its value as a measure of intelligence.</p>"}, {"key": "S15:0", "module": "S15", "q": "<p>What is Arithmetic Intensity in the Roofline Model?</p>", "a": "<p>The ratio of floating-point operations performed to bytes of DRAM memory transferred (FLOPs / Byte).</p>"}, {"key": "S15:1", "module": "S15", "q": "<p>What is the difference between memory-bound and compute-bound regimes?</p>", "a": "<p>Memory-bound kernels are limited by DRAM bandwidth; compute-bound kernels reach the processor's theoretical peak arithmetic capacity.</p>"}, {"key": "S15:2", "module": "S15", "q": "<p>What is the ARM NEON SDOT instruction?</p>", "a": "<p>A vector SIMD instruction that multiplies 16 signed 8-bit integers and accumulates into 4 32-bit integer lanes in a single clock cycle.</p>"}, {"key": "S15:3", "module": "S15", "q": "<p>How does cache tiling accelerate matrix multiplication?</p>", "a": "<p>By breaking matrices into blocks that fit within L1/L2 cache, reusing cached data and drastically reducing slow DRAM memory traffic.</p>"}, {"key": "S15:4", "module": "S15", "q": "<p>What is heterogeneous big.LITTLE scheduling?</p>", "a": "<p>Assigning latency-critical tasks to fast big cores and background tasks to power-efficient LITTLE cores to balance speed and battery life.</p>"}, {"key": "S15:5", "module": "S15", "q": "<p>Why does mobile thermal throttling occur?</p>", "a": "<p>Fanless smartphones cannot dissipate sustained high wattage; the OS reduces clock frequencies to prevent hardware damage.</p>"}, {"key": "L07:0", "module": "L07", "q": "<p>What is Scaled Dot-Product Attention?</p>", "a": "<p>An attention mechanism that computes $\\operatorname{softmax}(Q K^T / \\sqrt{d_k}) V$, using dot products between queries and keys to dynamically route values.</p>"}, {"key": "L07:1", "module": "L07", "q": "<p>Why divide by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ in scaled dot-product attention?</p>", "a": "<p>To maintain unit variance in the dot products, preventing large logits from saturating the softmax function into regions with vanishing gradients.</p>"}, {"key": "L07:2", "module": "L07", "q": "<p>What is causal masking in decoder-only Transformers?</p>", "a": "<p>Setting attention logits for future positions $j &gt; i$ to $-\\infty$ so tokens can only attend to prior tokens in the sequence.</p>"}, {"key": "L07:3", "module": "L07", "q": "<p>What are Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE)?</p>", "a": "<p>An encoding method that rotates adjacent feature pairs by angles proportional to token position, making inner products depend purely on relative distances.</p>"}, {"key": "L07:4", "module": "L07", "q": "<p>Why is pure self-attention permutation equivariant?</p>", "a": "<p>Because the attention weights depend only on pairwise similarity; reordering input rows permutes output rows without changing their values.</p>"}, {"key": "L07:5", "module": "L07", "q": "<p>What is the computational complexity of standard self-attention?</p>", "a": "<p>Quadratic in sequence length: $O(N^2 \\cdot d)$ time and $O(N^2)$ memory for the attention matrix.</p>"}, {"key": "L08:0", "module": "L08", "q": "<p>What is the difference between prefill and decode phases?</p>", "a": "<p>Prefill processes prompt tokens in parallel (compute-bound); decode generates one token at a time sequentially (memory-bound).</p>"}, {"key": "L08:1", "module": "L08", "q": "<p>Why does single-batch LLM decoding underutilize GPU compute cores?</p>", "a": "<p>Because loading gigabytes of model weights to perform a single token matrix-vector multiplication is bottlenecked by memory bandwidth.</p>"}, {"key": "L08:2", "module": "L08", "q": "<p>What does the KV cache store?</p>", "a": "<p>Past Key and Value activation vectors across attention layers, preventing quadratic $O(N^2)$ recomputation during autoregressive generation.</p>"}, {"key": "L08:3", "module": "L08", "q": "<p>How does speculative decoding achieve speedup without quality loss?</p>", "a": "<p>A small draft model proposes multiple tokens cheaply, which the target model verifies in a single parallel step with exact acceptance-rejection criteria.</p>"}, {"key": "L08:4", "module": "L08", "q": "<p>What is nucleus (top-p) sampling?</p>", "a": "<p>Sampling from the smallest set of high-probability tokens whose cumulative mass exceeds threshold $p$.</p>"}, {"key": "L08:5", "module": "L08", "q": "<p>How does repetition penalty discourage loops?</p>", "a": "<p>By dividing positive logits (or multiplying negative logits) of previously generated tokens by penalty scalar $\u0007lpha &gt; 1.0$.</p>"}, {"key": "L09:0", "module": "L09", "q": "<p>What is uniform affine quantization?</p>", "a": "<p>Mapping real numbers to integers via scale factor $S$ and zero-point $Z$: $q = \\operatorname{clip}(\\operatorname{round}(x/S) + Z)$.</p>"}, {"key": "L09:1", "module": "L09", "q": "<p>What is symmetric block quantization (Q8_0)?</p>", "a": "<p>Dividing weights into small chunks (e.g. 32 numbers) and scaling symmetrically by $d = \\max(|x|) / 127$ with zero-point fixed at 0.</p>"}, {"key": "L09:2", "module": "L09", "q": "<p>Why do large language models develop activation outliers?</p>", "a": "<p>Certain hidden feature dimensions emerge during pre-training to track critical syntax or attention sinks, exhibiting values up to 100x larger than normal.</p>"}, {"key": "L09:3", "module": "L09", "q": "<p>How does AWQ (Activation-aware Weight Quantization) protect model accuracy?</p>", "a": "<p>By identifying the 1% of weights corresponding to large activation magnitudes and preserving them with higher precision or scaling protection.</p>"}, {"key": "L09:4", "module": "L09", "q": "<p>How do you estimate single-token decode speed from memory bandwidth?</p>", "a": "<p>Divide memory bandwidth (GB/s) by model weight memory footprint (GB).</p>"}, {"key": "L09:5", "module": "L09", "q": "<p>What is the GGUF file format?</p>", "a": "<p>A binary container format developed for llama.cpp that packages model architecture metadata, vocabulary, and quantized weight tensors for fast memory-mapped loading.</p>"}, {"key": "L10:0", "module": "L10", "q": "<p>What four components comprise an on-device LLM memory budget?</p>", "a": "<p>Model weights, Key-Value (KV) cache, activation scratch buffers, and runtime heap/system overhead.</p>"}, {"key": "L10:1", "module": "L10", "q": "<p>Why does mmap prevent Android Low Memory Killer (LMK) termination?</p>", "a": "<p>Memory-mapped weights reside in the clean OS page cache backed by disk and can be dropped without swapping under memory pressure, keeping RSS low.</p>"}, {"key": "L10:2", "module": "L10", "q": "<p>Why is autoregressive decoding memory-bandwidth bound on mobile SoCs?</p>", "a": "<p>Because loading 3.5 GB of weights for every single decoded token saturates the 60-80 GB/s memory bus long before NPU compute units are fully utilized.</p>"}, {"key": "L10:3", "module": "L10", "q": "<p>What happens during mobile thermal throttling?</p>", "a": "<p>SoC governors reduce CPU and NPU clock frequencies by 30% to 50% to prevent chassis overheating, causing token generation speed to collapse.</p>"}, {"key": "L10:4", "module": "L10", "q": "<p>Why must warmup runs be excluded from mobile latency benchmarks?</p>", "a": "<p>To eliminate one-time cold-start overheads such as file loading, dynamic linking, and memory allocation from steady-state performance metrics.</p>"}, {"key": "L10:5", "module": "L10", "q": "<p>What is the difference between ARM big and LITTLE cores?</p>", "a": "<p>Big cores maximize clock speed and single-thread throughput for compute bursts; LITTLE cores maximize energy efficiency for background tasks.</p>"}, {"key": "L12:0", "module": "L12", "q": "<p>What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)?</p>", "a": "<p>A technique that retrieves relevant external documents based on user queries and injects them into the model's context before generation.</p>"}, {"key": "L12:1", "module": "L12", "q": "<p>How does BM25 improve on standard TF-IDF?</p>", "a": "<p>BM25 adds sub-linear term frequency saturation (preventing frequent terms from dominating) and document length normalization.</p>"}, {"key": "L12:2", "module": "L12", "q": "<p>What is Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)?</p>", "a": "<p>An algorithm that merges ranked lists from multiple search methods by summing $1 / (k + \text{rank})$ scores.</p>"}, {"key": "L12:3", "module": "L12", "q": "<p>What is an indirect prompt injection attack?</p>", "a": "<p>When malicious instructions embedded inside untrusted retrieved documents trick an LLM agent into executing unauthorized tool commands.</p>"}, {"key": "L12:4", "module": "L12", "q": "<p>Why must agent completion claims be audited against external evidence?</p>", "a": "<p>Because models frequently hallucinate that a task is finished without actually calling the required execution tools.</p>"}, {"key": "L12:5", "module": "L12", "q": "<p>What is the ReAct paradigm?</p>", "a": "<p>An agent framework interleaving Reasoning (chain-of-thought planning) and Acting (tool invocation and observation analysis).</p>"}, {"key": "V20:0", "module": "V20", "q": "<p>What are the FINER criteria for a research question?</p>", "a": "<p>Feasible, Interesting, Novel, Ethical, and Relevant.</p>"}, {"key": "V20:1", "module": "V20", "q": "<p>What does PRISMA stand for in systematic reviews?</p>", "a": "<p>Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses; standardizes transparent paper search and exclusion flows.</p>"}, {"key": "V20:2", "module": "V20", "q": "<p>What is HARKing and why is it scientifically invalid?</p>", "a": "<p>Hypothesizing After Results are Known: presenting a post-hoc finding found by data dredging as if it were a pre-planned hypothesis, inflating false-positive rates.</p>"}, {"key": "V20:3", "module": "V20", "q": "<p>Why must a measurement instrument be calibrated against a gold set before running experiments?</p>", "a": "<p>If the measurement tool has low F1 accuracy, experimental results reflect the instrument's noise rather than true treatment effects.</p>"}, {"key": "V20:4", "module": "V20", "q": "<p>What is qualitative thematic saturation?</p>", "a": "<p>The point during iterative qualitative data analysis where incoming interview transcripts yield no new thematic codes.</p>"}, {"key": "V20:5", "module": "V20", "q": "<p>What is the purpose of an experimental pilot study?</p>", "a": "<p>To test logistical feasibility, identify unhandled edge cases, and estimate baseline variances before committing full resources.</p>"}];
